<a name=3D"NAME"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Name</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">curl - transfer a URL </p><a name=3D"SYNOPSIS"></a><h2 =
class=3D"nroffsh">Synopsis</h2>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"bold">curl [options / URLs]</span> </p><=
a name=3D"DESCRIPTION"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Description</h2>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"bold">curl</span> is a tool for transfer=
ring data from or to a server using URLs. It supports these protocols: DICT=
, FILE, FTP, FTPS, GOPHER, GOPHERS, HTTP, HTTPS, IMAP, IMAPS, LDAP, LDAPS, =
MQTT, POP3, POP3S, RTMP, RTMPS, RTSP, SCP, SFTP, SMB, SMBS, SMTP, SMTPS, TE=
LNET, TFTP, WS and WSS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">curl is powered by libcurl for all transfer-related fea=
tures. See <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/libcurl.=
html">libcurl</a> for details. </p><a name=3D"URL"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh=
">Url</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">The URL syntax is protocol-dependent. You find a detail=
ed description in <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc3986.txt">RFC 3986=
</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">If you provide a URL without a leading <span class=3D"b=
old">protocol://</span> scheme, curl guesses what protocol you want. It the=
n defaults to HTTP but assumes others based on often-used hostname prefixes=
. For example, for hostnames starting with "ftp." curl assumes you want FTP=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">You can specify any amount of URLs on the command line.=
 They are fetched in a sequential manner in the specified order unless you =
use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--paral=
lel">--parallel</a>. You can specify command line options and URLs mixed an=
d in any order on the command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">curl attempts to reuse connections when doing multiple =
transfers, so that getting many files from the same server do not use multi=
ple connects and setup handshakes. This improves speed. Connection reuse ca=
n only be done for URLs specified for a single command line invocation and =
cannot be performed between separate curl runs. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Provide an IPv6 zone id in the URL with an escaped perc=
entage sign. Like in </p><pre class=3D"level0">http://[fe80::3%25eth0]/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">Everything provided on the command line that is not a c=
ommand line option or its argument, curl assumes is a URL and treats it as =
such. </p><a name=3D"GLOBBING"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Globbing</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">You can specify multiple URLs or parts of URLs by writi=
ng lists within braces or ranges within brackets. We call this "globbing". =
</p>
<p class=3D"level0">Provide a list with three different names like this: </=
p><pre class=3D"level0">https://fun.example/{one,two,three}.jpg
&nbsp;
sftp://{one,two,three}.example/README
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">Do sequences of alphanumeric series by using [] as in: =
</p><pre class=3D"level0">ftp://ftp.example.com/file[1-100].txt
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">With leading zeroes: </p><pre class=3D"level0">ftp://ft=
p.example.com/file[001-100].txt
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">With letters through the alphabet: </p><pre class=3D"le=
vel0">ftp://ftp.example.com/file[a-z].txt
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">Nested sequences are not supported, but you can use sev=
eral ones next to each other: </p><pre class=3D"level0">https://example.com=
/archive[1996-1999]/vol[1-4]/part{a,b,c}.html
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">You can specify a step counter for the ranges to get ev=
ery Nth number or letter: </p><pre class=3D"level0">https://example.com/fil=
e[1-100:10].txt
&nbsp;
https://example.com/file[a-z:2].txt
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">When using [] or {} sequences when invoked from a comma=
nd line prompt, you probably have to put the full URL within double quotes =
to avoid the shell from interfering with it. This also goes for other chara=
cters treated special, like for example '&amp;', '?' and '*'. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Switch off globbing with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--globoff">--globoff</a>. </p><a name=3D"=
VARIABLES"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Variables</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">curl supports command line variables (added in 8.3.0). =
Set variables with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--variable">--variable</a> name=3Dcontent or <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--variable">--variable</a> name@f=
ile (where "file" can be stdin if set to a single dash (-)). </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Variable contents can be expanded in option parameters =
using "{{name}}" if the option name is prefixed with "<span class=3D"emphas=
is">--expand-</span>". This gets the contents of the variable "name" insert=
ed, or a blank if the name does not exist as a variable. Insert "{{" verbat=
im in the string by prefixing it with a backslash, like "\{{". </p>
<p class=3D"level0">You access and expand environment variables by first im=
porting them. You select to either require the environment variable to be s=
et or you can provide a default value in case it is not already set. Plain =
"<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--variable=
">--variable</a> %name" imports the variable called "name" but exits with a=
n error if that environment variable is not already set. To provide a defau=
lt value if it is not set, use "<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--variable">--variable</a> %name=3Dcontent" or "<a cla=
ss=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--variable">--var=
iable</a> %name@content". </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Example. Get the USER environment variable into the URL=
, fail if USER is not set: </p><pre class=3D"level0">--variable '%USER'
--expand-url =3D "https://example.com/api/{{USER}}/method"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">When expanding variables, curl supports a set of functi=
ons that can make the variable contents more convenient to use. It can trim=
 leading and trailing white space with "trim", it can output the contents a=
s a JSON quoted string with "json", URL encode the string with "url", base6=
4 encode it with "b64" and base64 decode it with "64dec". To apply function=
s to a variable expansion, add them colon separated to the right side of th=
e variable. Variable content holding null bytes that are not encoded when e=
xpanded causes an error. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Example: get the contents of a file called $HOME/.secre=
t into a variable called "fix". Make sure that the content is trimmed and p=
ercent-encoded when sent as POST data: </p><pre class=3D"level0">--variable=
 %HOME
--expand-variable fix@{{HOME}}/.secret
--expand-data "{{fix:trim:url}}"
https://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">Command line variables and expansions were added in 8.3=
.0. </p><a name=3D"OUTPUT"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Output</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">If not told otherwise, curl writes the received data to=
 stdout. It can be instructed to instead save that data into a local file, =
using the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-output">--output</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a> options. If curl is given mu=
ltiple URLs to transfer on the command line, it similarly needs multiple op=
tions for where to save them. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">curl does not parse or otherwise "understand" the conte=
nt it gets or writes as output. It does no encoding or decoding, unless exp=
licitly asked to with dedicated command line options. </p><a name=3D"PROTOC=
OLS"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Protocols</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">curl supports numerous protocols, or put in URL terms: =
schemes. Your particular build may not support them all. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"DICT"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">DICT</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Lets you lookup words using online dictionaries. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"FILE"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">FILE</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Read or write local files. curl does not support access=
ing file:// URL remotely, but when running on Microsoft Windows using the n=
ative UNC approach works. Only absolute paths. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"FTPS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">FTP(S)</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports the File Transfer Protocol with a lot of =
tweaks and levers. With or without using TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"GOPHERS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">GOPHER(=
S)</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Retrieve files. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"HTTPS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HTTP(S)</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports HTTP with numerous options and variations=
. It can speak HTTP version 0.9, 1.0, 1.1, 2 and 3 depending on build optio=
ns and the correct command line options. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"IMAPS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">IMAP(S)</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Using the mail reading protocol, curl can download emai=
ls for you. With or without using TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"LDAPS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">LDAP(S)</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl can do directory lookups for you, with or without =
TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"MQTT"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">MQTT</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports MQTT version 3. Downloading over MQTT equ=
als subscribing to a topic while uploading/posting equals publishing on a t=
opic. MQTT over TLS is not supported (yet). </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"POP3S"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">POP3(S)</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Downloading from a pop3 server means getting an email. =
With or without using TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"RTMPS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">RTMP(S)</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The <span class=3D"bold">Realtime Messaging Protocol</s=
pan> is primarily used to serve streaming media and curl can download it. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"RTSP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">RTSP</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports RTSP 1.0 downloads. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SCP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SCP</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports SSH version 2 scp transfers. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SFTP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SFTP</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports SFTP (draft 5) done over SSH version 2. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SMBS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SMB(S)</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl supports SMB version 1 for upload and download. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SMTPS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SMTP(S)</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Uploading contents to an SMTP server means sending an e=
mail. With or without TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"TELNET"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">TELNET</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Fetching a telnet URL starts an interactive session whe=
re it sends what it reads on stdin and outputs what the server sends it. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"TFTP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">TFTP</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl can do TFTP downloads and uploads. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"WSS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">WS(S)</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">WebSocket done over HTTP/1. WSS implies that it works o=
ver HTTPS. </p><a name=3D"PROGRESS"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Progress mete=
r</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">curl normally displays a progress meter during operatio=
ns, indicating the amount of transferred data, transfer speeds and estimate=
d time left, etc. The progress meter displays the transfer rate in bytes pe=
r second. The used suffixes ("k" for kilo, "M" for mega, "G" for giga, "T" =
for tera, "P" for peta and "E" for exa) are 1024 based. For example 1k is 1=
024 bytes. 1M is 1048576 bytes. Strictly speaking this makes the units kibi=
byte and mebibyte etc. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">curl displays this data to the terminal by default, so =
if you invoke curl to do an operation and it is about to write data to the =
terminal, it <span class=3D"emphasis">disables</span> the progress meter as=
 otherwise it would mess up the output mixing progress meter and response d=
ata. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">If you want a progress meter for HTTP POST or PUT reque=
sts, you need to redirect the response output to a file, using shell redire=
ct (&gt;), <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--output">--output</a> or similar. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">This does not apply to FTP upload as that operation doe=
s not spit out any response data to the terminal. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">If you prefer a progress bar instead of the regular met=
er, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--progr=
ess-bar">--progress-bar</a> is your friend. You can also disable the progre=
ss meter completely with the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--silent">--silent</a> option. </p><a name=3D"VERSION"></=
a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Version</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">This man page describes curl 8.18.0. If you use a later=
 version, chances are this man page does not fully document it. If you use =
an earlier version, this document tries to include version information abou=
t which specific version that introduced changes. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">You can always learn which the latest curl version is b=
y running </p><pre class=3D"level0">curl https://curl.se/info
</pre>
<p class=3D"level0">The online version of this man page is always showing t=
he latest incarnation: <a href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html">https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html</a> </p><a name=3D"OPTIONS"></a><h2 class=3D"nr=
offsh">Options</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">Options start with one or two dashes. Many of the optio=
ns require an additional value next to them. If provided text does not star=
t with a dash, it is presumed to be and treated as a URL. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">The short "single-dash" form of the options, -d for exa=
mple, may be used with or without a space between it and its value, althoug=
h a space is a recommended separator. The long double-dash form, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> =
for example, requires a space between it and its value. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Short version options that do not need any additional v=
alues can be used immediately next to each other, like for example you can =
specify all the options <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#-O">-O</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#-L">-L</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#-v">-v</a> at once as <span class=3D"emphasis">-OLv</span=
>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">In general, all boolean options are enabled with --<spa=
n class=3D"bold">option</span> and yet again disabled with --<span class=3D=
"bold">no-</span>option. That is, you use the same option name but prefix i=
t with "no-". However, in this list we mostly only list and show the --<spa=
n class=3D"bold">option</span> version of them. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">When <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--next">--next</a> is used, it resets the parser state and yo=
u start again with a clean option state, except for the options that are gl=
obal. Global options retain their values and meaning even after <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--next">--next</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0">If the long option name ends with an equals sign ("=3D"=
), the argument is the text following on its right side. (Added in 8.16.0) =
</p>
<p class=3D"level0">The first argument that is exactly two dashes ("--"), m=
arks the end of options; any argument after the end of options is interpret=
ed as a URL argument even if it starts with a dash. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">curl does little to no verification of the contents of =
command line arguments. Passing in "creative octets" like newlines might tr=
igger unexpected results. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">The following options are global: <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail-early">--fail-early</a>, =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--libcurl">=
--libcurl</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--parallel-immediate">--parallel-immediate</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-max-host">--parallel-ma=
x-host</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--parallel-max">--parallel-max</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--progress-bar">--progress-bar</=
a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--rate"=
>--rate</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.htm=
l#--show-error">--show-error</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--stderr">--stderr</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--styled-output">--styled-output</a>,=
 <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-as=
cii">--trace-ascii</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--trace-config">--trace-config</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ids">--trace-ids</a>, <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-time">--tr=
ace-time</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--trace">--trace</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>. </p><a name=3D"ALL"></a><h2 clas=
s=3D"nroffsh">All options</h2>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--abstract-unix-socket"></a><span class=3D"n=
roffip">--abstract-unix-socket &lt;path&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Connect to the server through an abstract Unix d=
omain socket, instead of using the network. Note: netstat shows the path of=
 an abstract socket prefixed with "@", however the &lt;path&gt; argument sh=
ould not have this leading character. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--abstract-unix-socket">--abstract-unix-socket</a> is provided =
several times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --abstract-unix=
-socket socketpath https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--unix-socket">--unix-socket</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--alt-svc"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--alt=
-svc &lt;filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTPS) Enable the alt-svc parser. If the filename poin=
ts to an existing alt-svc cache file, that gets used. After a completed tra=
nsfer, the cache is saved to the filename again if it has been modified. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify a "" filename (zero length) to avoid loading/sa=
ving and make curl just handle the cache in memory. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You may want to restrict your umask to prevent other us=
ers on the same system to access the created file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is used several times, curl loads conten=
ts from all the files but the last one is used for saving. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--alt-svc">--alt-svc</a> can be used several times in a command li=
ne </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --alt-svc svc.t=
xt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--resolve">--resolve</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--connect-to">--connect-to</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--anyauth"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--any=
auth</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Figure out authentication method automatically, =
and use the most secure one the remote site claims to support. This is done=
 by first doing a request and checking the response-headers, thus possibly =
inducing an extra network round-trip. This option is used instead of settin=
g a specific authentication method, which you can do with <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--basic">--basic</a>, <a cla=
ss=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--digest">--diges=
t</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--nt=
lm">--ntlm</a>, and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--negotiate">--negotiate</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--anyauth">--anyauth</a> is not recommended if you do upload=
s from stdin, since it may require data to be sent twice and then the clien=
t must be able to rewind. If the need should arise when uploading from stdi=
n, the upload operation fails. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Used together with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --anyauth --use=
r me:pwd https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-anyauth">--proxy-anyauth</a>, <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--basic">--basic</a> and <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--digest">--dige=
st</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-a"></a><a name=3D"--append"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-a, --append</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP SFTP) When used in an upload, this option makes cu=
rl append to the target file instead of overwriting it. If the remote file =
does not exist, it is created. Note that this flag is ignored by some SFTP =
servers (including OpenSSH). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--append">--append</a> multiple times has no extra effec=
t. Disable it again with --no-append. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --upload-file l=
ocal --append ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--range">--range</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--continue-at">--continue-at</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--aws-sigv4"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--a=
ws-sigv4 &lt;provider1[:prvdr2[:reg[:srv]]]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use AWS V4 signature authentication in the trans=
fer. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The provider argument is a string that is used by the a=
lgorithm when creating outgoing authentication headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The region argument is a string that points to a geogra=
phic area of a resources collection (region-code) when the region name is o=
mitted from the endpoint. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The service argument is a string that points to a funct=
ion provided by a cloud (service-code) when the service name is omitted fro=
m the endpoint. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--aws-sigv4">--aws-sigv4</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --aws-sigv4 "aw=
s:amz:us-east-2:es" --user "key:secret" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.75.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--basic">--basic</a> and <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--basic"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--basic=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use HTTP Basic authentication with the remote ho=
st. This method is the default and this option is usually pointless, unless=
 you use it to override a previously set option that sets a different authe=
ntication method (such as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cu=
rl.se/docs/manpage.html#--digest">--digest</a>, or <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--negotiate">--negotiate</a>). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Used together with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--basic">--basic</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 Disable it again with --no-basic. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -u name:passwor=
d --basic https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-basic">--proxy-basic</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ca-native"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--c=
a-native</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the operating system's native CA store for ce=
rtificate verification. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is independent of other CA certificate loca=
tions set at run time or build time. Those locations are searched in additi=
on to the native CA store. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option works with OpenSSL and its forks (LibreSSL,=
 BoringSSL, etc) on Windows (Added in 7.71.0) and on Apple OS when libcurl =
is built with Apple SecTrust enabled. (Added in 8.17.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option works with wolfSSL on Windows, Linux (Debia=
n, Ubuntu, Gentoo, Fedora, RHEL), macOS, Android and iOS. (Added in 8.3.0) =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option works with GnuTLS (Added in 8.5.0) and also=
 uses Apple SecTrust when libcurl is built with it. (Added in 8.17.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option works with Rustls on Windows, macOS, Androi=
d and iOS. On Linux it is equivalent to using the Mozilla CA certificate bu=
ndle. When used with Rustls _only_ the native CA store is consulted, not ot=
her locations set at run time or build time. (Added in 8.13.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option currently has no effect for Schannel. This =
is the native TLS library from Microsoft, that by default uses the native C=
A store for verification unless overridden by a CA certificate location set=
ting. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ca-native">--ca-native</a> multiple times has no extra=
 effect. Disable it again with --no-ca-native. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ca-native htt=
ps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.2.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a>, <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath</a>, <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump-ca-embed=
">--dump-ca-embed</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ca-native">--proxy-ca-native</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--cacert"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--cace=
rt &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the specified certificate file to verify the =
peer. The file may contain multiple CA certificates. The certificate(s) mus=
t be in PEM format. Normally curl is built to use a default file for this, =
so this option is typically used to alter that default file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl recognizes the environment variable named 'CURL_CA=
_BUNDLE' if it is set and the TLS backend is not Schannel, and uses the giv=
en path as a path to a CA cert bundle. This option overrides that variable.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(Windows) curl automatically looks for a CA certs file =
named 'curl-ca-bundle.crt', either in the same directory as curl.exe, or in=
 the Current Working Directory, or in any folder along your PATH. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl 8.11.0 added a build-time option to disable this s=
earch behavior, and another option to restrict search to the application's =
directory. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(Schannel) This option is supported for Schannel in Win=
dows 7 or later (added in 7.60.0). This option is supported for backward co=
mpatibility with other SSL engines; instead it is recommended to use Window=
s' store of root certificates (the default for Schannel). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --cacert CA-fil=
e.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"htt=
ps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump-ca-embed">--dump-ca-embed</a> and <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--=
insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--capath"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--capa=
th &lt;dir&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the specified certificate directory to verify=
 the peer. If curl is built against OpenSSL, multiple paths can be provided=
 by separating them with the appropriate platform-specific separator (e.g. =
"path1:path2:path3" on Unix-style platforms for "path1;path2;path3" on Wind=
ows). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The certificates must be in PEM format, and if curl is =
built against OpenSSL, the directory must have been processed using the c_r=
ehash utility supplied with OpenSSL. Using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath</a> can allow OpenSSL-po=
wered curl to make SSL-connections much more efficiently than using <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert=
</a> if the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--cacert">--cacert</a> file contains many CA certificates. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is set, the default capath value is igno=
red. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--capath">--capath</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --capath /local=
/directory https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"htt=
ps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump-ca-embed">--dump-ca-embed</a> and <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--=
insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-E"></a><a name=3D"--cert"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-E, --cert &lt;certificate[:password]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the specified client certificate file when ge=
tting a file with HTTPS, FTPS or another SSL-based protocol. The certificat=
e must be PEM format. If the optional password is not specified, it is quer=
ied for on the terminal. Note that this option assumes a certificate file t=
hat is the private key and the client certificate concatenated. See <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert">--cert</a>=
 and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key"=
>--key</a> to specify them independently. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In the &lt;certificate&gt; portion of the argument, you=
 must escape the character ":" as "\:" so that it is not recognized as the =
password delimiter. Similarly, you must escape the double quote character a=
s \" so that it is not recognized as an escape character. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is built against OpenSSL, and the engine pkcs11=
 or pkcs11 provider is available, then a PKCS#11 URI (<a href=3D"https://ww=
w.ietf.org/rfc/rfc7512.txt">RFC 7512</a>) can be used to specify a certific=
ate located in a PKCS#11 device. A string beginning with "pkcs11:" is inter=
preted as a PKCS#11 URI. If a PKCS#11 URI is provided, then the <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--engine">--engine</a>=
 option is set as "pkcs11" if none was provided and the <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert-type">--cert-type</a> o=
ption is set as "ENG" or "PROV" if none was provided (depending on OpenSSL =
version). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is built against GnuTLS, a PKCS#11 URI can be u=
sed to specify a certificate located in a PKCS#11 device. A string beginnin=
g with "pkcs11:" is interpreted as a PKCS#11 URI. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(Schannel) Client certificates must be specified by a p=
ath expression to a certificate store. (Loading <span class=3D"emphasis">PF=
X</span> is not supported; you can import it to a store first). You can use=
 "&lt;store location&gt;\&lt;store name&gt;\&lt;thumbprint&gt;" to refer to=
 a certificate in the system certificates store, for example, <span class=
=3D"emphasis">"CurrentUser\MY\934a7ac6f8a5d579285a74fa61e19f23ddfe8d7a"</sp=
an>. Thumbprint is usually a SHA-1 hex string which you can see in certific=
ate details. Following store locations are supported: <span class=3D"emphas=
is">CurrentUser</span>, <span class=3D"emphasis">LocalMachine</span>, <span=
 class=3D"emphasis">CurrentService</span>, <span class=3D"emphasis">Service=
s</span>, <span class=3D"emphasis">CurrentUserGroupPolicy</span>, <span cla=
ss=3D"emphasis">LocalMachineGroupPolicy</span> and <span class=3D"emphasis"=
>LocalMachineEnterprise</span>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--cert">--cert</a> is provided several times, the last set valu=
e is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --cert certfile=
 --key keyfile https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cert-type">--cert-type</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key">--key</a> and <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key-type">--key-type</a>.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--cert-status"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-cert-status</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Verify the status of the server certificate by us=
ing the Certificate Status Request (aka. OCSP stapling) TLS extension. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is enabled and the server sends an inval=
id (e.g. expired) response, if the response suggests that the server certif=
icate has been revoked, or no response at all is received, the verification=
 fails. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This support is currently only implemented in the OpenS=
SL and GnuTLS backends. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--cert-status">--cert-status</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-cert-status. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --cert-status h=
ttps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--pinnedpubkey">--pinnedpubkey</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--cert-type"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--c=
ert-type &lt;type&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set type of the provided client certificate. PEM,=
 DER, ENG, PROV and P12 are recognized types. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The default type depends on the TLS backend and is usua=
lly PEM. For Schannel it is P12. If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert">--cert</a> is a pkcs11: URI then ENG or PR=
OV is the default type (depending on OpenSSL version). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--cert-type">--cert-type</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --cert-type PEM=
 --cert file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cert">--cert</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:/=
/curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key">--key</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key-type">--key-type</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ciphers"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--cip=
hers &lt;list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Specify which cipher suites to use in the connect=
ion if it negotiates TLS 1.2 (1.1, 1.0). The list of ciphers suites must sp=
ecify valid ciphers. Read up on cipher suite details on this URL: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html">https=
://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ciphers ECDHE=
-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tls13-ciphers">--tls13-ciphers</a>, <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ciphers">--proxy-ciphe=
rs</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--curves">--curves</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--compressed"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
compressed</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Request a compressed response using one of the a=
lgorithms curl supports, and automatically decompress the content. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Response headers are not modified when saved, so if the=
y are "interpreted" separately again at a later point they might appear to =
be saying that the content is (still) compressed; while in fact it has alre=
ady been decompressed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is used and the server sends an unsuppor=
ted encoding, curl reports an error. This is a request, not an order; the s=
erver may or may not deliver data compressed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--compressed">--compressed</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --no-compressed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --compressed ht=
tps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--compressed-ssh">--compressed-ssh</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--compressed-ssh"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--compressed-ssh</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SCP SFTP) Enable SSH compression. This is a request, n=
ot an order; the server may or may not do it. This allows the data to be se=
nt compressed over the wire, and automatically decompressed in the receivin=
g end, to save bandwidth. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--compressed-ssh">--compressed-ssh</a> multiple times ha=
s no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-compressed-ssh. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --compressed-ss=
h sftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--compressed">--compressed</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-K"></a><a name=3D"--config"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-K, --config &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify a text file to read curl arguments from. The co=
mmand line arguments found in the text file are used as if they were provid=
ed on the command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Options and their parameters must be specified on the s=
ame line in the file, separated by whitespace, colon, or the equals sign. L=
ong option names can optionally be given in the config file without the ini=
tial double dashes and if so, the colon or equals characters can be used as=
 separators. If the option is specified with one or two dashes, there can b=
e no colon or equals character between the option and its parameter. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the parameter contains whitespace or starts with a c=
olon (:) or equals sign (=3D), it must be specified enclosed within double =
quotes ("like this"). Within double quotes the following escape sequences a=
re available: \ \", \t, \n, \r and \v. A backslash preceding any other lett=
er is ignored. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the first non-blank column of a config line is a '#'=
 character, that line is treated as a comment. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Only write one option per physical line in the config f=
ile. A single line is required to be no more than 10 megabytes (since 8.2.0=
). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the filename to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a> as minus "-" to mak=
e curl read the file from stdin. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that to be able to specify a URL in the config fil=
e, you need to specify it using the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--url">--url</a> option, and not by simply writing=
 the URL on its own line. So, it could look similar to this: </p><pre class=
=3D"level1">url =3D "https://curl.se/docs/"
&nbsp;
# --- Example file ---
# this is a comment
url =3D "example.com"
output =3D "curlhere.html"
user-agent =3D "superagent/1.0"
&nbsp;
# and fetch another URL too
url =3D "example.com/docs/manpage.html"
-O
referer =3D "http://nowhereatall.example.com/"
# --- End of example file ---
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">When curl is invoked, it (unless <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--disable">--disable</a> is used)=
 checks for a default config file and uses it if found, even when <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config<=
/a> is used. The default config file is checked for in the following places=
 in this order: </p>
<p class=3D"level1">1) <span class=3D"bold">"$CURL_HOME/.curlrc"</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">2) <span class=3D"bold">"$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/curlrc"</span=
> (Added in 7.73.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">3) <span class=3D"bold">"$HOME/.curlrc"</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">4) Windows: <span class=3D"bold">"%USERPROFILE%\.curlrc=
"</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">5) Windows: <span class=3D"bold">"%APPDATA%\.curlrc"</s=
pan> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">6) Windows: <span class=3D"bold">"%USERPROFILE%\Applica=
tion Data\.curlrc"</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">7) Non-Windows: use getpwuid to find the home directory=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">8) On Windows, if it finds no <span class=3D"emphasis">=
.curlrc</span> file in the sequence described above, it checks for one in t=
he same directory the curl executable is placed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On Windows two filenames are checked per location: <spa=
n class=3D"emphasis">.curlrc</span> and <span class=3D"emphasis">_curlrc</s=
pan>, preferring the former. Older versions on Windows checked for <span cl=
ass=3D"emphasis">_curlrc</span> only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--config">--config</a> can be used several times in a command line=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --config file.t=
xt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--disable">--disable</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--connect-timeout"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--connect-timeout &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Maximum time in seconds that you allow curl's connectio=
n to take. This only limits the connection phase, so if curl connects withi=
n the given period it continues - if not it exits. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option accepts decimal values. The decimal value n=
eeds to be provided using a dot (.) as decimal separator - not the local ve=
rsion even if it might be using another separator. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The connection phase is considered complete when the DN=
S lookup and requested TCP, TLS or QUIC handshakes are done. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--connect-timeout">--connect-timeout</a> is provided several ti=
mes, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --connect-time=
out 20 https://example.com
curl --connect-timeout 3.14 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--max-time">--max-time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--connect-to"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
connect-to &lt;HOST1:PORT1:HOST2:PORT2&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For a request intended for the "HOST1:PORT1" pair, conn=
ect to "HOST2:PORT2" instead. This option is only used to establish the net=
work connection. It does NOT affect the hostname/port number that is used f=
or TLS/SSL (e.g. SNI, certificate verification) or for the application prot=
ocols. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">"HOST1" and "PORT1" may be empty strings, meaning any h=
ost or any port number. "HOST2" and "PORT2" may also be empty strings, mean=
ing use the request's original hostname and port number. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">A hostname specified to this option is compared as a st=
ring, so it needs to match the name used in the request URL. It can be eith=
er numerical such as "127.0.0.1" or the full host name such as "example.org=
". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: redirect connects from the example.com hostnam=
e to 127.0.0.1 independently of port number: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl=
 --connect-to example.com::127.0.0.1: https://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: redirect connects from all hostnames to 127.0.=
0.1 independently of port number: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --connect-=
to ::127.0.0.1: http://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--connect-to">--connect-to</a> can be used several times in a comm=
and line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --connect-to ex=
ample.com:443:example.net:8443 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--resolve">--resolve</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--header">--header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-C"></a><a name=3D"--continue-at"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-C, --continue-at &lt;offset&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Resume a previous transfer from the given byte offset. =
The given offset is the exact number of bytes that are skipped, counting fr=
om the beginning of the source file before it is transferred to the destina=
tion. If used with uploads, the FTP server command SIZE is not used by curl=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use "-C -" to instruct curl to automatically find out w=
here/how to resume the transfer. It then uses the given output/input files =
to figure that out. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When using this option for HTTP uploads using POST or P=
UT, functionality is not guaranteed. The HTTP protocol has no standard inte=
roperable resume upload and curl uses a set of headers for this purpose tha=
t once proved working for some servers and have been left for those who fin=
d that useful. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This command line option is mutually exclusive with <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--range">--ran=
ge</a>: you can only use one of them for a single transfer. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--no-clobber">--no-clobber</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remove-on-error">--remove-on-error<=
/a> options cannot be used together with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--continue-at">--continue-at</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--continue-at">--continue-at</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -C - https://e=
xample.com
curl -C 400 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--range">--range</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-b"></a><a name=3D"--cookie"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-b, --cookie &lt;data|filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) This option has two slightly separate cookie sen=
ding functions. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Either: pass the exact data to send to the HTTP server =
in the Cookie header. It is supposedly data previously received from the se=
rver in a "Set-Cookie:" line. The data should be in the format "NAME1=3DVAL=
UE1; NAME2=3DVALUE2". When given a set of specific cookies, curl populates =
its cookie header with this content explicitly in all outgoing request(s). =
If multiple requests are done due to authentication, followed redirects or =
similar, they all get this cookie header passed on. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Or: If no "=3D" symbol is used in the argument, it is i=
nstead treated as a filename to read previously stored cookie from. This op=
tion also activates the cookie engine which makes curl record incoming cook=
ies, which may be handy if you are using this in combination with the <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--lo=
cation</a> option or do multiple URL transfers on the same invoke. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the filename is a single minus ("-"), curl reads the=
 contents from stdin. If the filename is an empty string ("") and is the on=
ly cookie input, curl activates the cookie engine without any cookies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The file format of the file to read cookies from should=
 be plain HTTP headers (Set-Cookie style) or the Netscape/Mozilla cookie fi=
le format. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The file specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie">--cookie</a> is only used as inp=
ut. No cookies are written to that file. To store cookies, use the <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie-jar">--coo=
kie-jar</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you use the Set-Cookie file format and do not specif=
y a domain then the cookie is not sent since the domain never matches. To a=
ddress this, set a domain in Set-Cookie line (doing that includes subdomain=
s) or preferably: use the Netscape format. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Users often want to both read cookies from a file and w=
rite updated cookies back to a file, so using both <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie">--cookie</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie-jar">--coo=
kie-jar</a> in the same command line is common. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is built with PSL (<span class=3D"bold">Public =
Suffix List</span>) support, it detects and discards cookies that are speci=
fied for such suffix domains that should not be allowed to have cookies. If=
 curl is <span class=3D"emphasis">not</span> built with PSL support, it has=
 no ability to stop super cookies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--cookie">--cookie</a> can be used several times in a command line=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -b "" https://=
example.com
curl -b cookiefile https://example.com
curl -b cookiefile -c cookiefile https://example.com
curl -b name=3DJane https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cookie-jar">--cookie-jar</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--junk-session-cookies">--junk-se=
ssion-cookies</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-c"></a><a name=3D"--cookie-jar"></a><span c=
lass=3D"nroffip">-c, --cookie-jar &lt;filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Specify to which file you want curl to write all=
 cookies after a completed operation. curl writes all cookies from its in-m=
emory cookie storage to the given file at the end of operations. Even if no=
 cookies are known, a file is created so that it removes any formerly exist=
ing cookies from the file. The file uses the Netscape cookie file format. I=
f you set the filename to a single minus, "-", the cookies are written to s=
tdout. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The file specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie-jar">--cookie-jar</a> is only use=
d for output. No cookies are read from the file. To read cookies, use the <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie">--=
cookie</a> option. Both options can specify the same file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This command line option activates the cookie engine th=
at makes curl record and use cookies. The <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"htt=
ps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie">--cookie</a> option also activates=
 it. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the cookie jar cannot be created or written to, the =
whole curl operation does not fail or even report an error clearly. Using <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">-=
-verbose</a> gets a warning displayed, but that is the only visible feedbac=
k you get about this possibly lethal situation. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You may want to restrict your umask to prevent other us=
ers on the same system to access the created file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--cookie-jar">--cookie-jar</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -c store-here.=
txt https://example.com
curl -c store-here.txt -b read-these https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cookie">--cookie</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--junk-session-cookies">--junk-session-co=
okies</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--create-dirs"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-create-dirs</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When used in conjunction with the <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a> option, c=
url creates the necessary local directory hierarchy as needed. This option =
creates the directories mentioned with the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a> option combined with=
 the path possibly set with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--output-dir">--output-dir</a>. If the combined output fil=
ename uses no directory, or if the directories it mentions already exist, n=
o directories are created. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Created directories are made with mode 0750 on Unix-sty=
le file systems. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To create remote directories when using FTP or SFTP, tr=
y <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-cre=
ate-dirs">--ftp-create-dirs</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--create-dirs">--create-dirs</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-create-dirs. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --create-dirs -=
-output local/dir/file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-create-dirs">--ftp-create-dirs</a> and <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output-dir">--output-=
dir</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--create-file-mode"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--create-file-mode &lt;mode&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SFTP SCP FILE) When curl is used to create files remot=
ely using one of the supported protocols, this option allows the user to se=
t which 'mode' to set on the file at creation time, instead of the default =
0644. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option takes an octal number as argument. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--create-file-mode">--create-file-mode</a> is provided several =
times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --create-file-m=
ode 0777 -T localfile sftp://example.com/new
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.75.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-create-dirs">--ftp-create-dirs</a>=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--crlf"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--crlf</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP SMTP) Convert line feeds to carriage return plus l=
ine feeds in upload. Useful for <span class=3D"bold">MVS (OS/390)</span>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--crlf">--crlf</a> multiple times has no extra effect. D=
isable it again with --no-crlf. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --crlf -T file =
ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--use-ascii">--use-ascii</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--crlfile"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--crl=
file &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Provide a file using PEM format with a Certificat=
e Revocation List that may specify peer certificates that are to be conside=
red revoked. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--crlfile">--crlfile</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --crlfile rejec=
ts.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--curves"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--curv=
es &lt;list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set specific curves to use during SSL session est=
ablishment according to RFC 8422, 5.1. Multiple algorithms can be provided =
by separating them with ":" (e.g. "X25519:P-521"). The parameter is availab=
le identically in the OpenSSL "s_client" and "s_server" utilities. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--curves">--curves</a> allows a OpenSSL powered curl to make SSL-c=
onnections with exactly the (EC) curve requested by the client, avoiding no=
ntransparent client/server negotiations. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is set, the default curves list built in=
to OpenSSL are ignored. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--curves">--curves</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --curves X25519=
 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.73.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-d"></a><a name=3D"--data"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-d, --data &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP MQTT) Send the specified data in a POST request t=
o the HTTP server, in the same way that a browser does when a user has fill=
ed in an HTML form and presses the submit button. This option makes curl pa=
ss the data to the server using the content-type application/x-www-form-url=
encoded. Compared to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/man=
page.html#--form">--form</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--data-raw">--data-raw</a> is almost the same but does not have a =
special interpretation of the @ character. To post data purely binary, you =
should instead use the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--data-binary">--data-binary</a> option. To URL-encode the valu=
e of a form field you may use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--data-urlencode">--data-urlencode</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If any of these options is used more than once on the s=
ame command line, the data pieces specified are merged with a separating &a=
mp;-symbol. Thus, using '-d name=3Ddaniel -d skill=3Dlousy' would generate =
a post chunk that looks like 'name=3Ddaniel&amp;skill=3Dlousy'. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you start the data with the letter @, the rest shoul=
d be a filename to read the data from, or - if you want curl to read the da=
ta from stdin. Posting data from a file named 'foobar' would thus be done w=
ith <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data"=
>--data</a> @foobar. When <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> is told to read from a file like that, ca=
rriage returns, newlines and null bytes are stripped out. If you do not wan=
t the @ character to have a special interpretation use <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-raw">--data-raw</a> inst=
ead. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The data for this option is passed on to the server exa=
ctly as provided on the command line. curl does not convert, change or impr=
ove it. It is up to the user to provide the data in the correct form. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--data">--data</a> can be used several times in a command line </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -d "name=3Dcur=
l" https://example.com
curl -d "name=3Dcurl" -d "tool=3Dcmdline" https://example.com
curl -d @filename https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</a>, <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>=
 and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--uplo=
ad-file">--upload-file</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-binary">--data-binary</a>, <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-urlencode">--data-u=
rlencode</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--data-raw">--data-raw</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--data-ascii"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
data-ascii &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) This option is just an alias for <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--data-ascii">--data-ascii</a> can be used several times in a comm=
and line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --data-ascii @f=
ile https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--data-binary">--data-binary</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-raw">--data-raw</a> and <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-urlenco=
de">--data-urlencode</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--data-binary"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-data-binary &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Post data exactly as specified with no extra pro=
cessing whatsoever. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you start the data with the letter @, the rest shoul=
d be a filename. "@-" makes curl read the data from stdin. Data is posted i=
n a similar manner as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--data">--data</a> does, except that newlines and carriage retur=
ns are preserved and conversions are never done. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Like <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> the default content-type sent to the serve=
r is application/x-www-form-urlencoded. If you want the data to be treated =
as arbitrary binary data by the server then set the content-type to octet-s=
tream: -H "Content-Type: application/octet-stream". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is used several times, the ones followin=
g the first append data as described in <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--data-binary">--data-binary</a> can be used several times in a co=
mmand line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --data-binary @=
filename https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--data-ascii">--data-ascii</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--data-raw"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--da=
ta-raw &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Post data similarly to <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> but without the s=
pecial interpretation of the @ character. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--data-raw">--data-raw</a> can be used several times in a command =
line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --data-raw "he=
llo" https://example.com
curl --data-raw "@at@at@" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--data-urlencode"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--data-urlencode &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Post data, similar to the other <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> options =
with the exception that this performs URL-encoding. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To be CGI-compliant, the &lt;data&gt; part should begin=
 with a <span class=3D"emphasis">name</span> followed by a separator and a =
content specification. The &lt;data&gt; part can be passed to curl using on=
e of the following syntaxes: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"content"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">content=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">URL-encode the content and pass that on. Just be carefu=
l so that the content does not contain any "=3D" or "@" symbols, as that ma=
kes the syntax match one of the other cases below. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"content"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=3Dcont=
ent</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">URL-encode the content and pass that on. The preceding =
"=3D" symbol is not included in the data. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"namecontent"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">nam=
e=3Dcontent</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">URL-encode the content part and pass that on. Note that=
 the name part is expected to be URL-encoded already. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"filename"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">@filen=
ame</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">load data from the given file (including any newlines),=
 URL-encode that data and pass it on in the POST. Using "@-" makes curl rea=
d the data from stdin. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"namefilename"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">na=
me@filename</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">load data from the given file (including any newlines),=
 URL-encode that data and pass it on in the POST. The name part gets an equ=
al sign appended, resulting in <span class=3D"emphasis">name=3Durlencoded-f=
ile-content</span>. Note that the name is expected to be URL-encoded alread=
y. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--data-urlencode">--data-urlencode</a> can be used several times i=
n a command line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --data-urlenco=
de name=3Dval https://example.com
curl --data-urlencode =3Dencodethis https://example.com
curl --data-urlencode name@file https://example.com
curl --data-urlencode @fileonly https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-raw">--data-raw</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--delegation"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
delegation &lt;LEVEL&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(GSS/kerberos) Set LEVEL what curl is allowed to delega=
te when it comes to user credentials. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"none"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">none</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Do not allow any delegation. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"policy"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">policy</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Delegates if and only if the OK-AS-DELEGATE flag is set=
 in the Kerberos service ticket, which is a matter of realm policy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"always"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">always</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Unconditionally allow the server to delegate. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--delegation">--delegation</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --delegation "n=
one" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl">--ssl</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--digest"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--dige=
st</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Enable HTTP Digest authentication. This authenti=
cation scheme avoids sending the password over the wire in clear text. Use =
this in combination with the normal <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a> option to set username and pass=
word. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--digest">--digest</a> multiple times has no extra effec=
t. Disable it again with --no-digest. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -u name:passwor=
d --digest https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:/=
/curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-digest">--proxy-digest</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--anyauth">--anyaut=
h</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-q"></a><a name=3D"--disable"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">-q, --disable</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If used as the <span class=3D"bold">first</span> parame=
ter on the command line, the <span class=3D"emphasis">curlrc</span> config =
file is not read or used. See the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a> for details on the default co=
nfig file search path. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--disable">--disable</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-disable. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -q https://exam=
ple.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--disable-eprt"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--disable-eprt</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Disable the use of the EPRT and LPRT commands whe=
n doing active FTP transfers. curl normally first attempts to use EPRT befo=
re using PORT, but with this option, it uses PORT right away. EPRT is an ex=
tension to the original FTP protocol, and does not work on all servers, but=
 enables more functionality in a better way than the traditional PORT comma=
nd. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"emphasis">--eprt</span> can be used to e=
xplicitly enable EPRT again and <span class=3D"emphasis">--no-eprt</span> i=
s an alias for <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--disable-eprt">--disable-eprt</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the server is accessed using IPv6, this option has n=
o effect as EPRT is necessary then. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Disabling EPRT only changes the active behavior. If you=
 want to switch to passive mode you need to not use <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a> or forc=
e it with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-ftp-pasv">--ftp-pasv</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--disable-eprt">--disable-eprt</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-disable-eprt. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --disable-eprt =
ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--disable-epsv">--disable-epsv</a> and <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--disable-epsv"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--disable-epsv</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Disable the use of the EPSV command when doing pa=
ssive FTP transfers. curl normally first attempts to use EPSV before PASV, =
but with this option, it does not try EPSV. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"emphasis">--epsv</span> can be used to e=
xplicitly enable EPSV again and <span class=3D"emphasis">--no-epsv</span> i=
s an alias for <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--disable-epsv">--disable-epsv</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the server is an IPv6 host, this option has no effec=
t as EPSV is necessary then. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Disabling EPSV only changes the passive behavior. If yo=
u want to switch to active mode you need to use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--disable-epsv">--disable-epsv</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-disable-epsv. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --disable-epsv =
ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--disable-eprt">--disable-eprt</a> and <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--disallow-username-in-url"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">--disallow-username-in-url</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Exit with error if passed a URL containing a username. =
Probably most useful when the URL is being provided at runtime or similar. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">Accepting and using credentials in a URL is normally co=
nsidered a security hazard as they are easily leaked that way. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--disallow-username-in-url">--disallow-username-in-url</=
a> multiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-disallow-=
username-in-url. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --disallow-user=
name-in-url https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proto">--proto</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--dns-interface"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--dns-interface &lt;interface&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) Send outgoing DNS requests through the given inte=
rface. This option is a counterpart to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--interface">--interface</a> (which does not af=
fect DNS). The supplied string must be an interface name (not an address). =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--dns-interface">--dns-interface</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --dns-interface=
 eth0 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--dns-interface">--dns-interface</a> to work, it requires that=
 the underlying libcurl is built to support c-ares. See also <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dns-ipv4-addr">--dns-ip=
v4-addr</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--dns-ipv6-addr">--dns-ipv6-addr</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--dns-ipv4-addr"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--dns-ipv4-addr &lt;address&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) Bind to a specific IP address when making IPv4 DN=
S requests, so that the DNS requests originate from this address. The argum=
ent should be a single IPv4 address. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--dns-ipv4-addr">--dns-ipv4-addr</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --dns-ipv4-addr=
 10.1.2.3 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--dns-ipv4-addr">--dns-ipv4-addr</a> to work, it requires that=
 the underlying libcurl is built to support c-ares. See also <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dns-interface">--dns-in=
terface</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--dns-ipv6-addr">--dns-ipv6-addr</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--dns-ipv6-addr"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--dns-ipv6-addr &lt;address&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) Bind to a specific IP address when making IPv6 DN=
S requests, so that the DNS requests originate from this address. The argum=
ent should be a single IPv6 address. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--dns-ipv6-addr">--dns-ipv6-addr</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --dns-ipv6-addr=
 2a04:4e42::561 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--dns-ipv6-addr">--dns-ipv6-addr</a> to work, it requires that=
 the underlying libcurl is built to support c-ares. See also <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dns-interface">--dns-in=
terface</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--dns-ipv4-addr">--dns-ipv4-addr</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--dns-servers"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-dns-servers &lt;addresses&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) Set the list of DNS servers to be used instead of=
 the system default. The list of IP addresses should be separated with comm=
as. Port numbers may also optionally be given, appended to the IP address s=
eparated with a colon. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--dns-servers">--dns-servers</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --dns-servers =
192.168.0.1,192.168.0.2 https://example.com
curl --dns-servers 10.0.0.1:53 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--dns-servers">--dns-servers</a> to work, it requires that the=
 underlying libcurl is built to support c-ares. See also <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dns-interface">--dns-interf=
ace</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--dns-ipv4-addr">--dns-ipv4-addr</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--doh-cert-status"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--doh-cert-status</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) Same as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert-status">--cert-status</a> but used for DoH (D=
NS-over-HTTPS). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Verify the status of the DoH servers' certificate by us=
ing the Certificate Status Request (aka. OCSP stapling) TLS extension. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is enabled and the DoH server sends an i=
nvalid (e.g. expired) response, if the response suggests that the server ce=
rtificate has been revoked, or no response at all is received, the verifica=
tion fails. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This support is currently only implemented in the OpenS=
SL and GnuTLS backends. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--doh-cert-status">--doh-cert-status</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-doh-cert-status. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --doh-cert-stat=
us --doh-url https://doh.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.76.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--doh-insecure">--doh-insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--doh-insecure"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--doh-insecure</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) By default, every connection curl makes to a DoH =
server is verified to be secure before the transfer takes place. This optio=
n tells curl to skip the verification step and proceed without checking. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: using this option =
makes the DoH transfer and name resolution insecure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> and <a cla=
ss=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-insecure"=
>--proxy-insecure</a> but used for DoH (DNS-over-HTTPS) only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--doh-insecure">--doh-insecure</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-doh-insecure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --doh-insecure =
--doh-url https://doh.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.76.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--doh-url">--doh-url</a>, <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a=
> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--pro=
xy-insecure">--proxy-insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--doh-url"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--doh=
-url &lt;URL&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(DNS) Specify which DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) server to use =
to resolve hostnames, instead of using the default name resolver mechanism.=
 The URL must be HTTPS. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Some SSL options that you set for your transfer also ap=
ply to DoH since the name lookups take place over SSL. However, the certifi=
cate verification settings are not inherited but are controlled separately =
via <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--doh-i=
nsecure">--doh-insecure</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--doh-cert-status">--doh-cert-status</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By default, DoH is bypassed when initially looking up D=
NS records of the DoH server. You can specify the IP address(es) of the DoH=
 server with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.htm=
l#--resolve">--resolve</a> to avoid this. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is unset if an empty string "" is used as t=
he URL. (Added in 7.85.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--doh-url">--doh-url</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --doh-url http=
s://doh.example https://example.com
curl --doh-url https://doh.example --resolve doh.example:443:192.0.2.1 http=
s://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--doh-insecure">--doh-insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--dump-ca-embed"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--dump-ca-embed</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Write the CA bundle embedded in curl to standard =
output, then quit. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl was not built with a default CA bundle embedded=
, the output is empty. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--dump-ca-embed">--dump-ca-embed</a> multiple times has =
no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-dump-ca-embed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --dump-ca-embed
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.10.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ca-native">--ca-native</a>, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert<=
/a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capa=
th">--capath</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpag=
e.html#--proxy-ca-native">--proxy-ca-native</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-cacert">--proxy-cacert</a> an=
d <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-c=
apath">--proxy-capath</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-D"></a><a name=3D"--dump-header"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-D, --dump-header &lt;filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP FTP) Write the received protocol headers to the s=
pecified file. If no headers are received, the use of this option creates a=
n empty file. Specify "-" as filename (a single minus) to have it written t=
o stdout. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in curl 8.10.0, specify "%" (a single percent =
sign) as filename writes the output to stderr. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When used in FTP, the FTP server response lines are con=
sidered being "headers" and thus are saved there. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in curl 8.11.0, using the <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--create-dirs">--create-dirs</a=
> option can also create missing directory components for the path provided=
 in <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump-=
header">--dump-header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Having multiple transfers in one set of operations (i.e=
. the URLs in one <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpag=
e.html#--next">--next</a> clause), appends them to the same file, separated=
 by a blank line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--dump-header">--dump-header</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --dump-header =
store.txt https://example.com
curl --dump-header - https://example.com -o save
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ech"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ech &lt=
;config&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTPS) Specify how to do ECH (Encrypted Client Hello).=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The values allowed for &lt;config&gt; can be: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"false"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">false</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Do not attempt ECH. The is the default. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"grease"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">grease</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Send a GREASE ECH extension </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"true"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">true</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Attempt ECH if possible, but do not fail if ECH is not =
attempted. (The connection fails if ECH is attempted but fails.) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"hard"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">hard</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Attempt ECH and fail if that is not possible. ECH only =
works with TLS 1.3 and also requires using DoH or providing an ECHConfigLis=
t on the command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"eclb64val"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ecl:&=
lt;b64val&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">A base64 encoded ECHConfigList that is used for ECH. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"pnname"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">pn:&lt;n=
ame&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">A name to use to over-ride the "public_name" field of a=
n ECHConfigList (only available with OpenSSL TLS support) </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Most ECH related errors cause error <span class=3D"emph=
asis">CURLE_ECH_REQUIRED</span> (101). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ech">--ech</a> is provided several times, the last set value =
is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ech true http=
s://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.8.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--doh-url">--doh-url</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--egd-file"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--eg=
d-file &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Deprecated option (added in 7.84.0). Prior to tha=
t it only had an effect on curl if built to use old versions of OpenSSL. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the path name to the Entropy Gathering Daemon s=
ocket. The socket is used to seed the random engine for SSL connections. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--egd-file">--egd-file</a> is provided several times, the last =
set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --egd-file /ran=
dom/here https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--random-file">--random-file</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--engine"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--engi=
ne &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Select the OpenSSL crypto engine to use for ciphe=
r operations. Use "<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--engine">--engine</a> list" to print a list of build-time supporte=
d engines. Note that not all (and possibly none) of the engines may be avai=
lable at runtime. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The OpenSSL concept "engines" has been superseded by "p=
roviders" in OpenSSL 3, and this option should work fine to specify such as=
 well. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--engine">--engine</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --engine flavor=
 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--curves">--curves</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--etag-compare"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--etag-compare &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Make a conditional HTTP request for the specific=
 ETag read from the given file by sending a custom If-None-Match header usi=
ng the stored ETag. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For correct results, make sure that the specified file =
contains only a single line with the desired ETag. A non-existing or empty =
file is treated as an empty ETag. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the option <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cu=
rl.se/docs/manpage.html#--etag-save">--etag-save</a> to first save the ETag=
 from a response, and then use this option to compare against the saved ETa=
g in a subsequent request. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use this option with a single URL only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--etag-compare">--etag-compare</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --etag-compare =
etag.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.68.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--etag-save">--etag-save</a> and <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--time-cond">--tim=
e-cond</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--etag-save"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--e=
tag-save &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Save an HTTP ETag to the specified file. An ETag=
 is a caching related header, usually returned in a response. Use this opti=
on with a single URL only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If no ETag is sent by the server, an empty file is crea=
ted. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In many situations you want to use an existing etag in =
the request to avoid downloading the same resource again but also save the =
new etag if it has indeed changed, by using both etag options <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--etag-save">--etag-save=
</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
etag-compare">--etag-compare</a> with the same filename, in the same comman=
d line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in curl 8.12.0, using the <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--create-dirs">--create-dirs</a=
> option can also create missing directory components for the path provided=
 in <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--etag-=
save">--etag-save</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--etag-save">--etag-save</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --etag-save sto=
retag.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.68.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--etag-compare">--etag-compare</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--expect100-timeout"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--expect100-timeout &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Maximum time in seconds that you allow curl to w=
ait for a 100-continue response when curl emits an Expects: 100-continue he=
ader in its request. By default curl waits one second. This option accepts =
decimal values. When curl stops waiting, it continues as if a response was =
received. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The decimal value needs to be provided using a dot ("."=
) as decimal separator - not the local version even if it might be using an=
other separator. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--expect100-timeout">--expect100-timeout</a> is provided severa=
l times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --expect100-tim=
eout 2.5 -T file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--connect-timeout">--connect-timeout</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-f"></a><a name=3D"--fail"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-f, --fail</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Fail with error code 22 and with no response bod=
y output at all for HTTP transfers returning HTTP response codes at 400 or =
greater. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In normal cases when an HTTP server fails to deliver a =
document, it returns a body of text stating so (which often also describes =
why and more) and a 4xx HTTP response code. This command line option preven=
ts curl from outputting that data and instead returns error 22 early. By de=
fault, curl does not consider HTTP response codes to indicate failure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To get both the error code and also save the content, u=
se <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail-w=
ith-body">--fail-with-body</a> instead. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This method is not fail-safe and there are occasions wh=
ere non-successful response codes slip through, especially when authenticat=
ion is involved (response codes 401 and 407). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</a> multiple times has no extra effect. D=
isable it again with --no-fail. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --fail https://=
example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail-with-body">--fail-wi=
th-body</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--fail-with-body">--fail-with-body</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail-early">--fail-early</a>. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--fail-early"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
fail-early</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Fail and exit on the first detected transfer error. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">When curl is used to do multiple transfers on the comma=
nd line, it attempts to operate on each given URL, one by one. By default, =
it ignores errors if there are more URLs given and the last URL's success d=
etermines the error code curl returns. Early failures are "hidden" by subse=
quent successful transfers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Using this option, curl instead returns an error on the=
 first transfer that fails, independent of the amount of URLs that are give=
n on the command line. This way, no transfer failures go undetected by scri=
pts and similar. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not imply <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</a>, which causes tran=
sfers to fail due to the server's HTTP status code. You can combine the two=
 options, however note <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--fail">--fail</a> is not global and is therefore contained by =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--next">--n=
ext</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--fail-early">--fail-early</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --no-fail-early. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --fail-early ht=
tps://example.com https://two.example
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail-with-body">--fail-with-body</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--fail-with-body"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--fail-with-body</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Return an error on server errors where the HTTP =
response code is 400 or greater). In normal cases when an HTTP server fails=
 to deliver a document, it returns an HTML document stating so (which often=
 also describes why and more). This option allows curl to output and save t=
hat content but also to return error 22. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This is an alternative option to <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</a> which makes cu=
rl fail for the same circumstances but without saving the content. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--fail-with-body">--fail-with-body</a> multiple times ha=
s no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-fail-with-body. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --fail-with-bod=
y https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</a>. Added i=
n 7.76.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--fail">--fail</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.s=
e/docs/manpage.html#--fail-early">--fail-early</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--false-start"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-false-start</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) No TLS backend currently supports this feature. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use false start during the TLS handshake. False start i=
s a mode where a TLS client starts sending application data before verifyin=
g the server's Finished message, thus saving a round trip when performing a=
 full handshake. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--false-start">--false-start</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-false-start. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --false-start h=
ttps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tcp-fastopen">--tcp-fastopen</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--follow"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--foll=
ow</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Instructs curl to follow HTTP redirects and to d=
o the custom request method set with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a> when following redirects=
 as the HTTP specification says. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The method string set with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a> is used in s=
ubsequent requests for the status codes 307 or 308, but may be reset to GET=
 for 301, 302 and 303. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This is subtly different than <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a>, as that =
option always set the custom method in all subsequent requests independent =
of response code. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Restrict which protocols a redirect is accepted to foll=
ow with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--p=
roto-redir">--proto-redir</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a> multiple times has no extra effec=
t. Disable it again with --no-follow. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -X POST --follo=
w https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.16.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a>, <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a=
>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto-=
redir">--proto-redir</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--max-redirs">--max-redirs</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-F"></a><a name=3D"--form"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-F, --form &lt;name=3Dcontent&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP SMTP IMAP) For the HTTP protocol family, emulate =
a filled-in form in which a user has pressed the submit button. This makes =
curl POST data using the Content-Type multipart/form-data according to <a h=
ref=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2388.txt">RFC 2388</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For SMTP and IMAP protocols, this composes a multipart =
mail message to transmit. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This enables uploading of binary files etc. To force th=
e 'content' part to be a file, prefix the filename with an @ sign. To just =
get the content part from a file, prefix the filename with the symbol &lt;.=
 The difference between @ and &lt; is then that @ makes a file get attached=
 in the post as a file upload, while the &lt; makes a text field and just g=
ets the contents for that text field from a file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Read content from stdin instead of a file by using a si=
ngle "-" as filename. This goes for both @ and &lt; constructs. When stdin =
is used, the contents is buffered in memory first by curl to determine its =
size and allow a possible resend. Defining a part's data from a named non-r=
egular file (such as a named pipe or similar) is not subject to buffering a=
nd is instead read at transmission time; since the full size is unknown bef=
ore the transfer starts, such data is sent as chunks by HTTP and rejected b=
y IMAP. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: send an image to an HTTP server, where 'profil=
e' is the name of the form-field to which the file <span class=3D"bold">por=
trait.jpg</span> is the input: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F profile=3D=
@portrait.jpg https://example.com/upload.cgi
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: send your name and shoe size in two text field=
s to the server: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F name=3DJohn -F shoesize=
=3D11 https://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: send your essay in a text field to the server.=
 Send it as a plain text field, but get the contents for it from a local fi=
le: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F "story=3D&lt;hugefile.txt" https://ex=
ample.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">You can also instruct curl what Content-Type to use by =
using "type=3D", in a manner similar to: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F =
"web=3D@index.html;type=3Dtext/html" example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">or </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F "name=3Ddaniel;typ=
e=3Dtext/foo" example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">You can also explicitly change the name field of a file=
 upload part by setting filename=3D, like this: </p><pre class=3D"level1">c=
url -F "file=3D@localfile;filename=3Dnameinpost" example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">If filename/path contains ',' or ';', it must be quoted=
 by double-quotes like: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F "file=3D@\"local,=
file\";filename=3D\"name;in;post\"" \
&nbsp;   https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">or </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F 'file=3D@"local,fi=
le";filename=3D"name;in;post"' \
&nbsp;   https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that if a filename/path is quoted by double-quotes=
, any double-quote or backslash within the filename must be escaped by back=
slash. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Quoting must also be applied to non-file data if it con=
tains semicolons, leading/trailing spaces or leading double quotes: </p><pr=
e class=3D"level1">curl -F 'colors=3D"red; green; blue";type=3Dtext/x-myapp=
' \
&nbsp;  https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">You can add custom headers to the field by setting head=
ers=3D, like </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F "submit=3DOK;headers=3D\"X-s=
ubmit-type: OK\"" example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">or </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F "submit=3DOK;heade=
rs=3D@headerfile" example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">The headers=3D keyword may appear more than once and ab=
ove notes about quoting apply. When headers are read from a file, empty lin=
es and lines starting with '#' are ignored; each header can be folded by sp=
litting between two words and starting the continuation line with a space; =
embedded carriage-returns and trailing spaces are stripped. Here is an exam=
ple of a header file contents: </p><pre class=3D"level1"># This file contai=
ns two headers.
X-header-1: this is a header
&nbsp;
# The following header is folded.
X-header-2: this is
&nbsp;another header
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">To support sending multipart mail messages, the syntax =
is extended as follows: </p>
<p class=3D"level1">- name can be omitted: the equal sign is the first char=
acter of the argument, </p>
<p class=3D"level1">- if data starts with '(', this signals to start a new =
multipart: it can be followed by a content type specification. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">- a multipart can be terminated with a '=3D)' argument.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: the following command sends an SMTP mime email=
 consisting in an inline part in two alternative formats: plain text and HT=
ML. It attaches a text file: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F '=3D(;type=
=3Dmultipart/alternative' \
&nbsp;    -F '=3Dplain text message' \
&nbsp;    -F '=3D &lt;body&gt;HTML message&lt;/body&gt;;type=3Dtext/html' \
&nbsp;    -F '=3D)' -F '=3D@textfile.txt' ...  smtp://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Data can be encoded for transfer using encoder=3D. Avai=
lable encodings are <span class=3D"emphasis">binary</span> and <span class=
=3D"emphasis">8bit</span> that do nothing else than adding the correspondin=
g Content-Transfer-Encoding header, <span class=3D"emphasis">7bit</span> th=
at only rejects 8-bit characters with a transfer error, <span class=3D"emph=
asis">quoted-printable</span> and <span class=3D"emphasis">base64</span> th=
at encodes data according to the corresponding schemes, limiting lines leng=
th to 76 characters. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: send multipart mail with a quoted-printable te=
xt message and a base64 attached file: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -F '=
=3Dtext message;encoder=3Dquoted-printable' \
&nbsp;    -F '=3D@localfile;encoder=3Dbase64' ... smtp://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--form">--form</a> can be used several times in a command line </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --form "name=3D=
curl" --form "file=3D@loadthis" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a>, <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>=
 and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--uplo=
ad-file">--upload-file</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form-string">--form-string</a> and <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form-escape"=
>--form-escape</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--form-escape"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-form-escape</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP IMAP SMTP) Pass on names of multipart form fields=
 and files using backslash-escaping instead of percent-encoding. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--form-escape">--form-escape</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --form-escape -=
F 'field\name=3Dcurl' -F 'file=3D@load"this' https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.81.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--form-string"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-form-string &lt;name=3Dstring&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP SMTP IMAP) Similar to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</a> except that the va=
lue string for the named parameter is used literally. Leading @ and &lt; ch=
aracters, and the ";type=3D" string in the value have no special meaning. U=
se this in preference to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--form">--form</a> if there is any possibility that the strin=
g value may accidentally trigger the @ or &lt; features of <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--form-string">--form-string</a> can be used several times in a co=
mmand line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --form-string "=
name=3Ddata" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-account"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-ftp-account &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) When an FTP server asks for "account data" after =
username and password has been provided, this data is sent off using the AC=
CT command. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ftp-account">--ftp-account</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-account "=
mr.robot" ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-alternative-to-user"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">--ftp-alternative-to-user &lt;command&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) If authenticating with the USER and PASS commands=
 fails, send this command. When connecting to Tumbleweed's Secure Transport=
 server over FTPS using a client certificate, using "SITE AUTH" tells the s=
erver to retrieve the username from the certificate. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ftp-alternative-to-user">--ftp-alternative-to-user</a> is pro=
vided several times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-alternati=
ve-to-user "U53r" ftp://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-account">--ftp-account</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-create-dirs"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--ftp-create-dirs</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP SFTP) When an FTP or SFTP URL/operation uses a pat=
h that does not currently exist on the server, the standard behavior of cur=
l is to fail. Using this option, curl instead attempts to create missing di=
rectories. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-create-dirs">--ftp-create-dirs</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ftp-create-dirs. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-create-di=
rs -T file ftp://example.com/remote/path/file
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--create-dirs">--create-dirs</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-method"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
ftp-method &lt;method&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Control what method curl should use to reach a fi=
le on an FTP(S) server. The method argument should be one of the following =
alternatives: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"multicwd"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">multic=
wd</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Do a single CWD operation for each path part in the giv=
en URL. For deep hierarchies this means many commands. This is how <a href=
=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc1738.txt">RFC 1738</a> says it should be do=
ne. This is the default but the slowest behavior. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"nocwd"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">nocwd</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Do no CWD at all. curl does SIZE, RETR, STOR etc and gi=
ves the full path to the server for each of these commands. This is the fas=
test behavior. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"singlecwd"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">singl=
ecwd</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Do one CWD with the full target directory and then oper=
ate on the file "normally" (like in the multicwd case). This is somewhat mo=
re standards compliant than "nocwd" but without the full penalty of "multic=
wd". </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ftp-method">--ftp-method</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-method m=
ulticwd ftp://example.com/dir1/dir2/file
curl --ftp-method nocwd ftp://example.com/dir1/dir2/file
curl --ftp-method singlecwd ftp://example.com/dir1/dir2/file
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--list-only">--list-only</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-pasv"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ft=
p-pasv</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Use passive mode for the data connection. Passive=
 is the internal default behavior, but using this option can be used to ove=
rride a previous <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Reversing an enforced passive really is not doable but =
you must then instead enforce the correct <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"htt=
ps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a> again. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Passive mode means that curl tries the EPSV command fir=
st and then PASV, unless <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--disable-epsv">--disable-epsv</a> is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-pasv">--ftp-pasv</a> multiple times has no extra e=
ffect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-pasv ftp:=
//example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a>.=
 See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-disable-epsv">--disable-epsv</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-P"></a><a name=3D"--ftp-port"></a><span cla=
ss=3D"nroffip">-P, --ftp-port &lt;address&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Reverse the default initiator/listener roles when=
 connecting with FTP. This option makes curl use active mode. curl then com=
mands the server to connect back to the client's specified address and port=
, while passive mode asks the server to setup an IP address and port for it=
 to connect to. &lt;address&gt; should be one of: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"interface"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">inter=
face</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">e.g. <span class=3D"bold">eth0</span> to specify which =
interface's IP address you want to use (Unix only) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"IP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">IP address</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">e.g. <span class=3D"bold">192.168.10.1</span> to specif=
y the exact IP address </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"hostname"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">hostna=
me</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">e.g. <span class=3D"bold">my.host.domain</span> to spec=
ify the machine </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"-"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">make curl pick the same IP address that is already used=
 for the control connection. This is the recommended choice. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Disable the use of PORT with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-pasv">--ftp-pasv</a>. Disable t=
he attempt to use the EPRT command instead of PORT by using <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--disable-eprt">--disable-=
eprt</a>. EPRT is really PORT++. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You can also append ":[start]-[end]" to the right of th=
e address, to tell curl what TCP port range to use. That means you specify =
a port range, from a lower to a higher number. A single number works as wel=
l, but do note that it increases the risk of failure since the port may not=
 be available. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a> is provided several times, the last =
set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -P - ftp:/exam=
ple.com
curl -P eth0 ftp:/example.com
curl -P 192.168.0.2 ftp:/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-pasv">--ftp-pasv</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--disable-eprt">--disable-eprt</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-pret"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ft=
p-pret</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Send a PRET command before PASV (and EPSV). Certa=
in FTP servers, mainly drftpd, require this non-standard command for direct=
ory listings as well as up and downloads in PASV mode. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-pret">--ftp-pret</a> multiple times has no extra e=
ffect. Disable it again with --no-ftp-pret. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-pret ftp:=
//example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">--ftp-port</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-pasv">--ftp-pasv</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-skip-pasv-ip"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--ftp-skip-pasv-ip</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Do not use the IP address the server suggests in =
its response to curl's PASV command when curl connects the data connection.=
 Instead curl reuses the same IP address it already uses for the control co=
nnection. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is enabled by default (added in 7.74.0). </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option has no effect if PORT, EPRT or EPSV is used=
 instead of PASV. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-skip-pasv-ip">--ftp-skip-pasv-ip</a> multiple time=
s has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ftp-skip-pasv-ip. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-skip-pasv=
-ip ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-pasv">--ftp-pasv</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-ssl-ccc"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-ftp-ssl-ccc</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Use CCC (Clear Command Channel) Shuts down the SS=
L/TLS layer after authenticating. The rest of the control channel communica=
tion is unencrypted. This allows NAT routers to follow the FTP transaction.=
 The default mode is passive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-ccc">--ftp-ssl-ccc</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-ftp-ssl-ccc. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-ssl-ccc f=
tps://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ssl">--ssl</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-ccc-mode">--ftp-ssl-ccc-mode</a>. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-ssl-ccc-mode"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--ftp-ssl-ccc-mode &lt;active/passive&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Set the CCC mode. The passive mode does not initi=
ate the shutdown, but instead waits for the server to do it, and does not r=
eply to the shutdown from the server. The active mode initiates the shutdow=
n and waits for a reply from the server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-ccc-mode">--ftp-ssl-ccc-mode</a> multiple time=
s has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ftp-ssl-ccc-mode. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-ssl-ccc-m=
ode active --ftp-ssl-ccc ftps://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-ccc">--ftp-ssl-ccc</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ftp-ssl-control"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--ftp-ssl-control</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Require SSL/TLS for the FTP login, clear for tran=
sfer. Allows secure authentication, but non-encrypted data transfers for ef=
ficiency. Fails the transfer if the server does not support SSL/TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-control">--ftp-ssl-control</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ftp-ssl-control. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ftp-ssl-contr=
ol ftp://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ssl">--ssl</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-G"></a><a name=3D"--get"></a><span class=3D=
"nroffip">-G, --get</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) When used, this option makes all data specified =
with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data=
">--data</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--data-binary">--data-binary</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-urlencode">--data-urlencode</a> to be us=
ed in an HTTP GET request instead of the POST request that otherwise would =
be used. curl appends the provided data to the URL as a query string. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If used in combination with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>, the POST data is =
instead appended to the URL with a HEAD request. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--get">--get</a> multiple times has no extra effect. Dis=
able it again with --no-get. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --get https://=
example.com
curl --get -d "tool=3Dcurl" -d "age=3Dold" https://example.com
curl --get -I -d "tool=3Dcurl" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-g"></a><a name=3D"--globoff"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">-g, --globoff</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Switch off the URL globbing function. When you set this=
 option, you can specify URLs that contain the letters {}[] without having =
curl itself interpret them. Note that these letters are not normal legal UR=
L contents but they should be encoded according to the URI standard. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl detects numerical IPv6 addresses when used in URLs=
 and excludes them from the treatment, so they can still be used without ha=
ving to disable globbing. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--globoff">--globoff</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-globoff. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -g "https://exa=
mple.com/{[]}}}}"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--disable">--disable</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--happy-eyeballs-timeout-ms"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">--happy-eyeballs-timeout-ms &lt;ms&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the timeout for Happy Eyeballs. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Happy Eyeballs is an algorithm that attempts to connect=
 to both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses for dual-stack hosts, giving IPv6 a head-s=
tart of the specified number of milliseconds. If the IPv6 address cannot be=
 connected to within that time, then a connection attempt is made to the IP=
v4 address in parallel. The first connection to be established is the one t=
hat is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The range of suggested useful values is limited. Happy =
Eyeballs <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6555.txt">RFC 6555</a> says=
 "It is RECOMMENDED that connection attempts be paced 150-250 ms apart to b=
alance human factors against network load." libcurl currently defaults to 2=
00 ms. Firefox and Chrome currently default to 300 ms. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--happy-eyeballs-timeout-ms">--happy-eyeballs-timeout-ms</a> is=
 provided several times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --happy-eyeball=
s-timeout-ms 500 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--max-time">--max-time</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--connect-timeout">--connect-timeout<=
/a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--haproxy-clientip"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--haproxy-clientip &lt;ip&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Set a client IP in HAProxy PROXY protocol v1 hea=
der at the beginning of the connection. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For valid requests, IPv4 addresses must be indicated as=
 a series of exactly 4 integers in the range [0..255] inclusive written in =
decimal representation separated by exactly one dot between each other. Hea=
ding zeroes are not permitted in front of numbers in order to avoid any pos=
sible confusion with octal numbers. IPv6 addresses must be indicated as ser=
ies of 4 hexadecimal digits (upper or lower case) delimited by colons betwe=
en each other, with the acceptance of one double colon sequence to replace =
the largest acceptable range of consecutive zeroes. The total number of dec=
oded bits must be exactly 128. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Otherwise, any string can be accepted for the client IP=
 and get sent. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">It replaces <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--haproxy-protocol">--haproxy-protocol</a> if used, it=
 is not necessary to specify both flags. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--haproxy-clientip">--haproxy-clientip</a> is provided several =
times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --haproxy-clien=
tip $IP
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.2.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--haproxy-protocol"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--haproxy-protocol</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Send a HAProxy PROXY protocol v1 header at the b=
eginning of the connection. This is used by some load balancers and reverse=
 proxies to indicate the client's true IP address and port. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is primarily useful when sending test reque=
sts to a service that expects this header. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--haproxy-protocol">--haproxy-protocol</a> multiple time=
s has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-haproxy-protocol. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --haproxy-proto=
col https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-I"></a><a name=3D"--head"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-I, --head</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP FTP FILE) Fetch the headers only. HTTP-servers fe=
ature the command HEAD which this uses to get nothing but the header of a d=
ocument. When used on an FTP or FILE URL, curl displays the file size and l=
ast modification time only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a> multiple times has no extra effect. D=
isable it again with --no-head. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -I https://exam=
ple.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--get">--get</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ascii">--trace-ascii</a>=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-H"></a><a name=3D"--header"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-H, --header &lt;header/@file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP IMAP SMTP) Extra header to include in information=
 sent. When used within an HTTP request, it is added to the regular request=
 headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For an IMAP or SMTP MIME uploaded mail built with <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</=
a> options, it is prepended to the resulting MIME document, effectively inc=
luding it at the mail global level. It does not affect raw uploaded mails. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">You may specify any number of extra headers. Note that =
if you should add a custom header that has the same name as one of the inte=
rnal ones curl would use, your externally set header is used instead of the=
 internal one. This allows you to make even trickier stuff than curl would =
normally do. You should not replace internally set headers without knowing =
perfectly well what you are doing. Remove an internal header by giving a re=
placement without content on the right side of the colon, as in: -H "Host:"=
. If you send the custom header with no-value then its header must be termi=
nated with a semicolon, such as -H "X-Custom-Header;" to send "X-Custom-Hea=
der:". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl makes sure that each header you add/replace is sen=
t with the proper end-of-line marker, you should thus <span class=3D"bold">=
not</span> add that as a part of the header content: do not add newlines or=
 carriage returns, they only mess things up for you. curl passes on the ver=
batim string you give it without any filter or other safe guards. That incl=
udes white space and control characters. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option can take an argument in @filename style, wh=
ich then adds a header for each line in the input file. Using @- makes curl=
 read the header file from stdin. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Please note that most anti-spam utilities check the pre=
sence and value of several MIME mail headers: these are "From:", "To:", "Da=
te:" and "Subject:" among others and should be added with this option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You need <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-header">--proxy-header</a> to send custom headers=
 intended for an HTTP proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Passing on a "Transfer-Encoding: chunked" header when d=
oing an HTTP request with a request body, makes curl send the data using ch=
unked encoding. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: headers set with t=
his option are set in all HTTP requests - even after redirects are followed=
, like when told with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--location">--location</a>. This can lead to the header being se=
nt to other hosts than the original host, so sensitive headers should be us=
ed with caution combined with following redirects. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">"Authorization:" and "Cookie:" headers are explicitly <=
span class=3D"emphasis">not</span> passed on in HTTP requests when followin=
g redirects to other origins, unless <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location-trusted">--location-trusted</a> is use=
d. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--header">--header</a> can be used several times in a command line=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -H "X-First-Na=
me: Joe" https://example.com
curl -H "User-Agent: yes-please/2000" https://example.com
curl -H "Host:" https://example.com
curl -H @headers.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--user-agent">--user-agent</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--referer">--referer</a> and <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-header">--=
proxy-header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-h"></a><a name=3D"--help"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-h, --help &lt;subject&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Usage help. Provide help for the subject given as an op=
tional argument. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If no argument is provided, curl displays the most impo=
rtant command line arguments. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The argument can either be a <span class=3D"bold">categ=
ory</span> or a <span class=3D"bold">command line option</span>. When a cat=
egory is provided, curl shows all command line options within the given cat=
egory. Specify category "all" to list all available options. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If "category" is specified, curl displays all available=
 help categories. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the provided subject is instead an existing command =
line option, specified either in its short form with a single dash and a si=
ngle letter, or in the long form with two dashes and a longer name, curl di=
splays a help text for that option in the terminal. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The help output is extensive for some options. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the provided command line option is not known, curl =
says so. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --help all
curl --help --insecure
curl --help -f
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--hostpubmd5"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
hostpubmd5 &lt;md5&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SFTP SCP) Pass a string containing 32 hexadecimal digi=
ts. The string should be the 128 bit <span class=3D"bold">MD5</span> checks=
um of the remote host's public key, curl refuses the connection with the ho=
st unless the checksums match. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--hostpubmd5">--hostpubmd5</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --hostpubmd5 e5=
c1c49020640a5ab0f2034854c321a8 sftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--hostpubsha256">--hostpubsha256</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--hostpubsha256"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--hostpubsha256 &lt;sha256&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SFTP SCP) Pass a string containing a Base64-encoded SH=
A256 hash of the remote host's public key. curl refuses the connection with=
 the host unless the hashes match. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This feature requires libcurl to be built with libssh2 =
and does not work with other SSH backends. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--hostpubsha256">--hostpubsha256</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --hostpubsha256=
 NDVkMTQxMGQ1ODdmMjQ3MjczYjAyOTY5MmRkMjVmNDQ=3D sftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.80.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--hostpubmd5">--hostpubmd5</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--hsts"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--hsts &=
lt;filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTPS) Enable HSTS for the transfer. If the filename p=
oints to an existing HSTS cache file, that is used. After a completed trans=
fer, the cache is saved to the filename again if it has been modified. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is told to use HTTP:// for a transfer involving=
 a hostname that exists in the HSTS cache, it upgrades the transfer to use =
HTTPS. Each HSTS cache entry has an individual lifetime after which the upg=
rade is no longer performed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify a "" filename (zero length) to avoid loading/sa=
ving and make curl just handle HSTS in memory. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You may want to restrict your umask to prevent other us=
ers on the same system to access the created file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is used several times, curl loads conten=
ts from all the files but the last one is used for saving. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--hsts">--hsts</a> can be used several times in a command line </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --hsts cache.tx=
t https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.74.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto">--proto</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--http09"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--http=
0.9</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Accept an HTTP version 0.9 response. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">HTTP/0.9 is a response without headers and therefore yo=
u can also connect with this to non-HTTP servers and still get a response s=
ince curl simply transparently downgrades - if allowed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">HTTP/0.9 is disabled by default (added in 7.66.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http09">--http0.9</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. Disable it again with --no-http0.9. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http0.9 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a> and <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-0"></a><a name=3D"--http10"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-0, --http1.0</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use HTTP version 1.0 instead of using its intern=
ally preferred HTTP version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http10">--http1.0</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http1.0 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a>, <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--ht=
tp2</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prior-knowledge</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a>. See also =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http09">-=
-http0.9</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--http11">--http1.1</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--http11"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--http=
1.1</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use HTTP version 1.1. This is the default with H=
TTP:// URLs. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http1.1 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http10">--http1.0</a>, <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--ht=
tp2</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prior-knowledge</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a>. See also =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http10">-=
-http1.0</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--http09">--http0.9</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--http2"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--http2=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use HTTP/2. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For HTTPS, this means curl negotiates HTTP/2 in the TLS=
 handshake. curl does this by default. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For HTTP, this means curl attempts to upgrade the reque=
st to HTTP/2 using the Upgrade: request header. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When curl uses HTTP/2 over HTTPS, it does not itself in=
sist on TLS 1.2 or higher even though that is required by the specification=
. A user can add this version requirement with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv12">--tlsv1.2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http2 https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a> to work, it requires that the underlying =
libcurl is built to support HTTP/2. This option is mutually exclusive with =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">-=
-http1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--http10">--http1.0</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prior-knowledge</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--h=
ttp3</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--http11">--http1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--no-alpn">--no-alpn</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-http2">--proxy-h=
ttp2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--http2-prior-knowledge"></a><span class=3D"=
nroffip">--http2-prior-knowledge</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Issue a non-TLS HTTP request using HTTP/2 direct=
ly without HTTP/1.1 Upgrade. It requires prior knowledge that the server su=
pports HTTP/2 straight away. HTTPS requests still do HTTP/2 the standard wa=
y with negotiated protocol versions in the TLS handshake. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Since 8.10.0 if this option is set for an HTTPS request=
 then the application layer protocol version (ALPN) offered to the server i=
s only HTTP/2. Prior to that both HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 were offered. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prior-knowledge</a> mul=
tiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-http2-prior-kno=
wledge. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http2-prior-k=
nowledge https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prior-knowledge</a> to work, =
it requires that the underlying libcurl is built to support HTTP/2. This op=
tion is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http10">--http1.0</a>, <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--h=
ttp3</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--http2">--http2</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--http3"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--http3=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Attempt HTTP/3 to the host in the URL, but fallb=
ack to earlier HTTP versions if the HTTP/3 connection establishment fails o=
r is slow. HTTP/3 is only available for HTTPS and not for HTTP URLs. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option allows a user to avoid using the Alt-Svc me=
thod of upgrading to HTTP/3 when you know or suspect that the target speaks=
 HTTP/3 on the given host and port. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When asked to use HTTP/3, curl issues a separate attemp=
t to use older HTTP versions with a slight delay, so if the HTTP/3 transfer=
 fails or is slow, curl still tries to proceed with an older HTTP version. =
The fallback performs the regular negotiation between HTTP/1 and HTTP/2. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--http3-only">--http3-only</a> for similar functionality <span=
 class=3D"emphasis">without</span> a fallback. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl cannot do HTTP/3 over any proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http3 https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--http3">--http3</a> to work, it requires that the underlying =
libcurl is built to support HTTP/3. This option is mutually exclusive with =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">-=
-http1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--http10">--http1.0</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prior-knowledg=
e</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-http3-only">--http3-only</a>. Added in 7.66.0. See also <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--h=
ttp2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--http3-only"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
http3-only</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Instruct curl to use HTTP/3 to the host in the U=
RL, with no fallback to earlier HTTP versions. HTTP/3 can only be used for =
HTTPS and not for HTTP URLs. For HTTP, this option triggers an error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option allows a user to avoid using the Alt-Svc me=
thod of upgrading to HTTP/3 when you know that the target speaks HTTP/3 on =
the given host and port. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option makes curl fail if a QUIC connection cannot=
 be established, it does not attempt any other HTTP versions on its own. Us=
e <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http3">=
--http3</a> for similar functionality <span class=3D"emphasis">with</span> =
a fallback. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--http3-only">--http3-only</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --http3-only ht=
tps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--http3-only">--http3-only</a> to work, it requires that the u=
nderlying libcurl is built to support HTTP/3. This option is mutually exclu=
sive with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-http11">--http1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--http10">--http1.0</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:/=
/curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2-prior-knowledge">--http2-prio=
r-knowledge</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--http3">--http3</a>. Added in 7.88.0. See also <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a>, <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http=
2</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-http3">--http3</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ignore-content-length"></a><span class=3D"=
nroffip">--ignore-content-length</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP HTTP) For HTTP, ignore the Content-Length header. =
This is particularly useful for servers running Apache 1.x, which reports i=
ncorrect Content-Length for files larger than 2 gigabytes. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For FTP, this makes curl skip the SIZE command to figur=
e out the size before downloading a file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ignore-content-length">--ignore-content-length</a> mul=
tiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ignore-content-=
length. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ignore-conten=
t-length https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ftp-skip-pasv-ip">--ftp-skip-pasv-ip</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-k"></a><a name=3D"--insecure"></a><span cla=
ss=3D"nroffip">-k, --insecure</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS SFTP SCP) By default, every secure connection curl=
 makes is verified to be secure before the transfer takes place. This optio=
n makes curl skip the verification step and proceed without checking. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When this option is not used for protocols using TLS, c=
url verifies the server's TLS certificate before it continues: that the cer=
tificate contains the right name which matches the hostname used in the URL=
 and that the certificate has been signed by a CA certificate present in th=
e cert store. See this online resource for further details: <span class=3D"=
bold"><a href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/sslcerts.html">https://curl.se/docs/s=
slcerts.html</a></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For SFTP and SCP, this option makes curl skip the <span=
 class=3D"emphasis">known_hosts</span> verification. <span class=3D"emphasi=
s">known_hosts</span> is a file normally stored in the user's home director=
y in the ".ssh" subdirectory, which contains hostnames and their public key=
s. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: using this option =
makes the transfer insecure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When curl uses secure protocols it trusts responses and=
 allows for example HSTS and Alt-Svc information to be stored and used subs=
equently. Using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--insecure">--insecure</a> can make curl trust and use such informatio=
n from malicious servers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> multiple times has no extra e=
ffect. Disable it again with --no-insecure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --insecure http=
s://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-insecure">--proxy-insecure</a>, <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--=
capath</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--interface"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--i=
nterface &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Perform the operation using a specified interface. You =
can enter interface name, IP address or hostname. If you prefer to be speci=
fic, you can use the following special syntax: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"ifname"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">if!&lt;n=
ame&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Interface name. If the provided name does not match an =
existing interface, curl returns with error 45. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"hostname"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">host!&=
lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">IP address or hostname. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"ifhostinterfacehost"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">ifhost!&lt;interface&gt;!&lt;host&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Interface name and IP address or hostname. This syntax =
requires libcurl 8.9.0 or later. </p>
<p class=3D"level2">If the provided name does not match an existing interfa=
ce, curl returns with error 45. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl does not support using network interface names for=
 this option on Windows. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">That name resolve operation if a hostname is provided d=
oes <span class=3D"bold">not</span> use DNS-over-HTTPS even if <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--doh-url">--doh-url</a=
> is set. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On Linux this option can be used to specify a <span cla=
ss=3D"bold">VRF</span> (Virtual Routing and Forwarding) device, but the bin=
ary then needs to either have the <span class=3D"bold">CAP_NET_RAW</span> c=
apability set or to be run as root. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--interface">--interface</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --interface et=
h0 https://example.com
curl --interface "host!10.0.0.1" https://example.com
curl --interface "if!enp3s0" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--dns-interface">--dns-interface</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ip-tos"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ip-t=
os &lt;string&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set Type of Service (TOS) for IPv4 or Traffic Class for=
 IPv6. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The values allowed for &lt;string&gt; can be a numeric =
value between 1 and 255 or one of the following: </p>
<p class=3D"level1">CS0, CS1, CS2, CS3, CS4, CS5, CS6, CS7, AF11, AF12, AF1=
3, AF21, AF22, AF23, AF31, AF32, AF33, AF41, AF42, AF43, EF, VOICE-ADMIT, E=
CT1, ECT0, CE, LE, LOWCOST, LOWDELAY, THROUGHPUT, RELIABILITY, MINCOST </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ip-tos">--ip-tos</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ip-tos CS5 ht=
tps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.9.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tcp-nodelay">--tcp-nodelay</a> and <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--vlan-priority=
">--vlan-priority</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ipfs-gateway"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--ipfs-gateway &lt;URL&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(IPFS) Specify which gateway to use for IPFS and IPNS U=
RLs. Not specifying this instead makes curl check if the IPFS_GATEWAY envir=
onment variable is set, or if a "~/.ipfs/gateway" file holding the gateway =
URL exists. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you run a local IPFS node, this gateway is by defaul=
t available under "<a href=3D"http://localhost:8080&quot;./">http://localho=
st:8080".</a> A full example URL would look like: </p><pre class=3D"level1"=
>curl --ipfs-gateway http://localhost:8080 \
&nbsp;  ipfs://bafybeigagd5nmnn2iys2f3
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">There are many public IPFS gateways. See for example: <=
a href=3D"https://ipfs.github.io/public-gateway-checker/">https://ipfs.gith=
ub.io/public-gateway-checker/</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you opt to go for a remote gateway you need to be aw=
are that you completely trust the gateway. This might be fine in local gate=
ways that you host yourself. With remote gateways there could potentially b=
e malicious actors returning you data that does not match the request you m=
ade, inspect or even interfere with the request. You may not notice this wh=
en using curl. A mitigation could be to go for a "trustless" gateway. This =
means you locally verify the data. Consult the docs page on trusted vs trus=
tless: <a href=3D"https://docs.ipfs.tech/reference/http/gateway/#trusted-vs=
-trustless">https://docs.ipfs.tech/reference/http/gateway/#trusted-vs-trust=
less</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ipfs-gateway">--ipfs-gateway</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ipfs-gateway =
https://example.com ipfs://
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.4.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--help">--help</a> and <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--manual">--manual</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-4"></a><a name=3D"--ipv4"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-4, --ipv4</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use IPv4 addresses only when resolving hostnames, and n=
ot for example try IPv6. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ipv4">--ipv4</a> multiple times has no extra effect. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ipv4 https://=
example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ipv6">--ipv6</a>. See als=
o <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11"=
>--http1.1</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--http2">--http2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-6"></a><a name=3D"--ipv6"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-6, --ipv6</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use IPv6 addresses only when resolving hostnames, and n=
ot for example try IPv4. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Your resolver may respond to an IPv6-only resolve reque=
st by returning IPv6 addresses that contain "mapped" IPv4 addresses for com=
patibility purposes. macOS is known to do this. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ipv6">--ipv6</a> multiple times has no extra effect. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ipv6 https://=
example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ipv4">--ipv4</a>. See als=
o <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11"=
>--http1.1</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--http2">--http2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--json"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--json &=
lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Send the specified JSON data in a POST request t=
o the HTTP server. <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--json">--json</a> works as a shortcut for passing on these three o=
ptions: </p><pre class=3D"level1">--data-binary [arg]
--header "Content-Type: application/json"
--header "Accept: application/json"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">There is <span class=3D"bold">no verification</span> th=
at the passed in data is actual JSON or that the syntax is correct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you start the data with the letter @, the rest shoul=
d be a filename to read the data from, or a single dash (-) if you want cur=
l to read the data from stdin. Posting data from a file named 'foobar' woul=
d thus be done with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--json">--json</a> @foobar and to instead read the data from stdin=
, use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--jso=
n">--json</a> @-. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is used more than once on the same comma=
nd line, the additional data pieces are concatenated to the previous before=
 sending. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The headers this option sets can be overridden with <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--header">--he=
ader</a> as usual. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--json">--json</a> can be used several times in a command line </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --json '{ "dri=
nk": "coffee" }' https://example.com
curl --json '{ "drink":' --json ' "coffee" }' https://example.com
curl --json @prepared https://example.com
curl --json @- https://example.com &lt; json.txt
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--form">--form</a>, <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>=
 and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--uplo=
ad-file">--upload-file</a>. Added in 7.82.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-binary">--data-binary</a>=
 and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data=
-raw">--data-raw</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-j"></a><a name=3D"--junk-session-cookies"><=
/a><span class=3D"nroffip">-j, --junk-session-cookies</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) When curl is told to read cookies from a given f=
ile, this option makes it discard all session cookies. This has the same ef=
fect as if a new session is started. Typical browsers discard session cooki=
es when they are closed down. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Session cookies are cookies without a set expiry time. =
They are meant to only last for "a session". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--junk-session-cookies">--junk-session-cookies</a> multi=
ple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-junk-session-cook=
ies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --junk-session-=
cookies -b cookies.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--cookie">--cookie</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cookie-jar">--cookie-jar</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--keepalive-cnt"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--keepalive-cnt &lt;integer&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the maximum number of keepalive probes TCP should s=
end but get no response before dropping the connection. This option is usua=
lly used in conjunction with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--keepalive-time">--keepalive-time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is supported on Linux, *BSD/macOS, Windows =
&gt;=3D10.0.16299, Solaris 11.4, and recent AIX, HP-UX and more. This optio=
n has no effect if <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--no-keepalive">--no-keepalive</a> is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If unspecified, the option defaults to 9. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--keepalive-cnt">--keepalive-cnt</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --keepalive-cnt=
 3 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.9.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--keepalive-time">--keepalive-time</a> an=
d <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--no-keep=
alive">--no-keepalive</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--keepalive-time"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--keepalive-time &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the time a connection needs to remain idle before s=
ending keepalive probes and the time between individual keepalive probes. I=
t is currently effective on operating systems offering the "TCP_KEEPIDLE" a=
nd "TCP_KEEPINTVL" socket options (meaning Linux, *BSD/macOS, Windows, Sola=
ris, and recent AIX, HP-UX and more). Keepalive is used by the TCP stack to=
 detect broken networks on idle connections. The number of missed keepalive=
 probes before declaring the connection down is OS dependent and is commonl=
y 8 (*BSD/macOS/AIX), 9 (Linux/AIX) or 5/10 (Windows), and this number can =
be changed by specifying the curl option "keepalive-cnt". Note that this op=
tion has no effect if <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--no-keepalive">--no-keepalive</a> is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If unspecified, the option defaults to 60 seconds. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--keepalive-time">--keepalive-time</a> is provided several time=
s, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --keepalive-tim=
e 20 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--no-keepalive">--no-keepalive</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--keepalive-cnt">--keepalive-cnt=
</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
max-time">--max-time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--key"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--key &lt=
;key&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS SCP SFTP) Private key filename. Allows you to prov=
ide your private key in this separate file. For SSH, if not specified, curl=
 tries the following candidates in order: "~/.ssh/id_rsa", "~/.ssh/id_dsa",=
 "./id_rsa", "./id_dsa". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is built against OpenSSL library, and the engin=
e pkcs11 or pkcs11 provider is available, then a PKCS#11 URI (<a href=3D"ht=
tps://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc7512.txt">RFC 7512</a>) can be used to specify a =
private key located in a PKCS#11 device. A string beginning with "pkcs11:" =
is interpreted as a PKCS#11 URI. If a PKCS#11 URI is provided, then the <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--engine">--en=
gine</a> option is set as "pkcs11" if none was provided and the <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key-type">--key-type=
</a> option is set as "ENG" or "PROV" if none was provided (depending on Op=
enSSL version). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is built against Schannel then this option is i=
gnored for TLS protocols (HTTPS, etc). That backend expects the private key=
 to be already present in the keychain or PKCS#12 file containing the certi=
ficate. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--key">--key</a> is provided several times, the last set value =
is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --cert certific=
ate --key here https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--key-type">--key-type</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert">--cert</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--key-type"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ke=
y-type &lt;type&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Private key file type. Specify which type your <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key">--key<=
/a> provided private key is. DER, PEM, and ENG are supported. If not specif=
ied, PEM is assumed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--key-type">--key-type</a> is provided several times, the last =
set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --key-type DER =
--key here https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--key">--key</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--knownhosts"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
knownhosts &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SCP SFTP) When doing SCP and SFTP transfers, curl auto=
matically checks a database containing identification for all hosts it has =
ever been used with to verify that the host it connects to is the same as p=
reviously. Host keys are stored in such a known hosts file. curl uses the ~=
/.ssh/known_hosts in the user's home directory by default. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option lets a user specify a specific file to chec=
k the host against. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The known hosts check can be disabled with <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure<=
/a>, but that makes the transfer insecure and is strongly discouraged. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--knownhosts">--knownhosts</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --knownhosts fi=
lename --key here https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.17.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--hostpubsha256">--hostpubsha256</a>, <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--hostpubmd5"=
>--hostpubmd5</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key">--key</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--krb"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--krb &lt=
;level&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP) Deprecated option (added in 8.17.0). It has no fu=
nction anymore. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Enable Kerberos authentication and use. The level must =
be entered and should be one of "clear", "safe", "confidential", or "privat=
e". Should you use a level that is not one of these, "private" is used. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--krb">--krb</a> is provided several times, the last set value =
is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --krb clear ftp=
://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--krb">--krb</a> to work, it requires that the underlying libc=
url is built to support Kerberos. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--delegation">--delegation</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl">--ssl</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--libcurl"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--lib=
curl &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Append this option to any ordinary curl command line, a=
nd you get libcurl-using C source code written to the file that does the eq=
uivalent of what your command-line operation does. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--libcurl">--libcurl</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --libcurl clien=
t.c https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--limit-rate"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
limit-rate &lt;speed&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the maximum transfer rate you want curl to use =
- for both downloads and uploads. This feature is useful if you have a limi=
ted pipe and you would like your transfer not to use your entire bandwidth.=
 To make it slower than it otherwise would be. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The given speed is measured in bytes/second, unless a s=
uffix is appended. Appending 'k' or 'K' counts the number as kilobytes, 'm'=
 or 'M' makes it megabytes, while 'g' or 'G' makes it gigabytes. The suffix=
es (k, M, G, T, P) are 1024 based. For example 1k is 1024. Examples: 200K, =
3m and 1G. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The rate limiting logic works on averaging the transfer=
 speed to no more than the set threshold over a period of multiple seconds.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you also use the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--speed-limit">--speed-limit</a> option, that =
option takes precedence and might cripple the rate-limiting slightly, to he=
lp keep the speed-limit logic working. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--limit-rate">--limit-rate</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --limit-rate 1=
00K https://example.com
curl --limit-rate 1000 https://example.com
curl --limit-rate 10M https://example.com
curl --limit-rate 200K --max-time 60 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--rate">--rate</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:/=
/curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--speed-limit">--speed-limit</a> and <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--speed-time">--speed-=
time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-l"></a><a name=3D"--list-only"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"nroffip">-l, --list-only</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP POP3 SFTP FILE) When listing an FTP directory, for=
ce a name-only view. Maybe particularly useful if the user wants to machine=
-parse the contents of an FTP directory since the normal directory view doe=
s not use a standard look or format. When used like this, the option causes=
 an NLST command to be sent to the server instead of LIST. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note: Some FTP servers list only files in their respons=
e to NLST; they do not include subdirectories and symbolic links. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When listing an SFTP directory, this switch forces a na=
me-only view, one per line. This is especially useful if the user wants to =
machine-parse the contents of an SFTP directory since the normal directory =
view provides more information than just filenames. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When retrieving a specific email from POP3, this switch=
 forces a LIST command to be performed instead of RETR. This is particularl=
y useful if the user wants to see if a specific message-id exists on the se=
rver and what size it is. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For FILE, this option has no effect yet as directories =
are always listed in this mode. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note: When combined with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a>, this option can=
 be used to send a UIDL command instead, so the user may use the email's un=
ique identifier rather than its message-id to make the request. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--list-only">--list-only</a> multiple times has no extra=
 effect. Disable it again with --no-list-only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --list-only ftp=
://example.com/dir/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--quote">--quote</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--local-port"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
local-port &lt;range&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set a preferred single number or range (FROM-TO) of loc=
al port numbers to use for the connection(s). Note that port numbers by nat=
ure are a scarce resource so setting this range to something too narrow mig=
ht cause unnecessary connection setup failures. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--local-port">--local-port</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --local-port 10=
00-3000 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--globoff">--globoff</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-L"></a><a name=3D"--location"></a><span cla=
ss=3D"nroffip">-L, --location</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) If the server reports that the requested page ha=
s moved to a different location (indicated with a Location: header and a 3X=
X response code), this option makes curl redo the request to the new place.=
 If used together with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--show-headers">--show-headers</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>, headers from all=
 requested pages are shown. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When authentication is used, or when sending a cookie w=
ith "-H Cookie:", curl only sends its credentials to the initial host. If a=
 redirect takes curl to a different host, it does not get the credentials p=
assed on. See <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--location-trusted">--location-trusted</a> on how to change this. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Limit the amount of redirects to follow by using the <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--max-redirs"=
>--max-redirs</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When curl follows a redirect and if the request is a PO=
ST, it sends the following request with a GET if the HTTP response was 301,=
 302, or 303. If the response code was any other 3xx code, curl resends the=
 following request using the same unmodified method. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You can tell curl to not change POST requests to GET af=
ter a 30x response by using the dedicated options for that: <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--post301">--post301</a>, =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--post302">=
--post302</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpag=
e.html#--post303">--post303</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The method set with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a> overrides the method =
curl would otherwise select to use. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Restrict which protocols a redirect is accepted to foll=
ow with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--p=
roto-redir">--proto-redir</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a> multiple times has no extra e=
ffect. Disable it again with --no-location. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -L https://exam=
ple.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--resolve">--resolve</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--alt-svc">--alt-svc</a>, <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a>, <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto-redir"=
>--proto-redir</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--max-redirs">--max-redirs</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--location-trusted"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--location-trusted</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Instruct curl to follow HTTP redirects like <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--l=
ocation</a>, but permit curl to send credentials and other secrets along to=
 other hosts than the initial one. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This may or may not introduce a security breach if the =
site redirects you to a site to which you send this sensitive data to. Anot=
her host means that one or more of hostname, protocol scheme or port number=
 changed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option also allows curl to pass long cookies set e=
xplicitly with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--header">--header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--location-trusted">--location-trusted</a> multiple time=
s has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-location-trusted. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --location-tru=
sted -u user:password https://example.com
curl --location-trusted -H "Cookie: session=3Dabc" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--login-options"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--login-options &lt;options&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(IMAP LDAP POP3 SMTP) Specify the login options to use =
during server authentication. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You can use login options to specify protocol specific =
options that may be used during authentication. At present only IMAP, POP3 =
and SMTP support login options. For more information about login options pl=
ease see <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2384.txt">RFC 2384</a>, <a =
href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc5092.txt">RFC 5092</a> and the IETF dra=
ft <a href=3D"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-earhart-url-smtp-=
00">https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-earhart-url-smtp-00</a> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">Since 8.2.0, IMAP supports the login option "AUTH=3D+LO=
GIN". With this option, curl uses the plain (not SASL) "LOGIN IMAP" command=
 even if the server advertises SASL authentication. Care should be taken in=
 using this option, as it sends your password over the network in plain tex=
t. This does not work if the IMAP server disables the plain "LOGIN" (e.g. t=
o prevent password snooping). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--login-options">--login-options</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --login-options=
 'AUTH=3D*' imap://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--mail-auth"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--m=
ail-auth &lt;address&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SMTP) Specify a single address. This is used to specif=
y the authentication address (identity) of a submitted message that is bein=
g relayed to another server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--mail-auth">--mail-auth</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --mail-auth use=
r@example.com -T mail smtp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--mail-rcpt">--mail-rcpt</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--mail-from">--mail-from</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--mail-from"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--m=
ail-from &lt;address&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SMTP) Specify a single address that the given mail sho=
uld get sent from. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--mail-from">--mail-from</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --mail-from use=
r@example.com -T mail smtp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--mail-rcpt">--mail-rcpt</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--mail-auth">--mail-auth</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--mail-rcpt"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--m=
ail-rcpt &lt;address&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SMTP) Specify a single email address, username or mail=
ing list name. Repeat this option several times to send to multiple recipie=
nts. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When performing an address verification (<span class=3D=
"bold">VRFY</span> command), the recipient should be specified as the usern=
ame or username and domain (as per Section 3.5 of <a href=3D"https://www.ie=
tf.org/rfc/rfc5321.txt">RFC 5321</a>). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When performing a mailing list expand (EXPN command), t=
he recipient should be specified using the mailing list name, such as "Frie=
nds" or "London-Office". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--mail-rcpt">--mail-rcpt</a> can be used several times in a comman=
d line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --mail-rcpt use=
r@example.net smtp://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--mail-rcpt-allowfails">--mail-rcpt-allowfails</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--mail-rcpt-allowfails"></a><span class=3D"n=
roffip">--mail-rcpt-allowfails</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SMTP) When sending data to multiple recipients, by def=
ault curl aborts SMTP conversation if at least one of the recipients causes=
 RCPT TO command to return an error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The default behavior can be changed by passing <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--mail-rcpt-allowfa=
ils">--mail-rcpt-allowfails</a> command-line option which makes curl ignore=
 errors and proceed with the remaining valid recipients. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If all recipients trigger RCPT TO failures and this fla=
g is specified, curl still aborts the SMTP conversation and returns the err=
or received from to the last RCPT TO command. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--mail-rcpt-allowfails">--mail-rcpt-allowfails</a> multi=
ple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-mail-rcpt-allowfa=
ils. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --mail-rcpt-all=
owfails --mail-rcpt dest@example.com smtp://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.69.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--mail-rcpt">--mail-rcpt</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-M"></a><a name=3D"--manual"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-M, --manual</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Manual. Display the huge help text. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --manual
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--libcurl">--libcurl</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--max-filesize"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--max-filesize &lt;bytes&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP HTTP MQTT) When set to a non-zero value, it specif=
ies the maximum size (in bytes) of a file to download. If the file requeste=
d is larger than this value, the transfer does not start and curl returns w=
ith exit code 63. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Setting the maximum value to zero disables the limit. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">A size modifier may be used. For example, Appending 'k'=
 or 'K' counts the number as kilobytes, 'm' or 'M' makes it megabytes, whil=
e 'g' or 'G' makes it gigabytes. Examples: 200K, 3m and 1G. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">NOTE</span>: before curl 8.4.0, wh=
en the file size is not known prior to download, for such files this option=
 has no effect even if the file transfer ends up being larger than this giv=
en limit. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting with curl 8.4.0, this option aborts the transf=
er if it reaches the threshold during transfer. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--max-filesize">--max-filesize</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --max-filesize =
100K https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--limit-rate">--limit-rate</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--max-redirs"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
max-redirs &lt;num&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Set the maximum number of redirections to follow=
. When <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--lo=
cation">--location</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a> are used, this option prevents curl f=
rom following too many redirects. By default the limit is set to 50 redirec=
ts. Set this option to -1 to make it unlimited. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--max-redirs">--max-redirs</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --max-redirs 3 =
--location https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-m"></a><a name=3D"--max-time"></a><span cla=
ss=3D"nroffip">-m, --max-time &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the maximum time in seconds that you allow each tra=
nsfer to take. Prevents your batch jobs from hanging for hours due to slow =
networks or links going down. This option accepts decimal values. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you enable retrying the transfer (<a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a>) then th=
e maximum time counter is reset each time the transfer is retried. You can =
use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry=
-max-time">--retry-max-time</a> to limit the retry time. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The decimal value needs to be provided using a dot (.) =
as decimal separator - not the local version even if it might be using anot=
her separator. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--max-time">--max-time</a> is provided several times, the last =
set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --max-time 10 =
https://example.com
curl --max-time 2.92 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--connect-timeout">--connect-timeout</a> and <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-max-time">--ret=
ry-max-time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--metalink"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--me=
talink</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option was previously used to specify a Metalink r=
esource. Metalink support is disabled in curl for security reasons (added i=
n 7.78.0). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--metalink">--metalink</a> is provided several times, the last =
set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --metalink file=
 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--mptcp"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--mptcp=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Enable the use of Multipath TCP (MPTCP) for connections=
. MPTCP is an extension to the standard TCP that allows multiple TCP stream=
s over different network paths between the same source and destination. Thi=
s can enhance bandwidth and improve reliability by using multiple paths sim=
ultaneously. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">MPTCP is beneficial in networks where multiple paths ex=
ist between clients and servers, such as mobile networks where a device may=
 switch between WiFi and cellular data or in wired networks with multiple I=
nternet Service Providers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is currently only supported on Linux starti=
ng from kernel 5.6. Only TCP connections are modified, hence this option do=
es not affect HTTP/3 (QUIC) or UDP connections. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The server curl connects to must also support MPTCP. If=
 not, the connection seamlessly falls back to TCP. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--mptcp">--mptcp</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 Disable it again with --no-mptcp. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --mptcp https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.9.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tcp-fastopen">--tcp-fastopen</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--negotiate"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--n=
egotiate</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Enable Negotiate (SPNEGO) authentication. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option requires a library built with GSS-API or SS=
PI support. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--version">--version</a> to see if your curl supports GSS-API/SSPI or =
SPNEGO. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When using this option, you must also provide a fake <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--use=
r</a> option to activate the authentication code properly. Sending a '-u :'=
 is enough as the username and password from the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a> option are not act=
ually used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--negotiate">--negotiate</a> multiple times has no extra=
 effect. Disable it again with --no-negotiate. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --negotiate -u =
: https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--basic">--basic</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--anyauth">--anyauth</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-negotiate">=
--proxy-negotiate</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-n"></a><a name=3D"--netrc"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-n, --netrc</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl scan the <span class=3D"emphasis">.netrc</spa=
n> file in the user's home directory for login name and password. This is t=
ypically used for FTP on Unix. If used with HTTP, curl enables user authent=
ication. See <span class=3D"emphasis">netrc(5)</span> and <span class=3D"em=
phasis">ftp(1)</span> for details on the file format. curl does not complai=
n if that file does not have the right permissions (it should be neither wo=
rld- nor group-readable). The environment variable "HOME" is used to find t=
he home directory. If the "NETRC" environment variable is set, that filenam=
e is used as the netrc file. (Added in 8.16.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--netrc-file">--netrc-file</a> is used, that overrides all othe=
r ways to figure out the file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The netrc file provides credentials for a hostname inde=
pendent of which protocol and port number that are used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On Windows two filenames in the home directory are chec=
ked: <span class=3D"emphasis">.netrc</span> and <span class=3D"emphasis">_n=
etrc</span>, preferring the former. Older versions on Windows checked for <=
span class=3D"emphasis">_netrc</span> only. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">A quick and simple example of how to setup a <span clas=
s=3D"emphasis">.netrc</span> to allow curl to FTP to the machine host.examp=
le.com with username 'myself' and password 'secret' could look similar to: =
</p><pre class=3D"level1">machine host.example.com
login myself
password secret
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 Disable it again with --no-netrc. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --netrc https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc-file">--netrc-file<=
/a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--n=
etrc-optional">--netrc-optional</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc-file">--netrc-file</a>, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config<=
/a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--u=
ser">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--netrc-file"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
netrc-file &lt;filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the netrc file to use. Similar to <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a>, except=
 that you also provide the path (absolute or relative). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">It abides by <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc-optional">--netrc-optional</a> if specified. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--netrc-file">--netrc-file</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --netrc-file ne=
trc https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a>. See a=
lso <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc=
">--netrc</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--user">--user</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/do=
cs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--netrc-optional"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--netrc-optional</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Similar to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.s=
e/docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a>, but this option makes the .netrc =
usage <span class=3D"bold">optional</span> and not mandatory as the <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</=
a> option does. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--netrc-optional">--netrc-optional</a> multiple times ha=
s no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-netrc-optional. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --netrc-optiona=
l https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a>. See a=
lso <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc=
-file">--netrc-file</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-"></a><a name=3D"--next"></a><span class=3D=
"nroffip">-:, --next</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use a separate operation for the following URL and asso=
ciated options. This allows you to send several URL requests, each with the=
ir own specific options, for example, such as different usernames or custom=
 requests for each. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--next">--next</a> resets all local options and only global ones h=
ave their values survive over to the operation following the <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--next">--next</a> instru=
ction. Global options include <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a>, <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ascii">--trace-ascii</=
a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fa=
il-early">--fail-early</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For example, you can do both a GET and a POST in a sing=
le command line: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl www1.example.com --next -d =
postthis www2.example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--next">--next</a> can be used several times in a command line </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl https://exampl=
e.com --next -d postthis www2.example.com
curl -I https://example.com --next https://example.net/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--no-alpn"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--no-=
alpn</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTPS) Disable the ALPN TLS extension. ALPN is enabled=
 by default if libcurl was built with an SSL library that supports ALPN. AL=
PN is used by a libcurl that supports HTTP/2 to negotiate HTTP/2 support wi=
th the server during https sessions. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that this is the negated option name documented. Y=
ou can use <span class=3D"emphasis">--alpn</span> to enable ALPN. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-alpn">--no-alpn</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --alpn. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-alpn https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--no-alpn">--no-alpn</a> to work, it requires that the underly=
ing libcurl is built to support TLS. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--no-npn">--no-npn</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-N"></a><a name=3D"--no-buffer"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"nroffip">-N, --no-buffer</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Disable the buffering of the output stream. In normal w=
ork situations, curl uses a standard buffered output stream that has the ef=
fect that it outputs the data in chunks, not necessarily exactly when the d=
ata arrives. Using this option disables that buffering. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that this is the negated option name documented. Y=
ou can use <span class=3D"emphasis">--buffer</span> to enable buffering aga=
in. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-buffer">--no-buffer</a> multiple times has no extra=
 effect. Disable it again with --buffer. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-buffer htt=
ps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--progress-bar">--progress-bar</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--no-clobber"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
no-clobber</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When used in conjunction with the <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a>, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-header-nam=
e">--remote-header-name</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a>, or <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name-all">--remote-nam=
e-all</a> options, curl avoids overwriting files that already exist. Instea=
d, a dot and a number gets appended to the name of the file that would be c=
reated, up to filename.100 after which it does not create any file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that this is the negated option name documented. Y=
ou can thus use <span class=3D"emphasis">--clobber</span> to enforce the cl=
obbering, even if <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpag=
e.html#--remote-header-name">--remote-header-name</a> is specified. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--continue-at">--continue-at</a> option cannot be used togethe=
r with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--no=
-clobber">--no-clobber</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-clobber">--no-clobber</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --clobber. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-clobber --=
output local/dir/file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.83.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-=
name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--no-keepalive"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--no-keepalive</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Disable the use of keepalive messages on the TCP connec=
tion. curl otherwise enables them by default. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that this is the negated option name documented. Y=
ou can thus use <span class=3D"emphasis">--keepalive</span> to enforce keep=
alive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-keepalive">--no-keepalive</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --keepalive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-keepalive =
https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--keepalive-time">--keepalive-time</a> and <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--keepalive-cnt">--keepal=
ive-cnt</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--no-npn"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--no-n=
pn</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTPS) curl never uses NPN, this option has no effect =
(added in 7.86.0). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Disable the NPN TLS extension. NPN is enabled by defaul=
t if libcurl was built with an SSL library that supports NPN. NPN is used b=
y a libcurl that supports HTTP/2 to negotiate HTTP/2 support with the serve=
r during https sessions. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-npn">--no-npn</a> multiple times has no extra effec=
t. Disable it again with --npn. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-npn https:=
//example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--no-npn">--no-npn</a> to work, it requires that the underlyin=
g libcurl is built to support TLS. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--no-alpn">--no-alpn</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--no-progress-meter"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--no-progress-meter</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Option to switch off the progress meter output without =
muting or otherwise affecting warning and informational messages like <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--silent">--sile=
nt</a> does. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that this is the negated option name documented. Y=
ou can thus use <span class=3D"emphasis">--progress-meter</span> to enable =
the progress meter again. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-progress-meter">--no-progress-meter</a> multiple ti=
mes has no extra effect. Disable it again with --progress-meter. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-progress-m=
eter -o store https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.67.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--silent">--silent</a>=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--no-sessionid"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--no-sessionid</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Disable curl's use of SSL session-ID caching. By =
default all transfers are done using the cache. Note that while nothing sho=
uld ever get hurt by attempting to reuse SSL session-IDs, there seem to be =
broken SSL implementations in the wild that may require you to disable this=
 in order for you to succeed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that this is the negated option name documented. Y=
ou can thus use <span class=3D"emphasis">--sessionid</span> to enforce sess=
ion-ID caching. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--no-sessionid">--no-sessionid</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --sessionid. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --no-sessionid =
https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--noproxy"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--nop=
roxy &lt;no-proxy-list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Comma-separated list of hosts for which not to use a pr=
oxy, if one is specified. The only wildcard is a single "*" character, whic=
h matches all hosts, and effectively disables the proxy. Each name in this =
list is matched as either a domain which contains the hostname, or the host=
name itself. For example, "local.com" would match "local.com", "local.com:8=
0", and "www.local.com", but not "www.notlocal.com". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option overrides the environment variables that di=
sable the proxy ("no_proxy" and "NO_PROXY"). If there is an environment var=
iable disabling a proxy, you can set the no proxy list to "" to override it=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">IP addresses specified to this option can be provided u=
sing CIDR notation (added in 7.86.0): an appended slash and number specifie=
s the number of network bits out of the address to use in the comparison. F=
or example "192.168.0.0/16" would match all addresses starting with "192.16=
8". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--noproxy">--noproxy</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --noproxy "www.=
example" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ntlm"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ntlm</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use NTLM authentication. The NTLM authentication=
 method was designed by Microsoft and is used by IIS web servers. It is a p=
roprietary protocol, reverse-engineered by clever people and implemented in=
 curl based on their efforts. This kind of behavior should not be endorsed,=
 you should encourage everyone who uses NTLM to switch to a public and docu=
mented authentication method instead, such as Digest. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you want to enable NTLM for your proxy authenticatio=
n, then use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--proxy-ntlm">--proxy-ntlm</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a> multiple times has no extra effect. D=
isable it again with --no-ntlm. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ntlm -u user:=
password https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a> to work, it requires that the underlying li=
bcurl is built to support TLS. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ntlm">--proxy-ntlm</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ntlm-wb"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ntl=
m-wb</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Deprecated option (added in 8.8.0). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Enabled NTLM much in the style <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a> does, but handed=
 over the authentication to a separate executable that was executed when ne=
eded. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ntlm-wb">--ntlm-wb</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ntlm-wb -u us=
er:password https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ntlm">--proxy-ntlm</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--oauth2-bearer"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--oauth2-bearer &lt;token&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(IMAP LDAP POP3 SMTP HTTP) Specify the Bearer Token for=
 OAUTH 2.0 server authentication. The Bearer Token is used in conjunction w=
ith the username which can be specified as part of the <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--url">--url</a> or <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a> opt=
ions. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The Bearer Token and username are formatted according t=
o <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6750.txt">RFC 6750</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--oauth2-bearer">--oauth2-bearer</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --oauth2-bearer=
 "mF_9.B5f-4.1JqM" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--basic">--basic</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--digest">--digest</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--out-null"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ou=
t-null</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Discard all response output of a transfer silently. Thi=
s is the more efficient and portable version of </p><pre class=3D"level1">c=
url https://host.example -o /dev/null
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">The transfer is done in full, all data is received and =
checked, but the bytes are not written anywhere. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--out-null">--out-null</a> is associated with a single URL. Use it=
 once per URL when you use several URLs in a command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl "https://exampl=
e.com" --out-null
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.16.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a>, <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-nam=
e</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--re=
mote-name-all">--remote-name-all</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-header-name">--remote-header-name</a=
>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-o"></a><a name=3D"--output"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-o, --output &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Write output to the given file instead of stdout. If yo=
u are using globbing to fetch multiple documents, you should quote the URL =
and you can use "#" followed by a number in the filename. That variable is =
then replaced with the current string for the URL being fetched. Like in: <=
/p><pre class=3D"level1">curl "http://{one,two}.example.com" -o "file_#1.tx=
t"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">or use several variables like: </p><pre class=3D"level1=
">curl "http://{site,host}.host[1-5].example" -o "#1_#2"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">You may use this option as many times as the number of =
URLs you have. For example, if you specify two URLs on the same command lin=
e, you can use it like this: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -o aa example.c=
om -o bb example.net
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">and the order of the -o options and the URLs does not m=
atter, just that the first -o is for the first URL and so on, so the above =
command line can also be written as </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl example.=
com example.net -o aa -o bb
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--create-dirs">--create-dirs</a> option to create the=
 local directories dynamically. Specifying the output as '-' (a single dash=
) passes the output to stdout. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To suppress response bodies, you can redirect output to=
 /dev/null: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl example.com -o /dev/null
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Or for Windows: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl example.=
com -o nul
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Or, even more efficient and portable, use </p><pre clas=
s=3D"level1">curl example.com --out-null
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the filename as single minus to force the outpu=
t to stdout, to override curl's internal binary output in terminal preventi=
on: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl https://example.com/jpeg -o -
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--output">--output</a> is associated with a single URL. Use it onc=
e per URL when you use several URLs in a command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -o file https:=
//example.com
curl "http://{one,two}.example.com" -o "file_#1.txt"
curl "http://{site,host}.host[1-5].example" -o "#1_#2"
curl -o file https://example.com -o file2 https://example.net
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--out-null">--out-null</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a>, <a cla=
ss=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name-all=
">--remote-name-all</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--remote-header-name">--remote-header-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--output-dir"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
output-dir &lt;dir&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the directory in which files should be stored, =
when <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remo=
te-name">--remote-name</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a> are used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The given output directory is used for all URLs and out=
put options on the command line, up until the first <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the specified target directory does not exist, the o=
peration fails unless <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--create-dirs">--create-dirs</a> is also used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--output-dir">--output-dir</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --output-dir "t=
mp" -O https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.73.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a> and <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-heade=
r-name">--remote-header-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-Z"></a><a name=3D"--parallel"></a><span cla=
ss=3D"nroffip">-Z, --parallel</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl perform all transfers in parallel as compared=
 to the regular serial manner. Parallel transfer means that curl runs up to=
 N concurrent transfers simultaneously and if there are more than N transfe=
rs to handle, it starts new ones when earlier transfers finish. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">With parallel transfers, the progress meter output is d=
ifferent from when doing serial transfers, as it then displays the transfer=
 status for multiple transfers in a single line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The maximum amount of concurrent transfers is set with =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-=
max">--parallel-max</a> and it defaults to 50. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a> multiple times has no extra e=
ffect. Disable it again with --no-parallel. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --parallel http=
s://example.com -o file1 https://example.com -o file2
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.66.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--next">--next</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>, <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-max">=
--parallel-max</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--parallel-immediate">--parallel-immediate</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--parallel-immediate"></a><span class=3D"nro=
ffip">--parallel-immediate</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When doing parallel transfers, this option instructs cu=
rl to prefer opening up more connections in parallel at once rather than wa=
iting to see if new transfers can be added as multiplexed streams on anothe=
r connection. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By default, without this option set, curl prefers to wa=
it a little and multiplex new transfers over existing connections. It keeps=
 the number of connections low at the expense of risking a slightly slower =
transfer startup. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-immediate">--parallel-immediate</a> multiple =
times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-parallel-immediate. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --parallel-imme=
diate -Z https://example.com -o file1 https://example.com -o file2
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.68.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-max">--p=
arallel-max</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--parallel-max"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--parallel-max &lt;num&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When asked to do parallel transfers, using <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel<=
/a>, this option controls the maximum amount of transfers to do simultaneou=
sly. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The default is 50. 65535 is the largest supported value=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--parallel-max">--parallel-max</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --parallel-max =
100 -Z https://example.com ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.66.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-max-host=
">--parallel-max-host</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--parallel-max-host"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--parallel-max-host &lt;num&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When asked to do parallel transfers, using <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel<=
/a>, this option controls the maximum amount of concurrent connections curl=
 is allowed to do to the same protocol + hostname + port number target. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">The limit is enforced by libcurl and queued "internally=
", which means that transfers that are waiting for an available connection =
still look like started transfers in the progress meter. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The default is 0 (unlimited). 65535 is the largest supp=
orted value. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--parallel-max-host">--parallel-max-host</a> is provided severa=
l times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --parallel-max-=
host 5 -Z https://example.com ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.16.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel-max">--p=
arallel-max</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--pass"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pass &=
lt;phrase&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS SCP SFTP) Passphrase for the private key used for =
SSH or TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--pass">--pass</a> is provided several times, the last set valu=
e is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --pass secret -=
-key file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--key">--key</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--path-as-is"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
path-as-is</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Do not handle sequences of /../ or /./ in the given URL=
 path. Normally curl squashes or merges them according to standards but wit=
h this option set you tell it not to do that. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--path-as-is">--path-as-is</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --no-path-as-is. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --path-as-is ht=
tps://example.com/../../etc/passwd
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--request-target">--request-target</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--pinnedpubkey"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--pinnedpubkey &lt;hashes&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the specified public key file (or hashes) to =
verify the peer. This can be a path to a file which contains a single publi=
c key in PEM or DER format, or any number of base64 encoded sha256 hashes p=
receded by 'sha256//' and separated by ';'. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When negotiating a TLS or SSL connection, the server se=
nds a certificate indicating its identity. A public key is extracted from t=
his certificate and if it does not exactly match the public key provided to=
 this option, curl aborts the connection before sending or receiving any da=
ta. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is independent of option <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a>. I=
f you use both options together then the peer is still verified by public k=
ey. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">PEM/DER support: </p>
<p class=3D"level1">OpenSSL and GnuTLS, wolfSSL, mbedTLS, Schannel </p>
<p class=3D"level1">sha256 support: </p>
<p class=3D"level1">OpenSSL, GnuTLS and wolfSSL, mbedTLS, Schannel </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Other SSL backends not supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--pinnedpubkey">--pinnedpubkey</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --pinnedpubkey=
 keyfile https://example.com
curl --pinnedpubkey 'sha256//ce118b51897f4452dc' https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--hostpubsha256">--hostpubsha256</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--post301"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pos=
t301</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Respect <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc7=
231.txt">RFC 7231</a>/6.4.2 and do not convert POST requests into GET reque=
sts when following a 301 redirect. The non-RFC behavior is ubiquitous in we=
b browsers, so curl does the conversion by default to maintain consistency.=
 However, a server may require a POST to remain a POST after such a redirec=
tion. This option is meaningful only when using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--post301">--post301</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-post301. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --post301 --loc=
ation -d "data" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--post302">--post302</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--post303">--post303</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</=
a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--post302"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pos=
t302</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Respect <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc7=
231.txt">RFC 7231</a>/6.4.3 and do not convert POST requests into GET reque=
sts when following a 302 redirect. The non-RFC behavior is ubiquitous in we=
b browsers, so curl does the conversion by default to maintain consistency.=
 However, a server may require a POST to remain a POST after such a redirec=
tion. This option is meaningful only when using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--post302">--post302</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-post302. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --post302 --loc=
ation -d "data" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--post301">--post301</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--post303">--post303</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</=
a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--post303"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pos=
t303</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Violate <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc7=
231.txt">RFC 7231</a>/6.4.4 and do not convert POST requests into GET reque=
sts when following 303 redirect. A server may require a POST to remain a PO=
ST after a 303 redirection. This option is meaningful only when using <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--lo=
cation</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--post303">--post303</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-post303. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --post303 --loc=
ation -d "data" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--post302">--post302</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--post301">--post301</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</=
a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--preproxy"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pr=
eproxy &lt;[protocol://]host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified SOCKS proxy before connecting to an H=
TTP or HTTPS <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.htm=
l#--proxy">--proxy</a>. In such a case curl first connects to the SOCKS pro=
xy and then connects (through SOCKS) to the HTTP or HTTPS proxy. Hence pre =
proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The pre proxy string should be specified with a protoco=
l:// prefix to specify alternative proxy protocols. Use socks4://, socks4a:=
//, socks5:// or socks5h:// to request the specific SOCKS version to be use=
d. No protocol specified makes curl default to SOCKS4. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the port number is not specified in the proxy string=
, it is assumed to be 1080. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">User and password that might be provided in the proxy s=
tring are URL decoded by curl. This allows you to pass in special character=
s such as @ by using %40 or pass in a colon with %3a. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--preproxy">--preproxy</a> is provided several times, the last =
set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --preproxy sock=
s5://proxy.example -x http://http.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-"></a><a name=3D"--progress-bar"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-#, --progress-bar</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl display transfer progress as a simple progres=
s bar instead of the standard, more informational, meter. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This progress bar draws a single line of '#' characters=
 across the screen and shows a percentage if the transfer size is known. Fo=
r transfers without a known size, there is a space ship (-=3Do=3D-) that mo=
ves back and forth but only while data is being transferred, with a set of =
flying hash sign symbols on top. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--progress-bar">--progress-bar</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-progress-bar. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -# -O https://e=
xample.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--styled-output">--styled-output</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proto"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--proto=
 &lt;protocols&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Limit what protocols to allow for transfers. Protocols =
are evaluated left to right, are comma separated, and are each a protocol n=
ame or 'all', optionally prefixed by zero or more modifiers. Available modi=
fiers are: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D""></a><span class=3D"nroffip">+</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Permit this protocol in addition to protocols already p=
ermitted (this is the default if no modifier is used). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"-"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Deny this protocol, removing it from the list of protoc=
ols already permitted. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D""></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=3D</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Permit only this protocol (ignoring the list already pe=
rmitted), though subject to later modification by subsequent entries in the=
 comma separated list. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">For example: <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto">--proto</a> -ftps uses the default protocols=
, but disables ftps </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--proto">--proto</a> -all,https,+http only enables http and https =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--proto">--proto</a> =3Dhttp,https also only enables http and http=
s </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Unknown and disabled protocols produce a warning. This =
allows scripts to safely rely on being able to disable potentially dangerou=
s protocols, without relying upon support for that protocol being built int=
o curl to avoid an error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option can be used multiple times, in which case t=
he effect is the same as concatenating the protocols into one instance of t=
he option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proto">--proto</a> is provided several times, the last set va=
lue is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proto =3Dhttp=
,https,sftp https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proto-redir">--proto-redir</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto-default">--proto-defaul=
t</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proto-default"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--proto-default &lt;protocol&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use <span class=3D"emphasis">protocol</span> for any pr=
ovided URL missing a scheme. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">An unknown or unsupported protocol causes error <span c=
lass=3D"emphasis">CURLE_UNSUPPORTED_PROTOCOL</span>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not change the default proxy protocol =
(http). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Without this option set, curl guesses protocol based on=
 the hostname, see <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--url">--url</a> for details. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proto-default">--proto-default</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proto-default=
 https ftp.example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proto">--proto</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto-redir">--proto-redir</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proto-redir"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-proto-redir &lt;protocols&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Limit what protocols to allow on redirects. Protocols d=
enied by <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
proto">--proto</a> are not overridden by this option. See <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto">--proto</a> for how=
 protocols are represented. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example, allow only HTTP and HTTPS on redirect: </p><pr=
e class=3D"level1">curl --proto-redir -all,http,https --follow http://examp=
le.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">By default curl only allows HTTP, HTTPS, FTP and FTPS o=
n redirects . Specifying <span class=3D"emphasis">all</span> or <span class=
=3D"emphasis">+all</span> enables all protocols on redirects, which is not =
good for security. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proto-redir">--proto-redir</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proto-redir =
=3Dhttp,https --follow https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proto">--proto</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-x"></a><a name=3D"--proxy"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-x, --proxy &lt;[protocol://]host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The proxy string can be specified with a protocol:// pr=
efix. No protocol specified or http:// it is treated as an HTTP proxy. Use =
socks4://, socks4a://, socks5:// or socks5h:// to request a specific SOCKS =
version to be used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Unix domain sockets are supported for socks proxy. Set =
localhost for the host part. e.g. socks5h://localhost/path/to/socket.sock <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">HTTPS proxy support works with the https:// protocol pr=
efix for OpenSSL and GnuTLS. It also works for mbedTLS, Rustls, Schannel an=
d wolfSSL (added in 7.87.0). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Unrecognized and unsupported proxy protocol schemes cau=
se an error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the port number is not specified in the proxy string=
, it is assumed to be 1080. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option overrides existing environment variables th=
at set the proxy to use. If there is an environment variable setting a prox=
y, you can set proxy to "" to override it. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">All operations that are performed over an HTTP proxy ar=
e transparently converted to HTTP. It means that certain protocol specific =
operations might not be available. This is not the case if you can tunnel t=
hrough the proxy, as one with the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxytunnel">--proxytunnel</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">User and password that might be provided in the proxy s=
tring are URL decoded by curl. This allows you to pass in special character=
s such as @ by using %40 or pass in a colon with %3a. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The proxy host can be specified the same way as the pro=
xy environment variables, including the protocol prefix (<a href=3D"http://=
)/">http://)</a> and the embedded user + password. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When a proxy is used, the active FTP mode as set with <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-port">=
--ftp-port</a>, cannot be used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Doing FTP over an HTTP proxy without <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxytunnel">--proxytunnel<=
/a> makes curl do HTTP with an FTP URL over the proxy. For such transfers, =
common FTP specific options do not work, including <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-reqd">--ssl-reqd</a> and <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-contr=
ol">--ftp-ssl-control</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> is provided several times, the last set va=
lue is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy http://=
proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-basic">--proxy-basic</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-anyauth"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--proxy-anyauth</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Automatically pick a suitable authentication method whe=
n communicating with the given HTTP proxy. This might cause an extra reques=
t/response round-trip. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-anyauth=
 --proxy-user user:passwd -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-basic">--proxy-basic</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-digest">--p=
roxy-digest</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-basic"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-proxy-basic</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use HTTP Basic authentication when communicating with t=
he given proxy. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--basic">--basic</a> for enabling HTTP Basic with a remote host. B=
asic is the default authentication method curl uses with proxies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-basic">--proxy-basic</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-basic. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-basic -=
-proxy-user user:passwd -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-anyauth">--proxy-anyauth</a> and <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-digest">=
--proxy-digest</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-ca-native"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--proxy-ca-native</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the operating system's native CA store for ce=
rtificate verification of the HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is independent of other HTTPS proxy CA cert=
ificate locations set at run time or build time. Those locations are search=
ed in addition to the native CA store. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--ca-native">--ca-native</a> but used in HTTPS proxy=
 context. Refer to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--ca-native">--ca-native</a> for TLS backend limitations. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ca-native">--proxy-ca-native</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-ca-native. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-ca-nati=
ve https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.2.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ca-native">--ca-native</a>, <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a>=
, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath"=
>--capath</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--dump-ca-embed">--dump-ca-embed</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-cacert"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--proxy-cacert &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified certificate file to verify the HTTPS =
proxy. The file may contain multiple CA certificates. The certificate(s) mu=
st be in PEM format. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This allows you to use a different trust for the proxy =
compared to the remote server connected to via the proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a> but used in HTTPS proxy conte=
xt. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-cacert">--proxy-cacert</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-cacert =
CA-file.txt -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-capath">--proxy-capath</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a>, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath<=
/a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump=
-ca-embed">--dump-ca-embed</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cu=
rl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-capath"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--proxy-capath &lt;dir&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Same as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath</a> but used in HTTPS proxy context. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified certificate directory to verify the p=
roxy. Multiple paths can be provided by separating them with colon (":") (e=
.g. "path1:path2:path3"). The certificates must be in PEM format, and if cu=
rl is built against OpenSSL, the directory must have been processed using t=
he c_rehash utility supplied with OpenSSL. Using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-capath">--proxy-capath</a> ca=
n allow OpenSSL-powered curl to make SSL-connections much more efficiently =
than using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--proxy-cacert">--proxy-cacert</a> if the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"htt=
ps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-cacert">--proxy-cacert</a> file cont=
ains many CA certificates. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is set, the default capath value is igno=
red. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-capath">--proxy-capath</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-capath =
/local/directory -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-cacert">--proxy-cacert</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath<=
/a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--d=
ump-ca-embed">--dump-ca-embed</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-cert"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
proxy-cert &lt;cert[:passwd]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified client certificate file when communic=
ating with an HTTPS proxy. The certificate must be PEM format. If the optio=
nal password is not specified, it is queried for on the terminal. Use <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key">--p=
roxy-key</a> to provide the private key. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is the equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert">--cert</a> but used in HT=
TPS proxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-cert">--proxy-cert</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-cert fi=
le -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key">--proxy-key</a> and <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-cert-type">--pro=
xy-cert-type</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-cert-type"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--proxy-cert-type &lt;type&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set type of the provided client certificate when using =
HTTPS proxy. PEM, DER, ENG, PROV and P12 are recognized types. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The default type depends on the TLS backend and is usua=
lly PEM. For Schannel it is P12. If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-cert">--proxy-cert</a> is a pkcs11: URI th=
en ENG or PROV is the default type (depending on OpenSSL version). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--cert-type">--cert-type</a> but used in HTTPS proxy=
 context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-cert-type">--proxy-cert-type</a> is provided several ti=
mes, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-cert-ty=
pe PEM --proxy-cert file -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-cert">--proxy-cert</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key">--proxy-key</a>. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-ciphers"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--proxy-ciphers &lt;list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Same as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a> but used in HTTPS proxy con=
text. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify which cipher suites to use in the connection to=
 your HTTPS proxy when it negotiates TLS 1.2 (1.1, 1.0). The list of cipher=
s suites must specify valid ciphers. Read up on cipher suite details on thi=
s URL: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html">https=
://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-ciphers">--proxy-ciphers</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-ciphers=
 ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256 -x https://proxy=
.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tls13-ciphers">--proxy-tls13-ciphers</a>, <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphe=
rs</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-crlfile"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--proxy-crlfile &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Provide filename for a PEM formatted file with a Certif=
icate Revocation List that specifies peer certificates that are considered =
revoked when communicating with an HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--crlfile">--crlfile</a> but only used in HTTPS prox=
y context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-crlfile">--proxy-crlfile</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-crlfile=
 rejects.txt -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--crlfile">--crlfile</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-digest"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--proxy-digest</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use HTTP Digest authentication when communicating with =
the given proxy. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/man=
page.html#--digest">--digest</a> for enabling HTTP Digest with a remote hos=
t. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-digest">--proxy-digest</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-digest. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-digest =
--proxy-user user:passwd -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-anyauth">--proxy-anyauth</a> and <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-basic">-=
-proxy-basic</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-header"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--proxy-header &lt;header/@file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Extra header to include in the request when send=
ing HTTP to a proxy. You may specify any number of extra headers. This is t=
he equivalent option to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--header">--header</a> but is for proxy communication only lik=
e in CONNECT requests when you want a separate header sent to the proxy to =
what is sent to the actual remote host. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl makes sure that each header you add/replace is sen=
t with the proper end-of-line marker, you should thus <span class=3D"bold">=
not</span> add that as a part of the header content: do not add newlines or=
 carriage returns, they only mess things up for you. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Headers specified with this option are not included in =
requests that curl knows are not to be sent to a proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option can take an argument in @filename style, wh=
ich then adds a header for each line in the input file. Using @- makes curl=
 read the headers from stdin. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option can be used multiple times to add/replace/r=
emove multiple headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--proxy-header">--proxy-header</a> can be used several times in a =
command line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-header=
 "X-First-Name: Joe" -x http://proxy https://example.com
curl --proxy-header "User-Agent: surprise" -x http://proxy https://example.=
com
curl --proxy-header "Host:" -x http://proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--header">--header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-http2"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-proxy-http2</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Negotiate HTTP/2 with an HTTPS proxy. The proxy =
might still only offer HTTP/1 and then curl sticks to using that version. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">This has no effect for any other kinds of proxies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-http2">--proxy-http2</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-http2. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-http2 -=
x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--proxy-http2">--proxy-http2</a> to work, it requires that the=
 underlying libcurl is built to support HTTP/2. Added in 8.1.0. See also <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--pr=
oxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-insecure"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--proxy-insecure</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Same as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> but used in HTTPS proxy context=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Every secure connection curl makes is verified to be se=
cure before the transfer takes place. This option makes curl skip the verif=
ication step with a proxy and proceed without checking. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When this option is not used for a proxy using HTTPS, c=
url verifies the proxy's TLS certificate before it continues: that the cert=
ificate contains the right name which matches the hostname and that the cer=
tificate has been signed by a CA certificate present in the cert store. See=
 this online resource for further details: <span class=3D"bold"><a href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/sslcerts.html">https://curl.se/docs/sslcerts.html</a><=
/span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: using this option =
makes the transfer to the proxy insecure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-insecure">--proxy-insecure</a> multiple times ha=
s no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-insecure. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-insecur=
e -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-key"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--p=
roxy-key &lt;key&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the filename for your private key when using cl=
ient certificates with your HTTPS proxy. This option is the equivalent to <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--key">--key=
</a> but used in HTTPS proxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-key">--proxy-key</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-key her=
e -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key-type">--proxy-key-type</a> and <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-key-type"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--proxy-key-type &lt;type&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the private key file type your <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key">--proxy-key</a=
> provided private key uses. DER, PEM, and ENG are supported. If not specif=
ied, PEM is assumed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--key-type">--key-type</a> but used in HTTPS proxy c=
ontext. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-key-type">--proxy-key-type</a> is provided several time=
s, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-key-typ=
e DER --proxy-key here -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key">--proxy-key</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-negotiate"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--proxy-negotiate</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use HTTP Negotiate (SPNEGO) authentication when communi=
cating with the given proxy. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--negotiate">--negotiate</a> for enabling HTTP Negoti=
ate (SPNEGO) with a remote host. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-negotiate">--proxy-negotiate</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-negotia=
te --proxy-user user:passwd -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-anyauth">--proxy-anyauth</a>, <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-basic">--proxy-basic</=
a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--pr=
oxy-service-name">--proxy-service-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-ntlm"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
proxy-ntlm</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use HTTP NTLM authentication when communicating with th=
e given proxy. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--ntlm">--ntlm</a> for enabling NTLM with a remote host. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ntlm">--proxy-ntlm</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-ntlm. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-ntlm --=
proxy-user user:passwd -x http://proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-negotiate">--proxy-negotiate</a>, <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-anyauth">--proxy-a=
nyauth</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy-user">--proxy-user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-pass"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
proxy-pass &lt;phrase&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Passphrase for the private key for HTTPS proxy client c=
ertificate. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--pass">--pass</a> but used in HTTPS proxy context. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-pass">--proxy-pass</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-pass se=
cret --proxy-key here -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-key">--proxy-key</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-pinnedpubkey"></a><span class=3D"nro=
ffip">--proxy-pinnedpubkey &lt;hashes&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use the specified public key file (or hashes) to =
verify the proxy. This can be a path to a file which contains a single publ=
ic key in PEM or DER format, or any number of base64 encoded sha256 hashes =
preceded by 'sha256//' and separated by ';'. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When negotiating a TLS or SSL connection, the server se=
nds a certificate indicating its identity. A public key is extracted from t=
his certificate and if it does not exactly match the public key provided to=
 this option, curl aborts the connection before sending or receiving any da=
ta. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Before curl 8.10.0 this option did not work due to a bu=
g. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-pinnedpubkey">--proxy-pinnedpubkey</a> is provided seve=
ral times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-pinned=
pubkey keyfile https://example.com
curl --proxy-pinnedpubkey 'sha256//ce118b51897f4452dc' https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--pinnedpubkey">--pinnedpubkey</a> and <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-service-name"></a><span class=3D"nro=
ffip">--proxy-service-name &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the service name for SPNEGO when doing proxy authen=
tication. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-service-name">--proxy-service-name</a> is provided seve=
ral times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-service=
-name "shrubbery" -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--service-name">--service-name</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-negotiate"=
>--proxy-negotiate</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-ssl-allow-beast"></a><span class=3D"=
nroffip">--proxy-ssl-allow-beast</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Do not work around a security flaw in the TLS1.0 protoc=
ol known as BEAST when communicating to an HTTPS proxy. If this option is n=
ot used, the TLS layer may use workarounds known to cause interoperability =
problems with some older server implementations. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option only changes how curl does TLS 1.0 with an =
HTTPS proxy and has no effect on later TLS versions. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: this option loosen=
s the TLS security, and by using this flag you ask for exactly that. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-allow-beast">--ssl-allow-beast</a> but used in=
 HTTPS proxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ssl-allow-beast">--proxy-ssl-allow-beast</a> mul=
tiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy-ssl-allow=
-beast. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-ssl-all=
ow-beast -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ssl-allow-beast">--ssl-allow-beast</a> and <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-ssl-auto-client-cert"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">--proxy-ssl-auto-client-cert</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Same as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--ssl-auto-client-cert">--ssl-auto-client-cert</a> but use=
d in HTTPS proxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This is only supported by Schannel. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ssl-auto-client-cert">--proxy-ssl-auto-client-ce=
rt</a> multiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxy=
-ssl-auto-client-cert. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-ssl-aut=
o-client-cert -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.77.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-auto-client-cert">--ssl-auto-clien=
t-cert</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-tls13-ciphers"></a><span class=3D"nr=
offip">--proxy-tls13-ciphers &lt;list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Same as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls13-ciphers">--tls13-ciphers</a> but used in HTT=
PS proxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify which cipher suites to use in the connection to=
 your HTTPS proxy when it negotiates TLS 1.3. The list of ciphers suites mu=
st specify valid ciphers. Read up on TLS 1.3 cipher suite details on this U=
RL: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html">https=
://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is used when curl is built to use OpenSSL 1=
.1.1 or later, Schannel, wolfSSL, or mbedTLS 3.6.0 or later. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Before curl 8.10.0 with mbedTLS or wolfSSL, TLS 1.3 cip=
her suites were set by using the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ciphers">--proxy-ciphers</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-tls13-ciphers">--proxy-tls13-ciphers</a> is provided se=
veral times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-tls13-c=
iphers TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ciphers">--proxy-ciphers</a>, <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls13-ciphers">--tls13-ciphe=
rs</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-tlsauthtype"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--proxy-tlsauthtype &lt;type&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set TLS authentication type with HTTPS proxy. The only =
supported option is "SRP", for TLS-SRP (<a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc=
/rfc5054.txt">RFC 5054</a>). This option works only if the underlying libcu=
rl is built with TLS-SRP support. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsauthtype">--tlsauthtype</a> but used in HTTPS p=
roxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-tlsauthtype">--proxy-tlsauthtype</a> is provided severa=
l times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-tlsauth=
type SRP -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tlsuser">--proxy-tlsuser</a> and <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tlspassw=
ord">--proxy-tlspassword</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-tlspassword"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--proxy-tlspassword &lt;string&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set password to use with the TLS authentication method =
specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy-tlsauthtype">--proxy-tlsauthtype</a> when using HTTPS proxy. Re=
quires that <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--proxy-tlsuser">--proxy-tlsuser</a> is set. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not work with TLS 1.3. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlspassword">--tlspassword</a> but used in HTTPS p=
roxy context. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-tlspassword">--proxy-tlspassword</a> is provided severa=
l times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-tlspass=
word passwd -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tlsuser">--proxy-tlsuser</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-tlsuser"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--proxy-tlsuser &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set username for use for HTTPS proxy with the TLS authe=
ntication method specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tlsauthtype">--proxy-tlsauthtype</a>. Requires=
 that <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--pro=
xy-tlspassword">--proxy-tlspassword</a> also is set. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not work with TLS 1.3. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-tlsuser">--proxy-tlsuser</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-tlsuser=
 smith -x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tlspassword">--proxy-tlspassword</a=
>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy-tlsv1"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-proxy-tlsv1</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use at least TLS version 1.x when negotiating with an H=
TTPS proxy. That means TLS version 1.0 or higher </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Equivalent to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv1">--tlsv1</a> but for an HTTPS proxy context.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tlsv1">--proxy-tlsv1</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-tlsv1 -=
x https://proxy.example https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-U"></a><a name=3D"--proxy-user"></a><span c=
lass=3D"nroffip">-U, --proxy-user &lt;user:password&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the username and password to use for proxy auth=
entication. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you use a Windows SSPI-enabled curl binary and do ei=
ther Negotiate or NTLM authentication then you can tell curl to select the =
username and password from your environment by specifying a single colon wi=
th this option: "-U :". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On systems where it works, curl hides the given option =
argument from process listings. This is not enough to protect credentials f=
rom possibly getting seen by other users on the same system as they still a=
re visible for a moment before being cleared. Such sensitive data should be=
 retrieved from a file instead or similar and never used in clear text in a=
 command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy-user">--proxy-user</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy-user sm=
ith:secret -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-pass">--proxy-pass</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--proxy10"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pro=
xy1.0 &lt;host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified HTTP 1.0 proxy. If the port number is=
 not specified, it is assumed at port 1080. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The only difference between this and the HTTP proxy opt=
ion <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy=
">--proxy</a>, is that attempts to use CONNECT through the proxy specifies =
an HTTP 1.0 protocol instead of the default HTTP 1.1. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxy10">--proxy1.0</a> multiple times has no extra ef=
fect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxy1.0 http=
://proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a> and <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--preproxy">--preproxy</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-p"></a><a name=3D"--proxytunnel"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-p, --proxytunnel</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When an HTTP proxy is used <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, this option mak=
es curl tunnel the traffic through the proxy. The tunnel approach is made w=
ith the HTTP proxy CONNECT request and requires that the proxy allows direc=
t connection to the remote port number curl wants to tunnel through to. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">To suppress proxy CONNECT response headers when curl is=
 set to output headers use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/do=
cs/manpage.html#--suppress-connect-headers">--suppress-connect-headers</a>.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--proxytunnel">--proxytunnel</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-proxytunnel. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --proxytunnel -=
x http://proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--pubkey"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--pubk=
ey &lt;key&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SFTP SCP) Public key filename. Allows you to provide y=
our public key in this separate file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl attempts to automatically extract the public key f=
rom the private key file, so passing this option is generally not required.=
 Note that this public key extraction requires libcurl to be linked against=
 a copy of libssh2 1.2.8 or higher that is itself linked against OpenSSL. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--pubkey">--pubkey</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --pubkey file.p=
ub sftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--pass">--pass</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-Q"></a><a name=3D"--quote"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-Q, --quote &lt;command&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP SFTP) Send an arbitrary command to the remote FTP =
or SFTP server. Quote commands are sent BEFORE the transfer takes place (ju=
st after the initial <span class=3D"bold">PWD</span> command in an FTP tran=
sfer, to be exact). To make commands take place after a successful transfer=
, prefix them with a dash '-'. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP only) To make commands be sent after curl has chan=
ged the working directory, just before the file transfer command(s), prefix=
 the command with a '+'. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You may specify any number of commands. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By default curl stops at first failure. To make curl co=
ntinue even if the command fails, prefix the command with an asterisk (*). =
Otherwise, if the server returns failure for one of the commands, the entir=
e operation is aborted. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You must send syntactically correct FTP commands as <a =
href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc959.txt">RFC 959</a> defines to FTP ser=
vers, or one of the commands listed below to SFTP servers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">SFTP is a binary protocol. Unlike for FTP, curl interpr=
ets SFTP quote commands itself before sending them to the server. Filenames=
 must be provided within double quotes to embed spaces, backslashes, quotes=
 or double quotes. Within double quotes the following escape sequences are =
available for that purpose: \ \", and \'. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Following is the list of all supported SFTP quote comma=
nds: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"atime"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">atime dat=
e file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The atime command sets the last access time of the file=
 named by the file operand. The date expression can be all sorts of date st=
rings, see the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl=
_getdate.html">curl_getdate</a> man page for date expression details. (Adde=
d in 7.73.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"chgrp"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">chgrp gro=
up file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The chgrp command sets the group ID of the file named b=
y the file operand to the group ID specified by the group operand. The grou=
p operand is a decimal integer group ID. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"chmod"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">chmod mod=
e file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The chmod command modifies the file mode bits of the sp=
ecified file. The mode operand is an octal integer mode number. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"chown"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">chown use=
r file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The chown command sets the owner of the file named by t=
he file operand to the user ID specified by the user operand. The user oper=
and is a decimal integer user ID. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"ln"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ln source_fi=
le target_file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The ln and symlink commands create a symbolic link at t=
he target_file location pointing to the source_file location. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"mkdir"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">mkdir dir=
ectory_name</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The mkdir command creates the directory named by the di=
rectory_name operand. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"mtime"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">mtime dat=
e file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The mtime command sets the last modification time of th=
e file named by the file operand. The date expression can be all sorts of d=
ate strings, see the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/libcurl/=
c/curl_getdate.html">curl_getdate</a> man page for date expression details.=
 (Added in 7.73.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"pwd"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">pwd</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">The pwd command returns the absolute path name of the c=
urrent working directory. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"rename"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">rename s=
ource target</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The rename command renames the file or directory named =
by the source operand to the destination path named by the target operand. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"rm"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">rm file</spa=
n> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The rm command removes the file specified by the file o=
perand. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"rmdir"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">rmdir dir=
ectory</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The rmdir command removes the directory entry specified=
 by the directory operand, provided it is empty. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"symlink"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">symlink=
 source_file target_file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">See ln. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--quote">--quote</a> can be used several times in a command line <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --quote "DELE f=
ile" ftp://example.com/foo
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--random-file"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-random-file &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Deprecated option. This option is ignored (added in 7.8=
4.0). Prior to that it only had an effect on curl if built to use old versi=
ons of OpenSSL. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the path name to file containing random data. T=
he data may be used to seed the random engine for SSL connections. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--random-file">--random-file</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --random-file r=
ubbish https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--egd-file">--egd-file</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-r"></a><a name=3D"--range"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-r, --range &lt;range&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP FTP SFTP FILE) Retrieve a byte range (i.e. a part=
ial document) from an HTTP/1.1, FTP or SFTP server or a local FILE. Ranges =
can be specified in a number of ways. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"0-499"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">0-499</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">specifies the first 500 bytes </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"500-999"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">500-999=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">specifies the second 500 bytes </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"-500"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-500</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">specifies the last 500 bytes </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"9500-"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">9500-</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">specifies the bytes from offset 9500 and forward </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"0-0"></a><a name=3D"-1"></a><span class=3D"n=
roffip">0-0,-1</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">specifies the first and last byte only(*)(HTTP) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"100-199"></a><a name=3D"500-599"></a><span c=
lass=3D"nroffip">100-199,500-599</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">specifies two separate 100-byte ranges(*) (HTTP) </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(*) =3D NOTE that if specifying multiple ranges and the=
 server supports it then it replies with a multiple part response that curl=
 returns as-is. It contains meta information in addition to the requested b=
ytes. Parsing or otherwise transforming this response is the responsibility=
 of the caller. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Only digit characters (0-9) are valid in the 'start' an=
d 'stop' fields of the 'start-stop' range syntax. If a non-digit character =
is given in the range, the server's response is unspecified, depending on t=
he server's configuration. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Many HTTP/1.1 servers do not have this feature enabled,=
 so that when you attempt to get a range, curl instead gets the whole docum=
ent. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">FTP and SFTP range downloads only support the simple 's=
tart-stop' syntax (optionally with one of the numbers omitted). FTP use dep=
ends on the extended FTP command SIZE. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When using this option for HTTP uploads using POST or P=
UT, functionality is not guaranteed. The HTTP protocol has no standard inte=
roperable resume upload and curl uses a set of headers for this purpose tha=
t once proved working for some servers and have been left for those who fin=
d that useful. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This command line option is mutually exclusive with <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--continue-at"=
>--continue-at</a>: you can only use one of them for a single transfer. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--range">--range</a> is provided several times, the last set va=
lue is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --range 22-44 h=
ttps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--continue-at">--continue-at</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--append">--append</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--rate"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--rate &=
lt;max request rate&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the maximum transfer frequency you allow curl t=
o use - in number of transfer starts per time unit (sometimes called reques=
t rate). Without this option, curl starts the next transfer as fast as poss=
ible. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If given several URLs and a transfer completes faster t=
han the allowed rate, curl waits until the next transfer is started to main=
tain the requested rate. This option has no effect when <a class=3D"emphasi=
s" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--parallel">--parallel</a> is =
used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The request rate is provided as "N/U" where N is an int=
eger number and U is a time unit. Supported units are 's' (second), 'm' (mi=
nute), 'h' (hour) and 'd' /(day, as in a 24 hour unit). The default time un=
it, if no "/U" is provided, is number of transfers per hour. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl is told to allow 10 requests per minute, it doe=
s not start the next request until 6 seconds have elapsed since the previou=
s transfer was started. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This function uses millisecond resolution. If the allow=
ed frequency is set more than 1000 per second, it instead runs unrestricted=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When retrying transfers, enabled with <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a>, the se=
parate retry delay logic is used and not this setting. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in version 8.10.0, you can specify the number =
of time units in the rate expression. Make curl do no more than 5 transfers=
 per 15 seconds with "5/15s" or limit it to 3 transfers per 4 hours with "3=
/4h". No spaces allowed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--rate">--rate</a> is provided several times, the last set valu=
e is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --rate 2/s htt=
ps://example.com ...
curl --rate 3/h https://example.com ...
curl --rate 14/m https://example.com ...
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.84.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--limit-rate">--limit-rate</a> and <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-delay">-=
-retry-delay</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--raw"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--raw</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) When used, it disables all internal HTTP decodin=
g of content or transfer encodings and instead makes them passed on unalter=
ed, raw. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--raw">--raw</a> multiple times has no extra effect. Dis=
able it again with --no-raw. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --raw https://e=
xample.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tr-encoding">--tr-encoding</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-e"></a><a name=3D"--referer"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">-e, --referer &lt;URL&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Set the referrer URL in the HTTP request. This c=
an also be set with the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--header">--header</a> flag of course. When used with <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--locat=
ion</a> you can append ";auto"" to the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--referer">--referer</a> URL to make curl autom=
atically set the previous URL when it follows a Location: header. The ";aut=
o" string can be used alone, even if you do not set an initial <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--referer">--referer</a=
>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--referer">--referer</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --referer "htt=
ps://fake.example" https://example.com
curl --referer "https://fake.example;auto" -L https://example.com
curl --referer ";auto" -L https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--user-agent">--user-agent</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--header">--header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-J"></a><a name=3D"--remote-header-name"></a=
><span class=3D"nroffip">-J, --remote-header-name</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Tell the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a> option to use the=
 server-specified Content-Disposition filename instead of extracting a file=
name from the URL. If the server-provided filename contains a path, that is=
 stripped off before the filename is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The file is saved in the current directory, or in the d=
irectory specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--output-dir">--output-dir</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the server specifies a filename and a file with that=
 name already exists in the destination directory, it is not overwritten an=
d an error occurs - unless you allow it by using the <span class=3D"emphasi=
s">--clobber</span> option. If the server does not specify a filename then =
this option has no effect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">There is no attempt to decode %-sequences (yet) in the =
provided filename, so this option may provide you with rather unexpected fi=
lenames. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This feature uses the name from the "filename" field, i=
t does not yet support the "filename*" field (filenames with explicit chara=
cter sets). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: Exercise judicious=
 use of this option, especially on Windows. A rogue server could send you t=
he name of a DLL or other file that could be loaded automatically by Window=
s or some third party software. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--remote-header-name">--remote-header-name</a> multiple =
times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-remote-header-name. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -OJ https://exa=
mple.com/file
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-O"></a><a name=3D"--remote-name"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-O, --remote-name</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Write output to a local file named like the remote file=
 we get. (Only the file part of the remote file is used, the path is cut of=
f.) </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The file is saved in the current working directory. If =
you want the file saved in a different directory, make sure you change the =
current working directory before invoking curl with this option or use <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output-dir">-=
-output-dir</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The remote filename to use for saving is extracted from=
 the given URL, nothing else, and if it already exists it is overwritten. I=
f you want the server to be able to choose the filename refer to <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-header-nam=
e">--remote-header-name</a> which can be used in addition to this option. I=
f the server chooses a filename and that name already exists it is not over=
written. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">There is no URL decoding done on the filename. If it ha=
s %20 or other URL encoded parts of the name, they end up as-is as filename=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You may use this option as many times as the number of =
URLs you have. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Before curl 8.10.0, curl returned an error if the URL e=
nded with a slash, which means that there is no filename part in the URL. S=
tarting in 8.10.0, curl sets the filename to the last directory part of the=
 URL or if that also is missing to "curl_response" (without extension) for =
this situation. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a> is associated with a single URL. =
Use it once per URL when you use several URLs in a command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -O https://exa=
mple.com/filename
curl -O https://example.com/filename -O https://example.com/file2
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--remote-name-all">--remote-name-all</a>, <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output-dir">--output-dir=
</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
remote-header-name">--remote-header-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--remote-name-all"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--remote-name-all</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Change the default action for all given URLs to be deal=
t with as if <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.htm=
l#--remote-name">--remote-name</a> were used for each one. If you want to d=
isable that for a specific URL after <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name-all">--remote-name-all</a> has been=
 used, you must use "-o -" or <span class=3D"emphasis">--no-remote-name</sp=
an>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name-all">--remote-name-all</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-remote-name-all. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --remote-name-a=
ll ftp://example.com/file1 ftp://example.com/file2
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-R"></a><a name=3D"--remote-time"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-R, --remote-time</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl attempt to figure out the timestamp of the re=
mote file that is getting downloaded, and if that is available make the loc=
al file get that same timestamp. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--remote-time">--remote-time</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-remote-time. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --remote-time -=
o foo https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-name</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--time-cond">--time-cond</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--remove-on-error"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--remove-on-error</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Remove the output file if an error occurs. If curl retu=
rns an error when told to save output in a local file. This prevents curl f=
rom leaving a partial file in the case of an error during transfer. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the output is not a regular file, this option has no=
 effect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--continue-at">--continue-at</a> option cannot be used togethe=
r with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--re=
move-on-error">--remove-on-error</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--remove-on-error">--remove-on-error</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-remove-on-error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --remove-on-err=
or -o output https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.83.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-X"></a><a name=3D"--request"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">-X, --request &lt;method&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Change the method to use when starting the transfer. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl passes on the verbatim string you give it in the r=
equest without any filter or other safe guards. That includes white space a=
nd control characters. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"HTTP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HTTP</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Specifies a custom request method to use when communica=
ting with the HTTP server. The specified request method is used instead of =
the method otherwise used (which defaults to <span class=3D"emphasis">GET</=
span>). Read the HTTP 1.1 specification for details and explanations. Commo=
n additional HTTP requests include <span class=3D"emphasis">PUT</span> and =
<span class=3D"emphasis">DELETE</span>, while related technologies like Web=
DAV offers <span class=3D"emphasis">PROPFIND</span>, <span class=3D"emphasi=
s">COPY</span>, <span class=3D"emphasis">MOVE</span> and more. </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Normally you do not need this option. All sorts of <spa=
n class=3D"emphasis">GET</span>, <span class=3D"emphasis">HEAD</span>, <spa=
n class=3D"emphasis">POST</span> and <span class=3D"emphasis">PUT</span> re=
quests are rather invoked by using dedicated command line options. </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This option only changes the actual word used in the HT=
TP request, it does not alter the way curl behaves. For example if you want=
 to make a proper HEAD request, using -X HEAD does not suffice. You need to=
 use the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--=
head">--head</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level2">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--location">--location</a> is used, the method string you set w=
ith <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--reque=
st">--request</a> is used for all requests, which may cause unintended side=
-effects when curl does not change request method according to the HTTP 30x=
 response codes - and similar. Consider using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a> instead in combin=
ation with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--request">--request</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"FTP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">FTP</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">Specifies a custom FTP command to use instead of <span =
class=3D"emphasis">LIST</span> when doing file lists with FTP. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"POP3"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">POP3</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Specifies a custom POP3 command to use instead of <span=
 class=3D"emphasis">LIST</span> or <span class=3D"emphasis">RETR</span>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"IMAP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">IMAP</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Specifies a custom IMAP command to use instead of <span=
 class=3D"emphasis">LIST</span>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"SMTP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SMTP</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Specifies a custom SMTP command to use instead of <span=
 class=3D"emphasis">HELP</span> or <span class=3D"bold">VRFY</span>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--request">--request</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --request "DEL=
ETE" https://example.com
curl -X NLST ftp://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--request-target">--request-target</a> and <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--follow">--follow</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--request-target"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--request-target &lt;path&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Use an alternative target (path) instead of usin=
g the path as provided in the URL. Particularly useful when wanting to issu=
e HTTP requests without leading slash or other data that does not follow th=
e regular URL pattern, like "OPTIONS *". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl passes on the verbatim string you give it in the r=
equest without any filter or other safe guards. That includes white space a=
nd control characters. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--request-target">--request-target</a> is provided several time=
s, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --request-targe=
t "*" -X OPTIONS https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--resolve"></a><a name=3D"addr"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"nroffip">--resolve &lt;[+]host:port:addr[,addr]...&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Provide a custom address for a specific host and port p=
air. Using this, you can make the curl requests(s) use a specified address =
and prevent the otherwise normally resolved address to be used. Consider it=
 a sort of /etc/hosts alternative provided on the command line. The port nu=
mber should be the number used for the specific protocol the host is used f=
or. It means you need several entries if you want to provide addresses for =
the same host but different ports. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By specifying "*" as host you can tell curl to resolve =
any host and specific port pair to the specified address. Wildcard is resol=
ved last so any <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--resolve">--resolve</a> with a specific host and port is used first. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">The provided address set by this option is used even if=
 <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ipv4">--=
ipv4</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--ipv6">--ipv6</a> is set to make curl use another IP version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By prefixing the host with a '+' you can make the entry=
 time out after curl's default timeout (1 minute). Note that this only make=
s sense for long running parallel transfers with a lot of files. In such ca=
ses, if this option is used curl tries to resolve the host as it normally w=
ould once the timeout has expired. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Provide IPv6 addresses within [brackets]. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To redirect connects from a specific hostname or any ho=
stname, independently of port number, consider the <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--connect-to">--connect-to</a> opti=
on. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Support for resolving with wildcard was added in 7.64.0=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Support for the '+' prefix was added in 7.75.0. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Support for specifying the host component as an IPv6 ad=
dress was added in 8.13.0. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--resolve">--resolve</a> can be used several times in a command li=
ne </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --resolve exam=
ple.com:443:127.0.0.1 https://example.com
curl --resolve example.com:443:[2001:db8::252f:efd6] https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--connect-to">--connect-to</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--alt-svc">--alt-svc</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--retry"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--retry=
 &lt;num&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If a transient error is returned when curl tries to per=
form a transfer, it retries this number of times before giving up. Setting =
the number to 0 makes curl do no retries (which is the default). Transient =
error means either: a timeout, an FTP 4xx response code or an HTTP 408, 429=
, 500, 502, 503, 504, 522 or 524 response code. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When curl is about to retry a transfer, it first waits =
one second and then for all forthcoming retries it doubles the waiting time=
 until it reaches 10 minutes, which then remains the set fixed delay time b=
etween the rest of the retries. By using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-delay">--retry-delay</a> you disable =
this exponential backoff algorithm. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-max-time">--retry-max-time</a> to=
 limit the total time allowed for retries. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl complies with the Retry-After: response header if =
one was present to know when to issue the next retry (added in 7.66.0). </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--retry">--retry</a> is provided several times, the last set va=
lue is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --retry 7 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--retry-max-time">--retry-max-time</a>, <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-connrefused">--retry=
-connrefused</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/man=
page.html#--retry-delay">--retry-delay</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--retry-all-errors"></a><span class=3D"nroff=
ip">--retry-all-errors</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Retry on any error. This option is used together with <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--r=
etry</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is the "sledgehammer" of retrying. Do not u=
se this option by default (for example in your <span class=3D"bold">curlrc<=
/span>), there may be unintended consequences such as sending or receiving =
duplicate data. Do not use with redirected input or output. You might be be=
tter off handling your unique problems in a shell script. Please read the e=
xample below. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: For server compati=
bility curl attempts to retry failed flaky transfers as close as possible t=
o how they were started, but this is not possible with redirected input or =
output. For example, before retrying it removes output data from a failed p=
artial transfer that was written to an output file. However this is not tru=
e of data redirected to a | pipe or &gt; file, which are not reset. We stro=
ngly suggest you do not parse or record output via redirect in combination =
with this option, since you may receive duplicate data. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By default curl does not return an error for transfers =
with an HTTP response code that indicates an HTTP error, if the transfer wa=
s successful. For example, if a server replies 404 Not Found and the reply =
is fully received then that is not an error. When <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a> is used then cu=
rl retries on some HTTP response codes that indicate transient HTTP errors,=
 but that does not include most 4xx response codes such as 404. If you want=
 to retry on all response codes that indicate HTTP errors (4xx and 5xx) the=
n combine with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--fail">--fail</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--retry-all-errors">--retry-all-errors</a> multiple time=
s has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-retry-all-errors. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --retry 5 --ret=
ry-all-errors https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.71.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--retry-connrefused"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--retry-connrefused</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In addition to the other conditions, also consider ECON=
NREFUSED as a transient error for <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a>. This option is used together w=
ith <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry=
">--retry</a>. Normally, a confused connection is not considered a transien=
t error and therefore thus not otherwise trigger a retry. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--retry-connrefused">--retry-connrefused</a> multiple ti=
mes has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-retry-connrefused. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --retry-connref=
used --retry 7 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-all-errors">--retry-all-errors</a>.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--retry-delay"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-retry-delay &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl sleep this amount of time before each retry w=
hen a transfer has failed with a transient error (it changes the default ba=
ckoff time algorithm between retries). This option is only interesting if <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--r=
etry</a> is also used. Setting this delay to zero makes curl use the defaul=
t backoff time. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By default, curl uses an exponentially increasing timeo=
ut between retries. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in curl 8.16.0, this option accepts a time as =
decimal number for parts of seconds. The decimal value needs to be provided=
 using a dot (.) as decimal separator - not the local version even if it mi=
ght be using another separator. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--retry-delay">--retry-delay</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --retry-delay 5=
 --retry 7 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-max-time">--retry-max-time</a>. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--retry-max-time"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">--retry-max-time &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The retry timer is reset before the first transfer atte=
mpt. Retries are done as usual (see <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a>) as long as the timer has not=
 reached this given limit. Notice that if the timer has not reached the lim=
it, the request is made and while performing, it may take longer than this =
given time period. To limit a single request's maximum time, use <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--max-time">--max-t=
ime</a>. Set this option to zero to not timeout retries. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in curl 8.16.0, this option accepts a time as =
decimal number for parts of seconds. The decimal value needs to be provided=
 using a dot (.) as decimal separator - not the local version even if it mi=
ght be using another separator. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--retry-max-time">--retry-max-time</a> is provided several time=
s, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --retry-max-tim=
e 30 --retry 10 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry-delay">--retry-delay</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--sasl-authzid"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--sasl-authzid &lt;identity&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(LDAP IMAP POP3 SMTP) Use this authorization identity (=
<span class=3D"bold">authzid</span>), during SASL PLAIN authentication, in =
addition to the authentication identity (<span class=3D"bold">authcid</span=
>) as specified by <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--user">--user</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the option is not specified, the server derives the =
<span class=3D"bold">authzid</span> from the <span class=3D"bold">authcid</=
span>, but if specified, and depending on the server implementation, it may=
 be used to access another user's inbox, that the user has been granted acc=
ess to, or a shared mailbox for example. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--sasl-authzid">--sasl-authzid</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --sasl-authzid =
zid imap://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.66.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--login-options">--login-options</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--sasl-ir"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--sas=
l-ir</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(LDAP IMAP POP3 SMTP) Enable initial response in SASL a=
uthentication. Such an "initial response" is a message sent by the client t=
o the server after the client selects an authentication mechanism. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--sasl-ir">--sasl-ir</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-sasl-ir. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --sasl-ir imap:=
//example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--sasl-authzid">--sasl-authzid</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--service-name"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--service-name &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the service name for SPNEGO. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--service-name">--service-name</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --service-name =
sockd/server https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--negotiate">--negotiate</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-service-name">--proxy-servi=
ce-name</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-S"></a><a name=3D"--show-error"></a><span c=
lass=3D"nroffip">-S, --show-error</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When used with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cu=
rl.se/docs/manpage.html#--silent">--silent</a>, it makes curl show an error=
 message if it fails. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--show-error">--show-error</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --no-show-error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --show-error --=
silent https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--no-progress-meter">--no-progress-meter</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-i"></a><a name=3D"--show-headers"></a><span=
 class=3D"nroffip">-i, --show-headers</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP FTP) Show response headers in the output. HTTP re=
sponse headers can include things like server name, cookies, date of the do=
cument, HTTP version and more. With non-HTTP protocols, the "headers" are o=
ther server communication. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option makes the response headers get saved in the=
 same stream/output as the data. <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump-header">--dump-header</a> exists to save heade=
rs in a separate stream. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To view the request headers, consider the <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>=
 option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Prior to 7.75.0 curl did not print the headers if <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--fail">--fail</=
a> was used in combination with this option and there was an error reported=
 by the server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option was called <span class=3D"emphasis">--inclu=
de</span> before 8.10.0. The previous name remains functional. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--show-headers">--show-headers</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-show-headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -i https://exam=
ple.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--dump-header">--dump-header</a>. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--sigalgs"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--sig=
algs &lt;list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set specific signature algorithms to use during S=
SL session establishment according to RFC 5246, 7.4.1.4.1. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">An algorithm can use either a signature algorithm and a=
 hash algorithm pair separated by a "+" (e.g. "ECDSA+SHA224"), or its TLS 1=
.3 signature scheme name (e.g. "ed25519"). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Multiple algorithms can be provided by separating them =
with ":" (e.g. "DSA+SHA256:rsa_pss_pss_sha256"). The parameter is available=
 as "-sigalgs" in the OpenSSL "s_client" and "s_server" utilities. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">"<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/man=
page.html#--sigalgs">--sigalgs</a>" allows a OpenSSL powered curl to make S=
SL-connections with exactly the signature algorithms requested by the clien=
t, avoiding nontransparent client/server negotiations. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is set, the default signature algorithm =
list built into OpenSSL are ignored. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--sigalgs">--sigalgs</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --sigalgs ecdsa=
_secp256r1_sha256 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.14.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-s"></a><a name=3D"--silent"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-s, --silent</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Silent or quiet mode. Do not show progress meter or err=
or messages. Makes curl mute. It still outputs the data you ask for, potent=
ially even to the terminal/stdout unless you redirect it. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--show-error">--show-error</a> in addition to this option to d=
isable progress meter but still show error messages. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--silent">--silent</a> multiple times has no extra effec=
t. Disable it again with --no-silent. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -s https://exam=
ple.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--stderr">--stderr</a> and <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--no-progress-meter">--no-=
progress-meter</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--skip-existing"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--skip-existing</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If there is a local file present when a download is req=
uested, the operation is skipped. Note that curl cannot know if the local f=
ile was previously downloaded fine, or if it is incomplete etc, it just kno=
ws if there is a filename present in the file system or not and it skips th=
e transfer if it is. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--skip-existing">--skip-existing</a> multiple times has =
no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-skip-existing. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --skip-existing=
 --output local/dir/file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.10.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a>, <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-nam=
e</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-no-clobber">--no-clobber</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks4"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--sock=
s4 &lt;host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified SOCKS4 proxy. If the port number is n=
ot specified, it is assumed at port 1080. Using this socket type makes curl=
 resolve the hostname and pass the address on to the proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To specify the proxy on a Unix domain socket, use local=
host for host and append the absolute path to the domain socket. For exampl=
e: "socks4://localhost/path/to/socket.sock" (the scheme may be omitted). </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option overrides any previous use of <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, as=
 they are mutually exclusive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is superfluous since you can specify a sock=
s4 proxy with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--proxy">--proxy</a> using a socks4:// protocol prefix. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--preproxy">--preproxy</a> can be used to specify a SOCKS proxy at=
 the same time proxy is used with an HTTP/HTTPS proxy. In such a case, curl=
 first connects to the SOCKS proxy and then connects (through SOCKS) to the=
 HTTP or HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--socks4">--socks4</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks4 hostna=
me:4096 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4a">--soc=
ks4a</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-socks5">--socks5</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname</a>. See also <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4a">--socks4=
a</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--so=
cks5">--socks5</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks4a"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--soc=
ks4a &lt;host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified SOCKS4a proxy. If the port number is =
not specified, it is assumed at port 1080. This asks the proxy to resolve t=
he hostname. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To specify the proxy on a Unix domain socket, use local=
host for host and append the absolute path to the domain socket. For exampl=
e: "socks4a://localhost/path/to/socket.sock" (the scheme may be omitted). <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option overrides any previous use of <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, as=
 they are mutually exclusive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is superfluous since you can specify a sock=
s4a proxy with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy">--proxy</a> using a socks4a:// protocol prefix. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--preproxy">--preproxy</a> can be used to specify a SOCKS proxy at=
 the same time <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy">--proxy</a> is used with an HTTP/HTTPS proxy. In such a case, =
curl first connects to the SOCKS proxy and then connects (through SOCKS) to=
 the HTTP or HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--socks4a">--socks4a</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks4a hostn=
ame:4096 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4">--sock=
s4</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--s=
ocks5">--socks5</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname</a>. See also <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4">--socks4</a>=
, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5"=
>--socks5</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpag=
e.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks5"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--sock=
s5 &lt;host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified SOCKS5 proxy - but resolve the hostna=
me locally. If the port number is not specified, it is assumed at port 1080=
. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To specify the proxy on a Unix domain socket, use local=
host for host and append the absolute path to the domain socket. For exampl=
e: "socks5://localhost/path/to/socket.sock" (the scheme may be omitted). </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option overrides any previous use of <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, as=
 they are mutually exclusive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is superfluous since you can specify a sock=
s5 proxy with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.ht=
ml#--proxy">--proxy</a> using a socks5:// protocol prefix. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--preproxy">--preproxy</a> can be used to specify a SOCKS proxy at=
 the same time <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy">--proxy</a> is used with an HTTP/HTTPS proxy. In such a case, =
curl first connects to the SOCKS proxy and then connects (through SOCKS) to=
 the HTTP or HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not work with FTPS or LDAP. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks5 proxy=
.example:7000 https://example.com
curl --socks5 localhost/path/unix-domain https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4">--sock=
s4</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--s=
ocks4a">--socks4a</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname</a>. See also <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-hostname">=
--socks5-hostname</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--socks4a">--socks4a</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks5-basic"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--socks5-basic</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use username/password authentication when connecting to=
 a SOCKS5 proxy. The username/password authentication is enabled by default=
. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--soc=
ks5-gssapi">--socks5-gssapi</a> to force GSS-API authentication to SOCKS5 p=
roxies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-basic">--socks5-basic</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks5-basic =
--socks5 hostname:4096 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks5-gssapi"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--socks5-gssapi</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(GSS/kerberos) Use GSS-API authentication when connecti=
ng to a SOCKS5 proxy. The GSS-API authentication is enabled by default (if =
curl is compiled with GSS-API support). Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-basic">--socks5-basic</a> to forc=
e username/password authentication to SOCKS5 proxies. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-gssapi">--socks5-gssapi</a> multiple times has =
no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-socks5-gssapi. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks5-gssapi=
 --socks5 hostname:4096 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks5-gssapi-nec"></a><span class=3D"nrof=
fip">--socks5-gssapi-nec</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(GSS/kerberos) As part of the GSS-API negotiation a pro=
tection mode is negotiated. <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc1961.txt=
">RFC 1961</a> says in section 4.3/4.4 it should be protected, but the NEC =
reference implementation does not. The option <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-gssapi-nec">--socks5-gssapi-nec=
</a> allows the unprotected exchange of the protection mode negotiation. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-gssapi-nec">--socks5-gssapi-nec</a> multiple ti=
mes has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-socks5-gssapi-nec. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks5-gssapi=
-nec --socks5 hostname:4096 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks5-gssapi-service"></a><span class=3D"=
nroffip">--socks5-gssapi-service &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set the service name for a socks server. Default is <sp=
an class=3D"bold">rcmd/server-fqdn</span>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--socks5-gssapi-service">--socks5-gssapi-service</a> is provide=
d several times, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks5-gssapi=
-service sockd --socks5 hostname:4096 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--socks5-hostname"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--socks5-hostname &lt;host[:port]&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the specified SOCKS5 proxy (and let the proxy resol=
ve the hostname). If the port number is not specified, it is assumed at por=
t 1080. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To specify the proxy on a Unix domain socket, use local=
host for host and append the absolute path to the domain socket. For exampl=
e: "socks5h://localhost/path/to/socket.sock" (the scheme may be omitted). <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option overrides any previous use of <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, as=
 they are mutually exclusive. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is superfluous since you can specify a sock=
s5 hostname proxy with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> using a socks5h:// protocol prefix. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--preproxy">--preproxy</a> can be used to specify a SOCKS proxy at=
 the same time <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--proxy">--proxy</a> is used with an HTTP/HTTPS proxy. In such a case, =
curl first connects to the SOCKS proxy and then connects (through SOCKS) to=
 the HTTP or HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname</a> is provided several ti=
mes, the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --socks5-hostna=
me proxy.example:7000 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a>, <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4">--sock=
s4</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--s=
ocks4a">--socks4a</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4a">--socks4=
a</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-Y"></a><a name=3D"--speed-limit"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-Y, --speed-limit &lt;speed&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If a transfer is slower than this set speed (in bytes p=
er second) for a given number of seconds, it gets aborted. The time period =
is set with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html=
#--speed-time">--speed-time</a> and is 30 seconds by default. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--speed-limit">--speed-limit</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --speed-limit 3=
00 --speed-time 10 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--speed-time">--speed-time</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--limit-rate">--limit-rate</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--max-time">=
--max-time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-y"></a><a name=3D"--speed-time"></a><span c=
lass=3D"nroffip">-y, --speed-time &lt;seconds&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If a transfer runs slower than speed-limit bytes per se=
cond during a speed-time period, the transfer is aborted. If speed-time is =
used, the default speed-limit is 1 unless set with <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--speed-limit">--speed-limit</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option controls transfers (in both directions) but=
 does not affect slow connects etc. If this is a concern for you, try the <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--connect-ti=
meout">--connect-timeout</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--speed-time">--speed-time</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --speed-limit 3=
00 --speed-time 10 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--speed-limit">--speed-limit</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--limit-rate">--limit-rate</a>.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ssl</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP IMAP POP3 SMTP LDAP) Warning: this is considered a=
n insecure option. Consider using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-reqd">--ssl-reqd</a> instead to be sure curl u=
pgrades to a secure connection. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Try to use SSL/TLS for the connection - often referred =
to as STARTTLS or STLS because of the involved commands. Reverts to a non-s=
ecure connection if the server does not support SSL/TLS. See also <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ftp-ssl-control">=
--ftp-ssl-control</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--ssl-reqd">--ssl-reqd</a> for different levels of encryptio=
n required. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is handled in LDAP (added in 7.81.0). It is=
 fully supported by the OpenLDAP backend and ignored by the generic ldap ba=
ckend. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Please note that a server may close the connection if t=
he negotiation does not succeed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option was formerly known as <span class=3D"emphas=
is">--ftp-ssl</span>. That option name can still be used but might be remov=
ed in a future version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ssl">--ssl</a> multiple times has no extra effect. Dis=
able it again with --no-ssl. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl pop3://ex=
ample.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ssl-reqd">--ssl-reqd</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a> and <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--cipher=
s</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl-allow-beast"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--ssl-allow-beast</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Do not work around a security flaw in the TLS1.0 =
protocol known as BEAST. If this option is not used, the TLS layer may use =
workarounds known to cause interoperability problems with some older server=
 implementations. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option only changes how curl does TLS 1.0 and has =
no effect on later TLS versions. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: this option loosen=
s the TLS security, and by using this flag you ask for exactly that. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-allow-beast">--ssl-allow-beast</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ssl-allow-beast. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl-allow-bea=
st https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ssl-allow-beast">--proxy-ssl-allow-beast</a> and =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure"=
>--insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl-auto-client-cert"></a><span class=3D"n=
roffip">--ssl-auto-client-cert</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) (Schannel) Automatically locate and use a client =
certificate for authentication, when requested by the server. Since the ser=
ver can request any certificate that supports client authentication in the =
OS certificate store it could be a privacy violation and unexpected. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-auto-client-cert">--ssl-auto-client-cert</a> multi=
ple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ssl-auto-client-c=
ert. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl-auto-clie=
nt-cert https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.77.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-ssl-auto-client-cert">--proxy-ss=
l-auto-client-cert</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl-no-revoke"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--ssl-no-revoke</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) (Schannel) Disable certificate revocation checks.=
 WARNING: this option loosens the SSL security, and by using this flag you =
ask for exactly that. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-no-revoke">--ssl-no-revoke</a> multiple times has =
no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ssl-no-revoke. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl-no-revoke=
 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--crlfile">--crlfile</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl-reqd"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--ss=
l-reqd</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP IMAP POP3 SMTP LDAP) Require SSL/TLS for the conne=
ction - often referred to as STARTTLS or STLS because of the involved comma=
nds. Terminates the connection if the transfer cannot be upgraded to use SS=
L/TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is handled in LDAP (added in 7.81.0). It is=
 fully supported by the OpenLDAP backend and rejected by the generic ldap b=
ackend if explicit TLS is required. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is unnecessary if you use a URL scheme that=
 in itself implies immediate and implicit use of TLS, like for FTPS, IMAPS,=
 POP3S, SMTPS and LDAPS. Such a transfer always fails if the TLS handshake =
does not work. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option was formerly known as <span class=3D"emphas=
is">--ftp-ssl-reqd</span>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-reqd">--ssl-reqd</a> multiple times has no extra e=
ffect. Disable it again with --no-ssl-reqd. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl-reqd ftp:=
//example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ssl">--ssl</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl-revoke-best-effort"></a><span class=3D=
"nroffip">--ssl-revoke-best-effort</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) (Schannel) Ignore certificate revocation checks w=
hen they failed due to missing/offline distribution points for the revocati=
on check lists. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-revoke-best-effort">--ssl-revoke-best-effort</a> m=
ultiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-ssl-revoke-be=
st-effort. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl-revoke-be=
st-effort https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.70.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--crlfile">--crlfile</a> and <a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--insecure">--insecure=
</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--ssl-sessions"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--ssl-sessions &lt;filename&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) **WARNING**: this option is experimental. Do not =
use in production. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the given file to load SSL session tickets into cur=
l's cache before starting any transfers. At the end of a successful curl ru=
n, the cached SSL sessions tickets are saved to the file, replacing any pre=
vious content. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The file does not have to exist, but curl reports an er=
ror if it is unable to create it. Unused loaded tickets are saved again, un=
less they get replaced or purged from the cache for space reasons. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Using a session file allows "<a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls-earlydata">--tls-earlydata</a>"=
 to send the first request in "0-RTT" mode, should an SSL session with the =
feature be found. Note that a server may not support early data. Also note =
that early data does not provide forward secrecy, e.g. is not as secure. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">The SSL session tickets are stored as base64 encoded te=
xt, each ticket on its own line. The hostnames are cryptographically salted=
 and hashed. While this prevents someone from easily seeing the hosts you c=
ontacted, they could still check if a specific hostname matches one of the =
values. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This feature requires that the underlying libcurl was b=
uilt with the experimental SSL session import/export feature (SSLS-EXPORT) =
enabled. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--ssl-sessions">--ssl-sessions</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --ssl-sessions =
sessions.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.12.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls-earlydata">--tls-earlydata</a>. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-2"></a><a name=3D"--sslv2"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-2, --sslv2</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SSL) This option previously asked curl to use SSLv2, b=
ut is now ignored (added in 7.77.0). SSLv2 is widely considered insecure (s=
ee <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6176.txt">RFC 6176</a>). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--sslv2">--sslv2</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --sslv2 https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--sslv2">--sslv2</a> to work, it requires that the underlying =
libcurl is built to support TLS. This option is mutually exclusive with <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--sslv3">--ssl=
v3</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--t=
lsv1">--tlsv1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--tlsv11">--tlsv1.1</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv12">--tlsv1.2</a>. See also <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a> and=
 <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">-=
-http2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-3"></a><a name=3D"--sslv3"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-3, --sslv3</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(SSL) This option previously asked curl to use SSLv3, b=
ut is now ignored (added in 7.77.0). SSLv3 is widely considered insecure (s=
ee <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc7568.txt">RFC 7568</a>). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--sslv3">--sslv3</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --sslv3 https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--sslv3">--sslv3</a> to work, it requires that the underlying =
libcurl is built to support TLS. This option is mutually exclusive with <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--sslv2">--ssl=
v2</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--t=
lsv1">--tlsv1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--tlsv11">--tlsv1.1</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv12">--tlsv1.2</a>. See also <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a> and=
 <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">-=
-http2</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--stderr"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--stde=
rr &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Redirect all writes to stderr to the specified file ins=
tead. If the filename is a plain '-', it is instead written to stdout. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--stderr">--stderr</a> is provided several times, the last set =
value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --stderr output=
.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--silent">--silent</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--styled-output"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--styled-output</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Enable automatic use of bold font styles when writing H=
TTP headers to the terminal. Use <span class=3D"emphasis">--no-styled-outpu=
t</span> to switch them off. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Styled output requires a terminal that supports bold fo=
nts. This feature is not present on curl for Windows due to lack of this ca=
pability. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--styled-output">--styled-output</a> multiple times has =
no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-styled-output. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --styled-output=
 -I https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--suppress-connect-headers"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">--suppress-connect-headers</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--proxytunnel">--proxytunnel</a> is used and a CONNECT reques=
t is made, do not output proxy CONNECT response headers. This option is mea=
nt to be used with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--dump-header">--dump-header</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--show-headers">--show-headers</a> which a=
re used to show protocol headers in the output. It has no effect on debug o=
ptions such as <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--verbose">--verbose</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a>, or any statistics. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--suppress-connect-headers">--suppress-connect-headers</=
a> multiple times has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-suppress-=
connect-headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --suppress-conn=
ect-headers --show-headers -x proxy https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--dump-header">--dump-header</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--show-headers">--show-headers</a>=
 and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--prox=
ytunnel">--proxytunnel</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tcp-fastopen"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--tcp-fastopen</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Enable use of TCP Fast Open (<a href=3D"https://www.iet=
f.org/rfc/rfc7413.txt">RFC 7413</a>). TCP Fast Open is a TCP extension that=
 allows data to be sent earlier over the connection (before the final hands=
hake ACK) if the client and server have been connected previously. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tcp-fastopen">--tcp-fastopen</a> multiple times has no=
 extra effect. Disable it again with --no-tcp-fastopen. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tcp-fastopen =
https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--false-start">--false-start</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tcp-nodelay"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-tcp-nodelay</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Turn on the TCP_NODELAY option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option disables the Nagle algorithm on TCP connect=
ions. The purpose of this algorithm is to minimize the number of small pack=
ets on the network (where "small packets" means TCP segments less than the =
Maximum Segment Size for the network). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Maximizing the amount of data sent per TCP segment is g=
ood because it amortizes the overhead of the send. However, in some cases s=
mall segments may need to be sent without delay. This is less efficient tha=
n sending larger amounts of data at a time, and can contribute to congestio=
n on the network if overdone. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">curl sets this option by default and you need to explic=
itly switch it off if you do not want it on. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tcp-nodelay">--tcp-nodelay</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-tcp-nodelay. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tcp-nodelay h=
ttps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--no-buffer">--no-buffer</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-t"></a><a name=3D"--telnet-option"></a><spa=
n class=3D"nroffip">-t, --telnet-option &lt;opt=3Dval&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TELNET) Pass options to the telnet protocol. Supported=
 options are: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"TTYPEterm"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">TTYPE=
=3D&lt;term&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Sets the terminal type. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"XDISPLOCX"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">XDISP=
LOC=3D&lt;X display&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Sets the X display location. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"NEWENVvar"></a><a name=3D"val"></a><span cla=
ss=3D"nroffip">NEW_ENV=3D&lt;var,val&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Sets an environment variable. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--telnet-option">--telnet-option</a> can be used several times in =
a command line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -t TTYPE=3Dvt10=
0 telnet://example.com/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tftp-blksize"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--tftp-blksize &lt;value&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TFTP) Set the TFTP <span class=3D"bold">BLKSIZE</span>=
 option (must be 512 or larger). This is the block size that curl tries to =
use when transferring data to or from a TFTP server. By default 512 bytes a=
re used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--tftp-blksize">--tftp-blksize</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tftp-blksize =
1024 tftp://example.com/file
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tftp-no-options">--tftp-no-options</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tftp-no-options"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">--tftp-no-options</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TFTP) Do not send TFTP options requests. This improves=
 interop with some legacy servers that do not acknowledge or properly imple=
ment TFTP options. When this option is used <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tftp-blksize">--tftp-blksize</a> is igno=
red. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tftp-no-options">--tftp-no-options</a> multiple times =
has no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-tftp-no-options. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tftp-no-optio=
ns tftp://192.168.0.1/
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tftp-blksize">--tftp-blksize</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-z"></a><a name=3D"--time-cond"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"nroffip">-z, --time-cond &lt;time&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP FTP) Request a file that has been modified later =
than the given time and date, or one that has been modified before that tim=
e. The date expression can be all sorts of date strings or if it does not m=
atch any internal ones, it is treated as a filename and curl tries to get t=
he modification date (mtime) from that file instead. See the <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl_getdate.html">curl_getdate</=
a> man page for date expression details. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Start the date expression with a dash (-) to make it re=
quest for a document that is older than the given date/time, default is a d=
ocument that is newer than the specified date/time. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If provided a non-existing file, curl outputs a warning=
 about that fact and proceeds to do the transfer without a time condition. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--time-cond">--time-cond</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -z "Wed 01 Sep=
 2021 12:18:00" https://example.com
curl -z "-Wed 01 Sep 2021 12:18:00" https://example.com
curl -z file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--etag-compare">--etag-compare</a> and <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-time">--remote-time<=
/a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tls-earlydata"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--tls-earlydata</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Enable the use of TLSv1.3 early data, also known =
as '0RTT' where possible. This has security implications for the requests s=
ent that way. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option can be used when curl is built to use GnuTL=
S, wolfSSL, quictls and OpenSSL as a TLS provider (but not BoringSSL, AWS-L=
C, or Rustls). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If a server supports this TLSv1.3 feature, and to what =
extent, is announced as part of the TLS "session" sent back to curl. Until =
curl has seen such a session in a previous request, early data cannot be us=
ed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When a new connection is initiated with a known TLSv1.3=
 session, and that session announced early data support, the first request =
on this connection is sent <span class=3D"emphasis">before</span> the TLS h=
andshake is complete. While the early data is also encrypted, it is not pro=
tected against replays. An attacker can send your early data to the server =
again and the server would accept it. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If your request contacts a public server and only retri=
eves a file, there may be no harm in that. If the first request orders a re=
frigerator for you, it is probably not a good idea to use early data for it=
. curl cannot deduce what the security implications of your requests actual=
ly are and make this decision for you. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The amount of early data sent can be inspected by using=
 the "<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--wri=
te-out">--write-out</a>" variable "tls_earlydata". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">WARNING</span>: this option has se=
curity implications. See above for more details. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tls-earlydata">--tls-earlydata</a> multiple times has =
no extra effect. Disable it again with --no-tls-earlydata. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tls-earlydata=
 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.11.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a>, <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls-max">--tls-max</a> a=
nd <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl-se=
ssions">--ssl-sessions</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tls-max"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--tls=
-max &lt;VERSION&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set the maximum allowed TLS version. The minimum =
acceptable version is set by tlsv1.0, tlsv1.1, tlsv1.2 or tlsv1.3. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the connection is done without TLS, this option has =
no effect. This includes QUIC-using (HTTP/3) transfers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"default"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">default=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Use up to the recommended TLS version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"10"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">1.0</span> <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level2">Use up to TLSv1.0. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"11"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">1.1</span> <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level2">Use up to TLSv1.1. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"12"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">1.2</span> <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level2">Use up to TLSv1.2. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"13"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">1.3</span> <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level2">Use up to TLSv1.3. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--tls-max">--tls-max</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tls-max 1.2 =
https://example.com
curl --tls-max 1.3 --tlsv1.2 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--tls-max">--tls-max</a> to work, it requires that the underly=
ing libcurl is built to support TLS. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv10">--tlsv1.0</a>, <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv11">--tlsv1.1</a>, <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv12">--=
tlsv1.2</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.=
html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tls13-ciphers"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--tls13-ciphers &lt;list&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set which cipher suites to use in the connection =
if it negotiates TLS 1.3. The list of ciphers suites must specify valid cip=
hers. Read up on TLS 1.3 cipher suite details on this URL: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html">https=
://curl.se/docs/ssl-ciphers.html</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is used when curl is built to use OpenSSL 1=
.1.1 or later, wolfSSL, or mbedTLS 3.6.0 or later. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Before curl 8.10.0 with mbedTLS or wolfSSL, TLS 1.3 cip=
her suites were set by using the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl=
.se/docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--tls13-ciphers">--tls13-ciphers</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tls13-ciphers=
 TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--ciphers">--ciphers</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-tls13-ciphers">--proxy-tls13-ciphe=
rs</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--curves">--curves</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlsauthtype"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-tlsauthtype &lt;type&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set TLS authentication type. Currently, the only =
supported option is "SRP", for TLS-SRP (<a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc=
/rfc5054.txt">RFC 5054</a>). If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.=
se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsuser">--tlsuser</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" hre=
f=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlspassword">--tlspassword</a> are=
 specified but <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--tlsauthtype">--tlsauthtype</a> is not, then this option defaults to "=
SRP". This option works only if the underlying libcurl is built with TLS-SR=
P support, which requires OpenSSL or GnuTLS with TLS-SRP support. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--tlsauthtype">--tlsauthtype</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlsauthtype S=
RP https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlsuser">--tlsuser</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlspassword"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-tlspassword &lt;string&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set password to use with the TLS authentication m=
ethod specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/man=
page.html#--tlsauthtype">--tlsauthtype</a>. Requires that <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsuser">--tlsuser</a> is =
set. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not work with TLS 1.3. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--tlspassword">--tlspassword</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlspassword p=
wd --tlsuser user https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlsuser">--tlsuser</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlsuser"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--tls=
user &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Set username for use with the TLS authentication =
method specified with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--tlsauthtype">--tlsauthtype</a>. Requires that <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlspassword">--tlspasswor=
d</a> also is set. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option does not work with TLS 1.3. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--tlsuser">--tlsuser</a> is provided several times, the last se=
t value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlspassword p=
wd --tlsuser user https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlspassword">--tlspassword</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-1"></a><a name=3D"--tlsv1"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-1, --tlsv1</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Use at least TLS version 1.x when negotiating wit=
h a remote TLS server. That means TLS version 1.0 or higher </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv1">--tlsv1</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlsv1 https:/=
/example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">For <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/=
manpage.html#--tlsv1">--tlsv1</a> to work, it requires that the underlying =
libcurl is built to support TLS. This option is mutually exclusive with <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv11">--tl=
sv1.1</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--tlsv12">--tlsv1.2</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http11">--http1.1</a> and <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--http2">--http2</=
a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlsv10"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--tlsv=
1.0</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Force curl to use TLS version 1.0 or later when c=
onnecting to a remote TLS server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In old versions of curl this option was documented to a=
llow _only_ TLS 1.0. That behavior was inconsistent depending on the TLS li=
brary. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--tls-max">--tls-max</a> if you want to set a maximum TLS version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv10">--tlsv1.0</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlsv1.0 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlsv11"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--tlsv=
1.1</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Force curl to use TLS version 1.1 or later when c=
onnecting to a remote TLS server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In old versions of curl this option was documented to a=
llow _only_ TLS 1.1. That behavior was inconsistent depending on the TLS li=
brary. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--tls-max">--tls-max</a> if you want to set a maximum TLS version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv11">--tlsv1.1</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlsv1.1 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls-max">--tls-max</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlsv12"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--tlsv=
1.2</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Force curl to use TLS version 1.2 or later when c=
onnecting to a remote TLS server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In old versions of curl this option was documented to a=
llow _only_ TLS 1.2. That behavior was inconsistent depending on the TLS li=
brary. Use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#=
--tls-max">--tls-max</a> if you want to set a maximum TLS version. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv12">--tlsv1.2</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlsv1.2 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls-max">--tls-max</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tlsv13"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--tlsv=
1.3</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(TLS) Force curl to use TLS version 1.3 or later when c=
onnecting to a remote TLS server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the connection is done without TLS, this option has =
no effect. This includes QUIC-using (HTTP/3) transfers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that TLS 1.3 is not supported by all TLS backends.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tlsv13">--tlsv1.3</a> multiple times has no extra effe=
ct. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tlsv1.3 https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--tlsv12">--tlsv1.2</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tls-max">--tls-max</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--tr-encoding"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-tr-encoding</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Request a compressed Transfer-Encoding response =
using one of the algorithms curl supports, and uncompress the data while re=
ceiving it. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This method was once intended to be the way to do autom=
atic data compression for HTTP but for all practical purposes using Content=
-Encoding as done with <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--compressed">--compressed</a> has superseded transfer encoding=
. The <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tr-=
encoding">--tr-encoding</a> option is therefore often not be one you want. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--tr-encoding">--tr-encoding</a> multiple times has no e=
xtra effect. Disable it again with --no-tr-encoding. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --tr-encoding h=
ttps://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--compressed">--compressed</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--trace"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--trace=
 &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Save a full trace dump of all incoming and outgoing dat=
a, including descriptive information, in the given output file. Use "-" as =
filename to have the output sent to stdout. Use "%" as filename to have the=
 output sent to stderr. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that verbose output of curl activities and network=
 traffic might contain sensitive data, including usernames, credentials or =
secret data content. Be aware and be careful when sharing trace logs with o=
thers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--trace">--trace</a> is provided several times, the last set va=
lue is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --trace log.txt=
 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> an=
d <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-a=
scii">--trace-ascii</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cur=
l.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ascii">--trace-ascii</a>, <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-config">--trace-confi=
g</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tr=
ace-ids">--trace-ids</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--trace-time">--trace-time</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--trace-ascii"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-trace-ascii &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Save a full trace dump of all incoming and outgoing dat=
a, including descriptive information, in the given output file. Use "-" as =
filename to have the output sent to stdout. Use "%" as filename to send the=
 output to stderr. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This is similar to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a>, but leaves out the hex pa=
rt and only shows the ASCII part of the dump. It makes smaller output that =
might be easier to read for untrained humans. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that verbose output of curl activities and network=
 traffic might contain sensitive data, including usernames, credentials or =
secret data content. Be aware and be careful when sharing trace logs with o=
thers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--trace-ascii">--trace-ascii</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --trace-ascii l=
og.txt https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a> and <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--=
verbose</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--trace-config"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--trace-config &lt;string&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set configuration for trace output. A comma-separated l=
ist of components where detailed output can be made available from. Names a=
re case-insensitive. Specify 'all' to enable all trace components. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">In addition to trace component names, specify "ids" and=
 "time" to avoid extra <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--trace-ids">--trace-ids</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-time">--trace-time</a> parameters.=
 </p>
<p class=3D"level1">See the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/l=
ibcurl/c/curl_global_trace.html">curl_global_trace</a> man page for more de=
tails. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--trace-config">--trace-config</a> can be used several times in a =
command line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --trace-config =
ids,http/2 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.3.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--trace-ids"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--t=
race-ids</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Prepend the transfer and connection identifiers to each=
 trace or verbose line that curl displays. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The identifiers are unique numbers assigned to each con=
nection and transfer to allow a user to better understand which transfer an=
d connection each verbose output line refers to. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ids">--trace-ids</a> multiple times has no extra=
 effect. Disable it again with --no-trace-ids. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --trace-ids --t=
race-ascii output https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.2.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a> and <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--trace-time"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--=
trace-time</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Prepend a time stamp to each trace or verbose line that=
 curl displays. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--trace-time">--trace-time</a> multiple times has no ext=
ra effect. Disable it again with --no-trace-time. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --trace-time --=
trace-ascii output https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--unix-socket"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">-=
-unix-socket &lt;path&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Connect to the server through this Unix domain s=
ocket, instead of using the network. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To connect to a proxy over Unix domain socket, see <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy">--prox=
y</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--unix-socket">--unix-socket</a> is provided several times, the=
 last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --unix-socket s=
ocket-path https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--abstract-unix-socket">--abstract-unix-socket</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-T"></a><a name=3D"--upload-file"></a><span =
class=3D"nroffip">-T, --upload-file &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Upload the specified local file to the remote URL. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If there is no file part in the specified URL, curl app=
ends the local file name to the end of the URL before the operation starts.=
 You must use a trailing slash (/) on the last directory to prove to curl t=
hat there is no filename or curl thinks that your last directory name is th=
e remote filename to use. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When putting the local filename at the end of the URL, =
curl ignores what is on the left side of any slash (/) or backslash (\\) us=
ed in the filename and only appends what is on the right side of the rightm=
ost such character. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Use the filename "-" (a single dash) to use stdin inste=
ad of a given file. Alternately, the filename "." (a single period) may be =
specified instead of "-" to use stdin in non-blocking mode to allow reading=
 server output while stdin is being uploaded. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If this option is used with an HTTP(S) URL, the PUT met=
hod is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">You can specify one <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https=
://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--upload-file">--upload-file</a> for each URL =
on the command line. Each <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--upload-file">--upload-file</a> + URL pair specifies what t=
o upload and to where. curl also supports globbing of the <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--upload-file">--upload-file=
</a> argument, meaning that you can upload multiple files to a single URL b=
y using the same URL globbing style supported in the URL. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When uploading to an SMTP server: the uploaded data is =
assumed to be <a href=3D"https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc5322.txt">RFC 5322</a>=
 formatted. It has to feature the necessary set of headers and mail body fo=
rmatted correctly by the user as curl does not transcode nor encode it furt=
her in any way. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--upload-file">--upload-file</a> is associated with a single URL. =
Use it once per URL when you use several URLs in a command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -T file https:=
//example.com
curl -T "img[1-1000].png" ftp://ftp.example.com/
curl --upload-file "{file1,file2}" https://example.com
curl -T file -T file2 https://example.com https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--get">--get</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--request">--request</a> and <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data">--data</a>. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--upload-flags"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
--upload-flags &lt;flags&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(IMAP) Specify additional behavior to apply to uploaded=
 files. Flags are specified as either a single flag value or a comma-separa=
ted list of flag values. These values are case-sensitive and may be negated=
 by prepending them with a '-' character. Currently the following flag valu=
es are accepted: answered, deleted, draft, flagged, and seen. The currently=
-accepted flag values are used to set flags on IMAP uploads. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--upload-flags">--upload-flags</a> is provided several times, t=
he last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --upload-flags =
Flagged,!Seen --upload-file local/dir/file https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.13.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--upload-file">--upload-file</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--url"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--url &lt=
;url/file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify a URL to fetch or send data to. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the given URL is missing a scheme (such as "<a href=
=3D"http://&quot;/">http://"</a> or "<a href=3D"ftp://&quot;/">ftp://"</a> =
etc) curl guesses which scheme to use based on the hostname. If the outermo=
st subdomain name matches DICT, FTP, IMAP, LDAP, POP3 or SMTP case insensit=
ively, then that protocol is used, otherwise it assumes HTTP. Scheme guessi=
ng can be avoided by providing a full URL including the scheme, or disabled=
 by setting a default protocol, see <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://c=
url.se/docs/manpage.html#--proto-default">--proto-default</a> for details. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">To control where the contents of a retrieved URL is wri=
tten instead of the default stdout, use the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--output">--output</a> or the <a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote=
-name</a> options. When retrieving multiple URLs in a single invoke, each p=
rovided URL needs its own dedicated destination option unless <a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name-all">--rem=
ote-name-all</a> is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On Windows, "file://" accesses can be converted to netw=
ork accesses by the operating system. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Starting in curl 8.13.0, curl can be told to download U=
RLs provided in a text file, one URL per line. It is done with "<a class=3D=
"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--url">--url</a> @file=
name": so instead of a URL, you specify a filename prefixed with the "@" sy=
mbol. It can be told to load the list of URLs from stdin by providing an ar=
gument like "@-". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When downloading URLs given in a file, it implies using=
 <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-n=
ame">--remote-name</a> for each provided URL. The URLs are full, there is n=
o globbing applied or done on these. Features such as <a class=3D"emphasis"=
 href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--skip-existing">--skip-existing=
</a> work fine in combination with this. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Lines in the URL file that start with "#" are treated a=
s comments and are skipped. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--url">--url</a> can be used several times in a command line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --url https://=
example.com
curl --url @file
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--next">--next</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https:/=
/curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" hr=
ef=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--path-as-is">--path-as-is</a> and =
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--disallow-=
username-in-url">--disallow-username-in-url</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--url-query"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--u=
rl-query &lt;data&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Add a piece of data, usually a name + value pair, to th=
e end of the URL query part. The syntax is identical to that used for <a cl=
ass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-urlencode=
">--data-urlencode</a> with one extension: </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the argument starts with a '+' (plus), the rest of t=
he string is provided as-is unencoded. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The query part of a URL is the one following the questi=
on mark on the right end. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--url-query">--url-query</a> can be used several times in a comman=
d line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Examples: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --url-query na=
me=3Dval https://example.com
curl --url-query =3Dencodethis http://example.net/foo
curl --url-query name@file https://example.com
curl --url-query @fileonly https://example.com
curl --url-query "+name=3D%20foo" https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 7.87.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-urlencode">--data-urlencode</a> a=
nd <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--get">-=
-get</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-B"></a><a name=3D"--use-ascii"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"nroffip">-B, --use-ascii</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(FTP LDAP TFTP) Enable ASCII transfer mode. For FTP, th=
is can also be enforced by using a URL that ends with ";type=3DA". For TFTP=
, this can also be enforced by using a URL that ends with ";mode=3Dnetascii=
". This option causes data sent to stdout to be in text mode for Win32 syst=
ems. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--use-ascii">--use-ascii</a> multiple times has no extra=
 effect. Disable it again with --no-use-ascii. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -B ftp://exampl=
e.com/README
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--crlf">--crlf</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--data-ascii">--data-ascii</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-u"></a><a name=3D"--user"></a><span class=
=3D"nroffip">-u, --user &lt;user:password&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Specify the username and password to use for server aut=
hentication. Overrides <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://=
curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--netrc-optional">--netrc-optional</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you simply specify the username, curl prompts for a =
password. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The username and passwords are split up on the first co=
lon, which makes it impossible to use a colon in the username with this opt=
ion. The password can, still. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On systems where it works, curl hides the given option =
argument from process listings. This is not enough to protect credentials f=
rom possibly getting seen by other users on the same system as they still a=
re visible for a moment before being cleared. Such sensitive data should be=
 retrieved from a file instead or similar and never used in clear text in a=
 command line. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When using Kerberos V5 with a Windows based server you =
should include the Windows domain name in the username, in order for the se=
rver to successfully obtain a Kerberos Ticket. If you do not, then the init=
ial authentication handshake may fail. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When using NTLM, the username can be specified simply a=
s the username, without the domain, if there is a single domain and forest =
in your setup for example. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To specify the domain name use either Down-Level Logon =
Name or UPN (User Principal Name) formats. For example, EXAMPLE\user and us=
er@example.com respectively. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you use a Windows SSPI-enabled curl binary and perfo=
rm Kerberos V5, Negotiate, NTLM or Digest authentication then you can tell =
curl to select the username and password from your environment by specifyin=
g a single colon with this option: "-u :". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--user">--user</a> is provided several times, the last set valu=
e is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -u user:secret =
https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--netrc">--netrc</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"ht=
tps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-A"></a><a name=3D"--user-agent"></a><span c=
lass=3D"nroffip">-A, --user-agent &lt;name&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">(HTTP) Specify the User-Agent string to send to the HTT=
P server. To encode blanks in the string, surround the string with single q=
uote marks. This header can also be set with the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--header">--header</a> or the <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-header">--=
proxy-header</a> options. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you give an empty argument to <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--user-agent">--user-agent</a> ("=
"), it removes the header completely from the request. If you prefer a blan=
k header, you can set it to a single space (" "). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">By default, curl uses curl/VERSION, such as User-Agent:=
 curl/8.18.0. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--user-agent">--user-agent</a> is provided several times, the l=
ast set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -A "Agent 007" =
https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--header">--header</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--proxy-header">--proxy-header</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--variable"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--va=
riable &lt;[%]name=3Dtext/@file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set a variable with "name=3Dcontent" or "name@file" (wh=
ere "file" can be stdin if set to a single dash ("-")). The name is a case =
sensitive identifier that must consist of no other letters than a-z, A-Z, 0=
-9 or underscore. The specified content is then associated with this identi=
fier. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Setting the same variable name again overwrites the old=
 contents with the new. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The contents of a variable can be referenced in a later=
 command line option when that option name is prefixed with "<span class=3D=
"emphasis">--expand-</span>", and the name is used as "{{name}}". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--variable">--variable</a> can import environment variables into t=
he name space. Opt to either require the environment variable to be set or =
provide a default value for the variable in case it is not already set. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--variable">--variable</a> %name imports the variable called "name=
" but exits with an error if that environment variable is not already set. =
To provide a default value if the environment variable is not set, use <a c=
lass=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--variable">--v=
ariable</a> %name=3Dcontent or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.s=
e/docs/manpage.html#--variable">--variable</a> %name@content. Note that on =
some systems - but not all - environment variables are case insensitive. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in curl 8.12.0: you can get a byte range from the=
 source by appending "[start-end]" to the variable name, where <span class=
=3D"emphasis">start</span> and <span class=3D"emphasis">end</span> are byte=
 offsets to include from the contents. For example, asking for offset "2-10=
" means offset two to offset ten, inclusive, resulting in 9 bytes in total.=
 "2-2" means a single byte at offset 2. Not providing a second number impli=
es to the end of data. The start offset cannot be larger than the end offse=
t. Asking for a range that is outside of the file size makes the variable c=
ontents empty. For example, getting the first one hundred bytes from a give=
n file: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --variable "fraction[0-99]@filename"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Given a byte range that has no data results in an empty=
 string. Asking for a range that is larger than the content makes curl use =
the piece of the data that exists. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To assign a variable using contents from another variab=
le, use <span class=3D"emphasis">--expand-variable</span>. Like for example=
 assigning a new variable using contents from two other: </p><pre class=3D"=
level1">curl --expand-variable "user=3D{{firstname}} {{lastname}}"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">When expanding variables, curl supports a set of functi=
ons that can make the variable contents more convenient to use. You apply a=
 function to a variable expansion by adding a colon and then list the desir=
ed functions in a comma-separated list that is evaluated in a left-to-right=
 order. Variable content holding null bytes that are not encoded when expan=
ded causes an error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Available functions: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"trim"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">trim</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">removes all leading and trailing white space. </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level2">curl --expand-url ht=
tps://example.com/{{var:trim}}
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"json"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">json</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">outputs the content using JSON string quoting rules. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level2">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level2">curl --expand-data {=
{data:json}} https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"url"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">shows the content URL (percent) encoded. </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level2">curl --expand-url ht=
tps://example.com/{{path:url}}
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"b64"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">b64</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">expands the variable base64 encoded </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level2">curl --expand-url ht=
tps://example.com/{{var:b64}}
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"64dec"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">64dec</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">decodes a base64 encoded character sequence. If the seq=
uence is not possible to decode, it instead outputs "[64dec-fail]" </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level2">curl --expand-url ht=
tps://example.com/{{var:64dec}}
</pre>
<p class=3D"level2">(Added in 8.13.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manp=
age.html#--variable">--variable</a> can be used several times in a command =
line </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --variable name=
=3Dsmith --expand-url "https://example.com/{{name}}"
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.3.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-v"></a><a name=3D"--verbose"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">-v, --verbose</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl output verbose information during the operati=
on. Useful for debugging and seeing what's going on under the hood. A line =
starting with &gt; means header data sent by curl, &lt; means header data r=
eceived by curl that is hidden in normal cases, and a line starting with * =
means additional info provided by curl. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you only want HTTP headers in the output, <a class=
=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--show-headers">--s=
how-headers</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--dump-header">--dump-header</a> might be more suitable options. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1">Since curl 8.10, mentioning this option several times i=
n the same argument increases the level of the trace output. However, as be=
fore, a single <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.h=
tml#--verbose">--verbose</a> or <span class=3D"emphasis">--no-verbose</span=
> reverts any additions by previous "-vv" again. This means that "-vv -v" i=
s equivalent to a single -v. This avoids unwanted verbosity when the option=
 is mentioned in the command line <span class=3D"emphasis">and</span> curl =
config files. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Using it twice, e.g. "-vv", outputs time (<a class=3D"e=
mphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-time">--trace-ti=
me</a>) and transfer ids (<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--trace-ids">--trace-ids</a>), as well as enabling tracing f=
or all protocols (<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpag=
e.html#--trace-config">--trace-config</a> protocol). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Adding a third verbose outputs transfer content (<a cla=
ss=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ascii">--=
trace-ascii</a> %) and enables tracing of more components (<a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-config">--trace-con=
fig</a> read,write,ssl). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">A fourth time adds tracing of all network components. (=
<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-con=
fig">--trace-config</a> network). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Any addition of the verbose option after that has no ef=
fect. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you think this option does not give you the right de=
tails, consider using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/ma=
npage.html#--trace">--trace</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://cu=
rl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ascii">--trace-ascii</a> instead. Or use it=
 only once and use <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpa=
ge.html#--trace-config">--trace-config</a> to trace the specific components=
 you wish to see. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Note that verbose output of curl activities and network=
 traffic might contain sensitive data, including usernames, credentials or =
secret data content. Be aware and be careful when sharing trace logs with o=
thers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When the output contains protocol headers, those lines =
might include carriage return (ASCII code 13) characters, even on platforms=
 that otherwise normally only use linefeed to signify line separations - as=
 curl shows the exact contents arriving from the server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is global and does not need to be specified=
 for each use of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage=
.html#--next">--next</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> multiple times has no extra eff=
ect. Disable it again with --no-verbose. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --verbose https=
://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">This option is mutually exclusive with <a class=3D"emph=
asis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</a> and <a=
 class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace-ascii=
">--trace-ascii</a>. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--show-headers">--show-headers</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis=
" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--silent">--silent</a>, <a clas=
s=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--trace">--trace</=
a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--tr=
ace-ascii">--trace-ascii</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-V"></a><a name=3D"--version"></a><span clas=
s=3D"nroffip">-V, --version</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Display information about curl and the libcurl version =
it uses. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The first line includes the full version of curl, libcu=
rl and other 3rd party libraries linked with the executable. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This line may contain one or more TLS libraries. curl c=
an be built to support more than one TLS library which then makes curl - at=
 start-up - select which particular backend to use for this invocation. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl supports more than one TLS library like this, t=
he ones that are <span class=3D"emphasis">not</span> selected by default ar=
e listed within parentheses. Thus, if you do not specify which backend to u=
se (with the "CURL_SSL_BACKEND" environment variable) the one listed withou=
t parentheses is used. Such builds also have "MultiSSL" set as a feature. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1">The second line (starts with "Release-Date:") shows the=
 release date. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The third line (starts with "Protocols:") shows all pro=
tocols that libcurl reports to support. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The fourth line (starts with "Features:") shows specifi=
c features libcurl reports to offer. Available features include: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"alt-svc"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">alt-svc=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Support for the Alt-Svc: header is provided. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"AsynchDNS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Async=
hDNS</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This curl uses asynchronous name resolves. Asynchronous=
 name resolves can be done using either the c-ares or the threaded resolver=
 backends. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"brotli"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">brotli</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Support for automatic brotli compression over HTTP(S). =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"CharConv"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">CharCo=
nv</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">curl was built with support for character set conversio=
ns (like EBCDIC) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"Debug"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Debug</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This curl uses a libcurl built with Debug. This enables=
 more error-tracking and memory debugging etc. For curl-developers only. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"ECH"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ECH</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">ECH support is present. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"gsasl"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">gsasl</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The built-in SASL authentication includes extensions to=
 support SCRAM because libcurl was built with libgsasl. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"GSS-API"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">GSS-API=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">GSS-API is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"HSTS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HSTS</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">HSTS support is present. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"HTTP2"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HTTP2</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">HTTP/2 support has been built-in. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"HTTP3"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HTTP3</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">HTTP/3 support has been built-in. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"HTTPS-proxy"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HTT=
PS-proxy</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This curl is built to support HTTPS proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"IDN"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">IDN</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">This curl supports IDN - international domain names. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"IPv6"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">IPv6</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">You can use IPv6 with this. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"Kerberos"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Kerber=
os</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Kerberos V5 authentication is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"Largefile"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Large=
file</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This curl supports transfers of large files, files larg=
er than 2GB. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"libz"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">libz</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Automatic decompression (via gzip, deflate) of compress=
ed files over HTTP is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"MultiSSL"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">MultiS=
SL</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This curl supports multiple TLS backends. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"NTLM"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">NTLM</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">NTLM authentication is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"NTLMWB"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">NTLM_WB<=
/span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">NTLM delegation to winbind helper is supported. This fe=
ature was removed from curl in 8.8.0. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"PSL"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">PSL</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">PSL is short for Public Suffix List and means that this=
 curl has been built with knowledge about "public suffixes". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"SPNEGO"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SPNEGO</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">SPNEGO authentication is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"SSL"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SSL</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">SSL versions of various protocols are supported, such a=
s HTTPS, FTPS, POP3S and so on. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"SSLS-EXPORT"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SSL=
S-EXPORT</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">This build supports TLS session export/import, like wit=
h the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ssl=
-sessions">--ssl-sessions</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"SSPI"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SSPI</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">SSPI is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"TLS-SRP"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">TLS-SRP=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">SRP (Secure Remote Password) authentication is supporte=
d for TLS. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"TrackMemory"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Tra=
ckMemory</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Debug memory tracking is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"Unicode"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Unicode=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Unicode support on Windows. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"UnixSockets"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">Uni=
xSockets</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Unix sockets support is provided. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"zstd"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">zstd</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Automatic decompression (via zstd) of compressed files =
over HTTP is supported. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --version
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--help">--help</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"http=
s://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--manual">--manual</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--vlan-priority"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>--vlan-priority &lt;priority&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Set VLAN priority as defined in IEEE 802.1Q. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This field is set on Ethernet level, and only works wit=
hin a local network. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The valid range for &lt;priority&gt; is 0 to 7. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--vlan-priority">--vlan-priority</a> is provided several times,=
 the last set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --vlan-priority=
 4 https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">Added in 8.9.0. See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--ip-tos">--ip-tos</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"-w"></a><a name=3D"--write-out"></a><span cl=
ass=3D"nroffip">-w, --write-out &lt;format&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Make curl display information on stdout after a complet=
ed transfer. The format is a string that may contain plain text mixed with =
any number of variables. The format can be specified as a literal "string",=
 or you can have curl read the format from a file with "@filename" and to t=
ell curl to read the format from stdin you write "@-". </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The variables present in the output format are substitu=
ted by the value or text that curl thinks fit, as described below. All vari=
ables are specified as %{variable_name} and to output a normal % you just w=
rite them as %%. You can output a newline by using \n, a carriage return wi=
th \r and a tab space with \t. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The output is by default written to standard output, bu=
t can be changed with %{stderr} and %output{}. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Output HTTP header values from the transfer's most rece=
nt server response by using <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#headername">%header{name}</a> where <span class=3D"emphasi=
s">name</span> is the case insensitive name of the header (without the trai=
ling colon). The header contents are exactly as delivered over the network =
but with leading and trailing whitespace and newlines stripped off (added i=
n 7.84.0). </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Select a specific target destination file to write the =
output to, by using <span class=3D"emphasis">%output{name}</span> (added in=
 curl 8.3.0) where <span class=3D"emphasis">name</span> is the full filenam=
e. The output following that instruction is then written to that file. More=
 than one <span class=3D"emphasis">%output{}</span> instruction can be spec=
ified in the same write-out argument. If the filename cannot be created, cu=
rl leaves the output destination to the one used prior to the <span class=
=3D"emphasis">%output{}</span> instruction. Use <span class=3D"emphasis">%o=
utput{&gt;&gt;name}</span> to append data to an existing file. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This output is done independently of if the file transf=
er was successful or not. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If the specified action or output specified with this o=
ption fails in any way, it does not make curl return a (different) error. <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1"><span class=3D"bold">NOTE:</span> On Windows, the %-sym=
bol is a special symbol used to expand environment variables. In batch file=
s, all occurrences of % must be doubled when using this option to properly =
escape. If this option is used at the command prompt then the % cannot be e=
scaped and unintended expansion is possible. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The variables available are: </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"certs"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">certs</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Output the certificate chain with details. Supported on=
ly by the OpenSSL, GnuTLS, Schannel and Rustls backends. (Added in 7.88.0) =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"connid"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">conn_id<=
/span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The connection identifier last used by the transfer. Th=
e connection id is unique number among all connections using the same conne=
ction cache. (Added in 8.2.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"contenttype"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">con=
tent_type</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The Content-Type of the requested document, if there wa=
s any. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"errormsg"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">errorm=
sg</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The error message. (Added in 7.75.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"exitcode"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">exitco=
de</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The numerical exit code of the transfer. (Added in 7.75=
.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"filenameeffective"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">filename_effective</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The ultimate filename that curl writes out to. This is =
only meaningful if curl is told to write to a file with the <a class=3D"emp=
hasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-name">--remote-na=
me</a> or <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#-=
-output">--output</a> option. It is most useful in combination with the <a =
class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--remote-heade=
r-name">--remote-header-name</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"ftpentrypath"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ft=
p_entry_path</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The initial path curl ended up in when logging on to th=
e remote FTP server. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"headername"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">head=
er{name}</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The value of header "name" from the transfer's most rec=
ent server response. Unlike other variables, the variable name "header" is =
not in braces. For example "%header{date}". Refer to <a class=3D"emphasis" =
href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--write-out">--write-out</a> rema=
rks. (Added in 7.84.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Starting with 8.17.0, output the contents of <span clas=
s=3D"emphasis">all</span> header fields using a specific name - even for a =
whole redirect "chain" by appending ":all:[separator]" to the header name. =
The "[separator]" string (if not blank) is output between the headers if th=
ere are more than one. When more than one header is shown, they are output =
in the chronological order of appearance over the wire. To include a close =
brace ("}") in the separator, escape it with a backslash: "\}". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"headerjson"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">head=
er_json</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">A JSON object with all HTTP response headers from the r=
ecent transfer. Values are provided as arrays, since in the case of multipl=
e headers there can be multiple values. (Added in 7.83.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The header names provided in lowercase, listed in order=
 of appearance over the wire. Except for duplicated headers. They are group=
ed on the first occurrence of that header, each value is presented in the J=
SON array. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"httpcode"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">http_c=
ode</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The numerical response code that was found in the last =
retrieved HTTP(S) or FTP(s) transfer. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"httpconnect"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">htt=
p_connect</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The numerical code that was found in the last response =
(from a proxy) to a curl CONNECT request. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"httpversion"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">htt=
p_version</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The http version that was effectively used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"json"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">json</span=
> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">A JSON object with all available keys except "header_js=
on". (Added in 7.70.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"localip"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">local_i=
p</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The IP address of the local end of the most recently do=
ne connection - can be either IPv4 or IPv6. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"localport"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">local=
_port</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The local port number of the most recently done connect=
ion. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"method"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">method</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The http method used in the most recent HTTP request. (=
Added in 7.72.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"numcerts"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">num_ce=
rts</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Number of server certificates received in the TLS hands=
hake. Supported only by the OpenSSL, GnuTLS, Schannel and Rustls backends. =
(Added in 7.88.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"numconnects"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">num=
_connects</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Number of new connects made in the recent transfer. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"numheaders"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">num_=
headers</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The number of response headers in the most recent reque=
st (restarted at each redirect). Note that the status line IS NOT a header.=
 (Added in 7.73.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"numredirects"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">nu=
m_redirects</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Number of redirects that were followed in the request. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"numretries"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">num_=
retries</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Number of retries actually performed when "<a class=3D"=
emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--retry">--retry</a>" h=
as been used. (Added in 8.9.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"onerror"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">onerror=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The rest of the output is only shown if the transfer re=
turned a non-zero error. (Added in 7.75.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"outputfilename"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
output{filename}</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">From this point on, the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--write-out">--write-out</a> output is wri=
tten to the filename specified in braces. The filename can be prefixed with=
 "&gt;&gt;" to append to the file. Unlike other variables, the variable nam=
e "output" is not in braces. For example "%output{&gt;&gt;stats.txt}". Refe=
r to <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--writ=
e-out">--write-out</a> remarks. (Added in 8.3.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"proxysslverifyresult"></a><span class=3D"nro=
ffip">proxy_ssl_verify_result</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The result of the HTTPS proxy's SSL peer certificate ve=
rification that was requested. 0 means the verification was successful. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"proxyused"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">proxy=
_used</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Returns 1 if the previous transfer used a proxy, otherw=
ise 0. Useful to for example determine if a "NOPROXY" pattern matched the h=
ostname or not. (Added in 8.7.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"redirecturl"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">red=
irect_url</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">When an HTTP request was made without <a class=3D"empha=
sis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--location">--location</a> t=
o follow redirects (or when <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/d=
ocs/manpage.html#--max-redirs">--max-redirs</a> is met), this variable show=
s the actual URL a redirect <span class=3D"emphasis">would</span> have gone=
 to. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"referer"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">referer=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The Referer: header, if there was any. (Added in 7.76.0=
) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"remoteip"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">remote=
_ip</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The remote IP address of the most recently done connect=
ion - can be either IPv4 or IPv6. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"remoteport"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">remo=
te_port</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The remote port number of the most recently done connec=
tion. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"responsecode"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">re=
sponse_code</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The numerical response code that was found in the last =
transfer (formerly known as "http_code"). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"scheme"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">scheme</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The URL scheme (sometimes called protocol) that was eff=
ectively used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"sizedownload"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">si=
ze_download</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The total amount of bytes that were downloaded. This is=
 the size of the body/data that was transferred, excluding headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"sizeheader"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">size=
_header</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The total amount of bytes of the downloaded headers. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"sizerequest"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">siz=
e_request</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The total amount of bytes that were sent in the HTTP re=
quest. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"sizeupload"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">size=
_upload</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The total amount of bytes that were uploaded. This is t=
he size of the body/data that was transferred, excluding headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"speeddownload"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">s=
peed_download</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The average download speed that curl measured for the c=
omplete download. Bytes per second. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"speedupload"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">spe=
ed_upload</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The average upload speed that curl measured for the com=
plete upload. Bytes per second. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"sslverifyresult"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>ssl_verify_result</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The result of the SSL peer certificate verification tha=
t was requested. 0 means the verification was successful. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"stderr"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">stderr</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">From this point on, the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--write-out">--write-out</a> output is wri=
tten to standard error. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"stdout"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">stdout</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">From this point on, the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"h=
ttps://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--write-out">--write-out</a> output is wri=
tten to standard output. This is the default, but can be used to switch bac=
k after switching to stderr. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timeformat"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">time=
{format}</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">Output the current UTC time using "strftime()" format. =
See TIME OUTPUT FORMAT below for details. (Added in 8.16.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timeappconnect"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
time_appconnect</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, it took from the start until the =
SSL/SSH/etc connect/handshake to the remote host was completed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timeconnect"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">tim=
e_connect</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, it took from the start until the =
TCP connect to the remote host (or proxy) was completed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timenamelookup"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
time_namelookup</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, it took from the start until the =
name resolving was completed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timeposttransfer"></a><span class=3D"nroffip=
">time_posttransfer</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time it took from the start until the last byte is =
sent by libcurl. In microseconds. (Added in 8.10.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timepretransfer"></a><span class=3D"nroffip"=
>time_pretransfer</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, it took from the start until the =
file transfer was just about to begin. This includes all pre-transfer comma=
nds and negotiations that are specific to the particular protocol(s) involv=
ed. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timequeue"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">time_=
queue</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, the transfer was queued during it=
s run. This adds the queue time for each redirect step that may have happen=
ed. Transfers may be queued for significant amounts of time when connection=
 or parallel limits are in place. (Added in 8.12.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timeredirect"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ti=
me_redirect</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, it took for all redirection steps=
 including name lookup, connect, pretransfer and transfer before the final =
transaction was started. "time_redirect" shows the complete execution time =
for multiple redirections. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timestarttransfer"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">time_starttransfer</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The time, in seconds, it took from the start until the =
first byte was received. This includes time_pretransfer and also the time t=
he server needed to calculate the result. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"timetotal"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">time_=
total</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The total time, in seconds, that the full operation las=
ted. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"tlsearlydata"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">tl=
s_earlydata</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The amount of bytes that were sent as TLSv1.3 early dat=
a. This is 0 if this TLS feature was not used and negative if the data sent=
 had been rejected by the server. The use of early data is enabled via the =
command line option "<a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/man=
page.html#--tls-earlydata">--tls-earlydata</a>". (Added in 8.12.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"url"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url</span> =
</p>
<p class=3D"level2">The URL that was fetched. (Added in 7.75.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlscheme"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.s=
cheme</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The scheme part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in =
8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urluser"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.use=
r</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The user part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in 8.=
1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlpassword"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url=
.password</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The password part of the URL that was fetched. (Added i=
n 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urloptions"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.=
options</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The options part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in=
 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlhost"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.hos=
t</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The host part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in 8.=
1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlport"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.por=
t</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The port number of the URL that was fetched. If no port=
 number was specified and the URL scheme is known, that scheme's default po=
rt number is shown. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlpath"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.pat=
h</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The path part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in 8.=
1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlquery"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.qu=
ery</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The query part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in 8=
.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlfragment"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url=
.fragment</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The fragment part of the URL that was fetched. (Added i=
n 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlzoneid"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url.z=
oneid</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The zone id part of the URL that was fetched. (Added in=
 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlescheme"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle=
.scheme</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The scheme part of the effective (last) URL that was fe=
tched. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urleuser"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle.u=
ser</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The user part of the effective (last) URL that was fetc=
hed. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlepassword"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ur=
le.password</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The password part of the effective (last) URL that was =
fetched. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urleoptions"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">url=
e.options</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The options part of the effective (last) URL that was f=
etched. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlehost"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle.h=
ost</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The host part of the effective (last) URL that was fetc=
hed. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urleport"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle.p=
ort</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The port number of the effective (last) URL that was fe=
tched. If no port number was specified, but the URL scheme is known, that s=
cheme's default port number is shown. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlepath"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle.p=
ath</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The path part of the effective (last) URL that was fetc=
hed. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlequery"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle.=
query</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The query part of the effective (last) URL that was fet=
ched. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlefragment"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ur=
le.fragment</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The fragment part of the effective (last) URL that was =
fetched. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlezoneid"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urle=
.zoneid</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The zone id part of the effective (last) URL that was f=
etched. (Added in 8.1.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urlnum"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">urlnum</=
span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The URL index number of this transfer, 0-indexed. Unglo=
bbed URLs share the same index number as the origin globbed URL. (Added in =
7.75.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"urleffective"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ur=
l_effective</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The URL that was fetched last. This is most meaningful =
if you have told curl to follow location: headers. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"xferid"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">xfer_id<=
/span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">The numerical identifier of the last transfer done. -1 =
if no transfer has been started yet for the handle. The transfer id is uniq=
ue among all transfers performed using the same connection cache. (Added in=
 8.2.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">TIME OUTPUT FORMAT </p>
<p class=3D"level1">To show time with "%time{}" the characters within "{}" =
creates a special format string that may contain special character sequence=
s called conversion specifications. Each conversion specification starts wi=
th "%" and is followed by a character that instructs curl to output a parti=
cular time detail. All other characters used are displayed as-is and- </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The following conversion specification are available: <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"a"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%a</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The abbreviated name of the day of the week according t=
o the current locale. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"A"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%A</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The full name of the day of the week according to the c=
urrent locale. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"b"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%b</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The abbreviated month name according to the current loc=
ale. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"B"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%B</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The full month name according to the current locale. </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"c"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%c</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The preferred date and time representation for the curr=
ent locale. (In the POSIX locale this is equivalent to "%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %=
Y".) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"C"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%C</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The century number (year/100) as a 2-digit integer. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"d"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%d</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The day of the month as a decimal number (range 01 to 3=
1). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"D"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%D</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Equivalent to "%m/%d/%y". In international contexts, th=
is format is ambiguous and should be avoided.) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"e"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%e</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Like "%d", the day of the month as a decimal number, bu=
t a leading zero is replaced by a space. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"f"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%f</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The number of microseconds elapsed of the current secon=
d. (This a curl special code and not a standard one.) </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"F"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%F</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Equivalent to "%Y-%m-%d" (the ISO 8601 date format). </=
p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"G"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%G</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The ISO 8601 week-based year with century as a decimal =
number. The 4-digit year corresponding to the ISO week number (see "%V"). T=
his has the same format and value as "%Y", except that if the ISO week numb=
er belongs to the previous or next year, that year is used instead. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"g"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%g</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Like "%G", but without century, that is, with a 2-digit=
 year (00-99). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"h"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%h</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Equivalent to "%b". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"H"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%H</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The hour as a decimal number using a 24-hour clock (ran=
ge 00 to 23). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"I"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%I</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The hour as a decimal number using a 12-hour clock (ran=
ge 01 to 12). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"j"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%j</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The day of the year as a decimal number (range 001 to 3=
66). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"k"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%k</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The hour (24-hour clock) as a decimal number (range 0 t=
o 23); single digits are preceded by a blank. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"l"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%l</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The hour (12-hour clock) as a decimal number (range 1 t=
o 12); single digits are preceded by a blank. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"m"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%m</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The month as a decimal number (range 01 to 12). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"M"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%M</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The minute as a decimal number (range 00 to 59). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"p"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%p</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Either "AM" or "PM" according to the given time value, =
or the corresponding strings for the current locale. Noon is treated as "PM=
" and midnight as "AM". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"P"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%P</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">Like "%p" but in lowercase: "am" or "pm" or a correspon=
ding string for the current locale. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"r"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%r</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The time in am or pm notation. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"R"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%R</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The time in 24-hour notation ("%H:%M"). For a version i=
ncluding the seconds, see "%T" below. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"s"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%s</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The number of seconds since the Epoch, 1970-01-01 00:00=
:00 +0000 (UTC). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"S"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%S</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The second as a decimal number (range 00 to 60). (The r=
ange is up to 60 to allow for occasional leap seconds.) See "%f" for micros=
econds. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"T"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%T</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The time in 24-hour notation ("%H:%M:%S"). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"u"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%u</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The day of the week as a decimal, range 1 to 7, Monday =
being 1. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"U"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%U</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The week number of the current year as a decimal number=
, range 00 to 53, starting with the first Sunday as the first day of week 0=
1. See also "%V" and "%W". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"V"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%V</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The ISO 8601 week number (see NOTES) of the current yea=
r as a decimal number, range 01 to 53, where week 1 is the first week that =
has at least 4 days in the new year. See also "%U" and "%W". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"w"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%w</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The day of the week as a decimal, range 0 to 6, Sunday =
being 0. See also "%u". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"W"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%W</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The week number of the current year as a decimal number=
, range 00 to 53, starting with the first Monday as the first day of week 0=
1. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"x"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%x</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The preferred date representation for the current local=
e without the time. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"X"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%X</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The preferred time representation for the current local=
e without the date. </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"y"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%y</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The year as a decimal number without a century (range 0=
0 to 99). </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"Y"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%Y</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The year as a decimal number including the century. </p=
>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"z"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%z</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The "+hhmm" or "-hhmm" numeric timezone (that is, the h=
our and minute offset from UTC). As time is always UTC, this outputs "+0000=
". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D"Z"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%Z</span> </p=
>
<p class=3D"level2">The timezone name. For some reason "GMT". </p>
<p class=3D"level1"><a name=3D""></a><span class=3D"nroffip">%%</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level2">A literal "%" character. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"nroffip"></span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#--write-out">--write-out</a> is provided several times, the las=
t set value is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl -w '%{response_=
code}\n' https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--verbose">--verbose</a> and <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--head">--head</a>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"--xattr"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">--xattr=
</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Store metadata in the extended file attributes. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">When saving output to a file, tell curl to store file m=
etadata in extended file attributes. Currently, "curl" is stored in the "cr=
eator" attribute, the URL is stored in the "xdg.origin.url" attribute and, =
for HTTP, the content type is stored in the "mime_type" attribute. If the f=
ile system does not support extended attributes, a warning is issued. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Providing <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se=
/docs/manpage.html#--xattr">--xattr</a> multiple times has no extra effect.=
 Disable it again with --no-xattr. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Example: </p><pre class=3D"level1">curl --xattr -o stor=
age https://example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">See also <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/=
docs/manpage.html#--remote-time">--remote-time</a>, <a class=3D"emphasis" h=
ref=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--write-out">--write-out</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--verbose">-=
-verbose</a>. </p><a name=3D"FILES"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Files</h2>
<p class=3D"level0"><span class=3D"emphasis">~/.curlrc</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Default config file, see <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"=
https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--config">--config</a> for details. </p><=
a name=3D"ENVIRONMENT"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Environment</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">The environment variables can be specified in lower cas=
e or upper case. The lower case version has precedence. "http_proxy" is an =
exception as it is only available in lower case. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">Using an environment variable to set the proxy has the =
same effect as using the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs=
/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> option. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"httpproxy"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">http_=
proxy [protocol://]&lt;host&gt;[:port]</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Sets the proxy server to use for HTTP. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"HTTPSPROXY"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HTTP=
S_PROXY [protocol://]&lt;host&gt;[:port]</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Sets the proxy server to use for HTTPS. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"url-protocolPROXY"></a><span class=3D"nroffi=
p">[url-protocol]_PROXY [protocol://]&lt;host&gt;[:port]</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Sets the proxy server to use for [url-protocol], where =
the protocol is a protocol that curl supports and as specified in a URL. FT=
P, FTPS, POP3, IMAP, SMTP, LDAP, etc. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"ALLPROXY"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">ALL_PR=
OXY [protocol://]&lt;host&gt;[:port]</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Sets the proxy server to use if no protocol-specific pr=
oxy is set. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"NOPROXY"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">NO_PROX=
Y &lt;comma-separated list of hosts/domains&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">list of hostnames that should not go through any proxy.=
 If set to an asterisk '*' only, it matches all hosts. Each name in this li=
st is matched as either a domain name which contains the hostname, or the h=
ostname itself. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">This environment variable disables use of the proxy eve=
n when specified with the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/doc=
s/manpage.html#--proxy">--proxy</a> option. That is </p><pre class=3D"level=
1">NO_PROXY=3Ddirect.example.com curl -x http://proxy.example.com
https://direct.example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">accesses the target URL directly, and </p><pre class=3D=
"level1">NO_PROXY=3Ddirect.example.com curl -x http://proxy.example.com
https://somewhere.example.com
</pre>
<p class=3D"level1">accesses the target URL through the proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">The list of hostnames can also include numerical IP add=
resses, and IPv6 versions should then be given without enclosing brackets. =
</p>
<p class=3D"level1">IP addresses can be specified using CIDR notation: an a=
ppended slash and number specifies the number of "network bits" out of the =
address to use in the comparison (added in 7.86.0). For example "192.168.0.=
0/16" would match all addresses starting with "192.168". </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"APPDATA"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">APPDATA=
 &lt;directory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On Windows, this variable is used when trying to find t=
he home directory. If the primary home variables are all unset. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"COLUMNS"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">COLUMNS=
 &lt;terminal width&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, the specified number of characters is used as t=
he terminal width when the alternative progress-bar is shown. If not set, c=
url tries to figure it out using other ways. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"CURLCABUNDLE"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">CU=
RL_CA_BUNDLE &lt;file&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, it is used as the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a> value. This envir=
onment variable is ignored if Schannel is used as the TLS backend. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"CURLHOME"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">CURL_H=
OME &lt;directory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, is the first variable curl checks when trying t=
o find its home directory. If not set, it continues to check <a class=3D"em=
phasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#XDGCONFIGHOME">XDG_CONFIG=
_HOME</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"CURLSSLBACKEND"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">=
CURL_SSL_BACKEND &lt;TLS backend&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl was built with support for "MultiSSL", meaning =
that it has built-in support for more than one TLS backend, this environmen=
t variable can be set to the case insensitive name of the particular backen=
d to use when curl is invoked. Setting a name that is not a built-in altern=
ative makes curl stay with the default. </p>
<p class=3D"level1">SSL backend names (case-insensitive): <span class=3D"bo=
ld">gnutls</span>, <span class=3D"bold">mbedtls</span>, <span class=3D"bold=
">openssl</span>, <span class=3D"bold">rustls</span>, <span class=3D"bold">=
schannel</span>, <span class=3D"bold">wolfssl</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"HOME"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">HOME &lt;d=
irectory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, this is used to find the home directory when th=
at is needed. Like when looking for the default .curlrc. <a class=3D"emphas=
is" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#CURLHOME">CURL_HOME</a> and <=
a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#XDGCONFIGHOM=
E">XDG_CONFIG_HOME</a> have preference. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"NETRC"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">NETRC &lt=
;path&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, this is used to find the ".netrc" file. It over=
rides all other netrc file location mechanisms and should be set to the ful=
l file path. (Added in curl 8.16.0) </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"QLOGDIR"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">QLOGDIR=
 &lt;directory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If curl was built with HTTP/3 support, setting this env=
ironment variable to a local directory makes curl produce <span class=3D"bo=
ld">qlogs</span> in that directory, using file names named after the destin=
ation connection id (in hex). Do note that these files can become rather la=
rge. Works with the ngtcp2 and quiche QUIC backends. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SHELL"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SHELL</sp=
an> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Used on VMS when trying to detect if using a <span clas=
s=3D"bold">DCL</span> or a <span class=3D"bold">Unix</span> shell. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SSLCERTDIR"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SSL_=
CERT_DIR &lt;directory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, it is used as the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--capath">--capath</a> value. This envir=
onment variable is ignored if Schannel is used as the TLS backend. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SSLCERTFILE"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">SSL=
_CERT_FILE &lt;path&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If set, it is used as the <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D=
"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--cacert">--cacert</a> value. This envir=
onment variable is ignored if Schannel is used as the TLS backend. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"SSLKEYLOGFILE"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">S=
SLKEYLOGFILE &lt;path&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If you set this environment variable to a filename, cur=
l stores TLS secrets from its connections in that file when invoked to enab=
le you to analyze the TLS traffic in real time using network analyzing tool=
s such as Wireshark. This works with the following TLS backends: OpenSSL, L=
ibreSSL (TLS 1.2 max), BoringSSL, GnuTLS, wolfSSL and Rustls. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"USERPROFILE"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">USE=
RPROFILE &lt;directory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">On Windows, this variable is used when trying to find t=
he home directory. If the other, primary, variables are all unset. If set, =
curl uses the path <span class=3D"bold">"$USERPROFILE\Application Data"</sp=
an>. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"XDGCONFIGHOME"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">X=
DG_CONFIG_HOME &lt;directory&gt;</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">If <a class=3D"emphasis" href=3D"https://curl.se/docs/m=
anpage.html#CURLHOME">CURL_HOME</a> is not set, this variable is checked wh=
en looking for a default .curlrc file. </p><a name=3D"PROXY"></a><h2 class=
=3D"nroffsh">Proxy protocol prefixes</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">The proxy string may be specified with a protocol:// pr=
efix to specify alternative proxy protocols. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">If no protocol is specified in the proxy string or if t=
he string does not match a supported one, the proxy is treated as an HTTP p=
roxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level0">The supported proxy protocol prefixes are as follows: <=
/p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"http"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">http://</s=
pan> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Makes it use it as an HTTP proxy. The default if no sch=
eme prefix is used. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"https"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">https://<=
/span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Makes it treated as an <a class=3D"bold" href=3D"https:=
//curl.se/docs/manpage.html#HTTPS">HTTPS</a> proxy. </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"socks4"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">socks4:/=
/</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Makes it the equivalent of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4">--socks4</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"socks4a"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">socks4a=
://</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Makes it the equivalent of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks4a">--socks4a</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"socks5"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">socks5:/=
/</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Makes it the equivalent of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5">--socks5</a> </p>
<p class=3D"level0"><a name=3D"socks5h"></a><span class=3D"nroffip">socks5h=
://</span> </p>
<p class=3D"level1">Makes it the equivalent of <a class=3D"emphasis" href=
=3D"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html#--socks5-hostname">--socks5-hostname<=
/a> </p><a name=3D"EXIT"></a><h2 class=3D"nroffsh">Exit codes</h2>
<p class=3D"level0">There are a bunch of different error codes and their co=
rresponding error messages that may appear under error conditions. At the t=
ime of this writing, the exit codes are: </p>
